      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>ASK YOUR GROCER FORMcDOUGALL'S Patent SELF—RAISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>KEEP PEPS HANDY.</p>
        <p>For Coughs, Colds, and Bronchitis. The Wonderful Medicine in Solid</p>
        <p>Tablet Form.</p>
        <p>Keep a box of Peps always bandy, for coughs and colds are common in every borne at this season of the year, and these " common " colds are the seeds from which spring bronchitis, chronic asthma, pleurisy, pneumonia, and other thi oat and chest troubles -even consumption itself. Don't go out ih bad weather without your box of Peps. The wonderful medicinal fumes from Peps will check that ominous chill and effectually prevent " catching cold," even in the worst weather. You can't tackle a bad sold too soon, so keep Peps always handy in the house. The time lost in getting a supply from the nearest chemist may mean all the difference between recovery and a severe attack of pneumonia or pleurisy. Keep Peps always handy in the nursery, to cure thoso wasting night coughs, and in readiness for those sudden attacks of croup or acute bron- chitis. Don't forget that children who take Peps regularly grow up strong-lunged, deep-chested, and vigorous. Keep Peps bandy for the old folks. Peps will cure that worrying cough which so often makes old folks' days and nights full of misery. Keep Peps bandy by the bedside. If you suffer from bronchitis, bronchial asthma, night coughs, or that deep-seated morning cough which tells of serious lung mischief, a few Peps will stop the worst attack„ and one or two tablets regularly night and mthming means a sure and speedy cure. Travellers by road, • rail or sea should keep Peps always bandy. Draughty trains foster colds, and exposure to constant changes of weather and climate is full of peril to throat and chest. Peps are a sure and handy safeguard against infection, and the only safe and reliable medicine for lung troubles. • Take Peps When sneezing gives warning of a "fresh cold," and so ward off the threatened trouble and banish the cold. Peps are always handy • a wonderfully potent medicine reduced to solid tablet form; always constant in quality and certain in result ; a medicine you can breathe direct into the lungs t free from opium, chloral, or any other drowsy drug; safe alike for young and old; and a sure cure for coughs, colds, bronchitis, sore throat, asthma, and other throat and chest troub:es. If you see the short, distinctive title —Peps on the box and on every tablet, you will know that you have the only genuine, and not a worth- ess substitute. Sold by 01l chemists in handy boxes at is. I;d. and 2s. 9d.</p>
        <p>In an instant Gwendoline was beside her, her arms round her neck, her soft young cheek, glawing with health, laid gently against her pale, scared face. "Mother, you are ill," the girl cried, in alarm. "Oh! dear one, why did you get up? It was naughty of you to dress without help; and now, you see "—reproachfully—" you have made your- self ill. Come back to your room and lie down. Why, you dear little woman "—with a playful A special meeting of the District Executive was held smi l e—" you look like a ghost." in the parlour of Wycliffe Congregational Church, Mrs. Travers clung to her young daughter, Bewsey, on Saturday night for the purpose of opening pressing her to her breast in an agony of a new adult branch of the Order. Brethren J. Hilly emotion, every word she tried to utter strangled D.C.R., T. Longshaw D.D.R., S. Pelve Levite, C.G. by a sob or a moan of 'anguish. Fellowes, Guardian, R. Harrison, P.C.R., T. Winstanley. . Gwendoline gazed at her, alarmed and per- D.S., acting as the instituting officers, supported by plexed. Something terrible was wrong—some- the District Executive, and also a good number of members belonging to the other tents in the town. thing awful must have happened. A shiver The new members necessary for the opening of a passed over her. A little while ago she had felt • . branch were duly initiated and deep)), interested while so happy; now her soul was sick within her, and the ritual of the Order was being proceedikl with, she trembled as she watched hap unhappy mother The new tent was named Wycliffe, and declared open and listened to her sobbing. as a branch of the Order--by the District Chief Ruler. "Mother dear," she whispered, "what is it? The election of officers for the new tent resulted as What is wrong ? " follows :—J. Garner, C.R., G. Colley, D.R., T. A. Mrs. Travers sat up, dashed her hand across Bradley, P.C.R., J. S. Brown, Levite, T. G. Tailor, her faCe, then sprang suddenly to her feet. Guardian, W. Wakefield, Treasurer, Peter Howard, "I am not generally weak or easily alarmed," Secretary, Joseph Owen, G. A. Donaldson and H. Woolley, Trustees, District Representative, G. Colley. she cried, vehemently. "But this note "—holding Bro. J. HILL, D.C.K., expressed his pleasure at being out an envelope bearing English post-marks and present to open the new tent, and judging from what he English stamps—"has unnerved me. See, child, could see of the members present, he was hopeful of it is from your Uncle Max. Read it, and tell ma good work being done. that I have good cause for fear. Oh ! "—clasp- Bro. T. MASSEY, D.G. said he had a pleasing duty in. her hands with a gesture of despair—" if he to perform on behalf of the officers and members of had only said why I was wanted; but he says Charity tents (the mother tent of the District and one nothing—nothing, so I fear the worst ! " and she of the oldest tents in the Order) to make a presentation flung herself across her daughter's bed. of two very useful articles, namely, a Secretary's White and trembling, Gwendoline read her Leather Bag, and Treasurer's Cash Bag, and he did not know of anything that would be more useful in con- uncle's letter. It was brief, but undoubtedly nection with the work of Wycliffe tent, and hoped that alarming: _ they would be very successful in extending the Order in "My dear Laura, you are urgently needed here. that part of the town. Come back at once. —MAX." Bro. J. GARNER, C.R. of the new tent in acceptipg The letter fluttered to the ground, Gwendoline the presents from Charity tent said it was very kind and gave a little gasp, and turned a terrified glance thoughtful of the members of Charity tent to give upon her mother. them a good start in their work at Wycliffe and he "It is alarming, dearest. But "—trying to could assure there on behalf of the members of the speak reassuringly—" Uncle Max always ex- new branch they should not forget the kindness that had agger es.. y at Write to-night and ask him