        <p>If you would avoid rheu-natism, kidney and bladder troubles, he moderate in the consumption of heavy rich foods, substitute as far as possible soups, broths, fresh milk, and drink water—lots of water. Take plenty of time to eat, and don't eat after you have had enough, even if it does taste good. If your work is confining take a moderate amount of exercise each day in the open air.</p>
        <p>wroua." "You shall know all I know"—in a choking voice. "But alas! that is .very little. It tells us nothing." "Nothing! " Mrs. Travers crumpled the telegram in her clenched fist. "Live cheaply." She gazed round her in a bewildered way. "How can we do that, Gwen ? This is an expensive hotel. But why "—petulantly—" is money scarce? :Et used Fact to be so. My husband's business was one of the ,best on the Stock Exchange. Max must be 4/waning or mad. Don't you think so, dear ? "</p>
        <p>Of course, neither diet, rest, water, nor exer,:ise will cure these afflictions. We advise them as preventives only. For the benefit of readers of this article, who are.now afflicted with rheumatism, kidney, bladder or urinary troubles, and desire to be cured quickly, we give below, complete in every detail, the complete prescrip- tion which has made a great authority on tbe subject so successful in the treatment of these disehses. It is the most certain cure which has been used. It is pleasant to take, it is not expensive, it can be supplied by any chemist, and we believe it to be the greatest prescrip- tion for rheumatism, kidney and bladder troubles ever written. It is also a valuable tonic and blood purifier. If you are a sufferer, take this prescription to any chemist, and have it prepared: or, you can obtain the ingredients separately, and mix them all at home.</p>
        <p>Cwendoline made no reply as she paced the room from end to end in deep thought. Pepressed, bewildered, stupefied; she scarcely heard her mother's question. Their position %'as, she felt, a terrible one. With little money and no friends, in a foreign land, her mother in</p>
        <p>bad health, with no one to look after or care for her but a young, ignorant, inexperienced girl like herself-- Gwendoline wrung her hands. The situation appalled her. Her mother seemed incapable of action; and, unaccustomed though she was to take the lead, she saw that she must do so now, and without delay. To make what money they had in hand last as long as possible was, she told herself, not only a duty, but a necessity. To do this they must leave the Italie at once, and seek a smaller and cheaper lodging. "I'll return to England," Mrs. Travers declared, when her daughter unfolded her plans. "I should go mad here, ignorant of all that was going on at home. Do not oppose me, Gwen. This shock may—will, 1 feel, kill me! For Heaven's.sake let mc breathe my last in my native land ! " To argue seemed useless, and full of misgiv- i'3 r.,?s, certain that it was unwise to spend so much of the money still left to them upon a journey to England, Gwendoline sank sobbing upon th 3 floor at her rrother's feet. "Poor dai ling!" Mrs. Travers whispered, caressing the golden hair. "Oh my Gwendoline, poverty and misery will be our lot henceforth. The business has collapsed—l feel—l know! What will become of us ? ' "Things cannot be as bad as you imagine, dearest." Yes. I know now why your father fled—why Max looked like a ghost. All is overt" 932 hopeful, dear one. We'll go home this</p>
        <p>Tincture Gentian Compound, one ounce; Syrup Rhubarb one ounce; Liquid Barkola Compound, one ounce; Syrup of Ginger, five ounces. The dose for adults is one teaspoonful taken after meals, and one at hedtimP. Children should be given one-fourth to one-hnlf teaspoonful, after meals only.</p>
        <p>After you are cured, follow the advance given as to diet, water, and exercise, and you will not need the services of a physician again for these ailments. NOTE—Other papers are privileged to copy the above.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AYOAL RECEPTION'.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress of St. Ilelens (Alderman and Mrs. Martin) held a receptior at the Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon. The large ball was utilised for the first time for this purpose, and there was a large attendance of townspeople and residents of the district. The hall was artistically decorated, and Miss Ross's Ladies' Orchestra rendered a most interesting pro- gramme of music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] A LUCKY ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] A LUCKY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>BY CLARA MULHOLLAND,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Unexpected Guest," "A False Step," "Tessa's Song," "Lady Strathmore' Stratagem," "Kathleen Mavourneen," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The sun shone brightly, and with a warmth I that was delightful to Gwendoline Travers after the fog and damp mists of England. This was her first awakening in Rome; and, springing out of bed, she flung a soft woollen dressing- gown round her shoulders, ran, over to the window of her pretty room in the Hotel d'ltalie, and gazed out through the high, branching palms and dark green cypress trees in the garden of the Palazzo Barberini just beneath, and up the hilly Via Quattro Fountain, down which at that moment trooped a number of Germanic° students, their scarlet costumes giving a beautiful touch of colour to the already charming scene. "What a morning for February," Gwendoline murmured, with a little chuckle of satisfaction— "and what a picture ! Oh, decidedly we did well to come to Rome. Everything was so gloomy in London, inside the house and out. The weather was wretched. My father in low spirits—my mother in bad health. Poor dears! they wanted a change. Father went unexpectedly and without saying where., and mother, after a short consulta- tion with Uncle Max, came off here. A time in Rome will be really delightful. I long to see the Grand Forum, the Catacombs, and the world- famed. St. Peters. Oh! I know I'll enjoy myself," turning to the glass and beginning to brush out her golden hair; "and so will mother, although, poor dear, she'll miss her maid sadly. But she would not bring Selina. It was foolish to come without her. But there! when mothers are determined, daughters can only submit. I must hurry now and dress. If I slip into her room soon I may be able to help her, or, better still, persuade her to remain in bed all day. Poor mother! she was so done up on our arrival here at midnight; those last hours in the train were almost more than she could bear. After such a journey she will certainly want a rest. And I "—sighing—"feel so fresh, and am longing to set out to see something of this glorious town. But I can't go alone, and---" The door opened, and Mrs. Travers appeared, fully dressed, upon the threshold. She was very pale, with a worried, almost hunted, look in her eyes, which were red as though she had been weeping. She opened her quivering lips, and seemed about to speak, then sank with a deep sob into a chair and covered her face with her hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>very day and race the worst. Uncertainty is killing. Our future----" "Yours would be safe enough if only you would send one word to John Goldschmidt."_.</p>
        <p>"No, no!" Red and white, and trembling visibly, Gwendoline started away. "Impossible I Oh, mother, not that—never that! I told him so."</p>
        <p>"But things have changed. Fred is--" "Poor—yes. But we are poor now; he " smiling—"may grow rich " "A wild idea. Without a professional business, lnd a small income that just enables him to live in cheap places, impossible. And, oh! don't forget that John Goldschmidt declared that if ever you were in trouble you were to send for him. At one word he would be at your feet again, and our misery would be less desperate." "Mother ! " A shiver passed over the girl, and a deep blush dyed her pale cheeks crimson. "Don't talk of such a thing. I could not do it."</p>
        <p>"Not even for me? Nol to save me from— want ? "</p>
        <p>Gwendoline breathed heavily. Her bosom heaved, and she grew suddenly pale as marble. "I—l would do much—give my life—for you, mother," she stammered. • "I see no reason for that. To most girls marriage with John Goldschmidt would be no martyrdom, and I am sure that if you had never met Fred Langdale you would have looked at him with different eyes." Gwendoline turned away, her tears running down like rain. "Perhaps,' she murmured. "But till the day of my death, mother, I could never Look at him but in one way now." Mrs. Travers uttered a deep groan and fell back upon her pillow. "Sentiment and fancy before your mother's happiness! I thought so.' "Mother ! " Gwendoline hid her face upon her breast.</p>
        <p>"My poor darling! Forgive me "—with emotipn. "My troubles have embittered me. But yßut happiness is my one desire. Only, when I think of what I might have been, and "—sighing— " w hat is, I— But there, there! I'm ashamed of my outburst, and will say no more. You have !nough sorrow, my brave little girl. I must not add to it by my lamentations." Gwendoline kissed her mother passionately. Iler heart was too full for words. In all her young life she had never suffered as she did now. This was indeed a dark and gloomy hour. "The darkest hour is," 'tis said, "the hour before dawn." But look where she would, she could see no ray of comfort. 'Twas the darkest hour with- out any prospect of a coming dawn. "Oh! if I were back in London," sighed Mrs. Travers. "I could surelv.nut things right Max</p>
        <p>MEMO: UWennoline in tier ,tu, owtr., itritm.</p>
        <p>"Ah, yes; plenty. My house is large, signorina. 'I am glad of that, and also that you speak English,' Gwendoline answered ; and she drew her mother's hand within her arm and followed the Signorina Prato up a broad, dimly-lighted flight of stone stairs into the pension on the third floor.</p>
        <p>"The servant will bring your luggage up presently," the little Italian said, leading the way with light and nimble feet. "Meanwhile you must see your, rooms." The rooms were good, the sicrbnora's terms extremely low, and Gwendoline felt truly thankful for such a haven of rest.</p>
        <p>"Have you many people staying with you ? " she asked, when all matters of business were amicably settled. "A dizaine—ten, you say. A widow and het daughter, who live much with me. Three maiden sisters—rather old. An Italian and his brother, not much here,who study law, and an old English- man from Via Regia to try the climate. If Pisa agrees with him he will bring his wife, and they will take a house and live in this town. If not, he will depart for ever." Gwendoline laughed. "He is hard to please in the matter of climate, then ? " The signorina made a grimace and shrugged her shoulders.</p>
        <p>"Ah, yes. Well, and I hope you and the Signora mother will not mind, but because of his health he must always wear a hat." "Out of doors? Poor thing, of course." "No, but indoors. In the drawing-room, at the dinner table—everywhere. 'Tis an idea. I cannot prevent him. He must do his will." "Quite. I understand." Gwendoline cast a hasty and somewhat alarmed glance at her mother.</p>
        <p>-"Barbarians one and all, I'm sure," Mrs. Travers said under her breath. "But nothing seems to matter now. I'm tired, Gwen., and will go to bed at once. I could not bear to face that hatted old crank to-night. I'll go to bed." "Very well, dear, so you shall." And as the Signorina Prato hurried away the girl began to unpack the things her mother required for the night. "We must leave this to-morrow, Gwen," Mrs. Travers said, petulantly. "I cannot mix with such people. Who did the queer little woman say they all were ? " Gwendoline went over the various inmates of the pension mentioned by Signorina Prato. "That makes nine—she distinctly said there were ten. Is the tenth a man or a woman, l 041.diw "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES. Warringtoa District No 4.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES. Warringtoa District No 4.</p>
        <p>ADVICE TO RHEUMATICS.</p>
        <p>always tooKs at tne aarKest side ot everything." Gwendoline dashed aside her tears. "You'll soon be there, dearest. We'll stop a night at Pisa, and get home in a couple of days." "Stopping is expensive, Gwen. Remember, we must be careful."</p>
        <p>"Trust me. But you must have a rest. To travel straight through, mother, would be too trying. I have the address of a cheap pension at Pisa. The Lesters' governess stayed there for tier holidays and liked it." Mrs. Travers groaned. Times were indeed ?hanged when she was obliged to put up at second-rate pensions, frequented by governesses. -"We'll see a new life," G wendotine smiled faintly. "Sophie said she met nice people there. We may do the same." "Don't "—impatiently —"dream of the im- possible, child. People .of our class don't go 'o such places."</p>
        <p>'Tis the unexpected that happens,</p>
        <p>" remember." "True"—with a moan—"or we should not be breaking our journey at such a pension. Truly indeed 'tis the unexpected that happens." The next few hours were spent by Gwendoline in packing her own and her mother's belonginc;-. Accustomed all her life to a smart maid, Who waited upon her hand and foot, this ma no easy task to the young girl, and the work progressed but slowly. "'Tis well that Selina did not come with us," she thought, as she bent, with an aching hack over her mother's large trunk. "She would have been useful, but her wages and ticket home would have ruined us."</p>
        <p>Weary and slow seemed the journey to Pisa, and when at last they found themselves in that quiet, picturesque little town, both mother and daughter were thankful that they had decided to spend the night there. "If only the. Signora Prato can give us rooms," thought Gwendoline ,anxiously, as they drove along, their cab laden with trunks and packages, "all will be well. Hotels are beyond us now." •</p>
        <p>Evening was closing in; and when in answer to their cabman's loud ring, the door of the big, high house on the Lung' Arno was thrown open, it was difficult to distinguish any person or thing in the dark hall. But presently a small, thin woman in a short brown skirt, a black cloth jacket buttoned up to her neck, her scanty grey hair combed backed from her sad little face and twisted into a knot at the back of her head, stepped out, and, smiling and bowing, welcomed the two ladies in a mixture of Italian, French, and English, that was perplexing and difficult to understand.</p>
        <p>"YOU havp rooms ankean take us in, signora?"</p>
