        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY CASE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>Prepared by W. T. OWBRIDGE, Ltd., Hull. Sold eTerywhere in bottles, at 18. lid.,</p>
        <p>28. 9d., 48. 6d., and Ils</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>me tor saying iv, you iutsnovritiseestimoon two."</p>
        <p>Gwendoline smiled. "I don't think we'll stay here long. My mothar is anxious to get back to London."</p>
        <p>"I'm sorry for that. It would have been pleasant to have had a girl to talk to. These people are all such frumps! " As they took their places at the table Gwendo- line looked round and sighed.,</p>
        <p>, "Frumps indeed! lam glad my mother stayed in her room. They .would have driven her mad. I wonder what the show guest—number ten—is like? Ah, well, it matters little to me, as we shall certainly shake the dust of Pisa from off our feet to-morrow. How lucky he is to have come in for money. I hope he will spend it well. But how absurd I am." And then she turned to the girl upon her right, and questioned her about Pisa and the neighbourhood. _ _</p>
        <p>Next morning Mrs. Travers was far from well. Fretting and anxiety had weakened and upset her nerves. She was quite unfit for travel, and sadly dejected and sick at heart. Gvvendoline found herself obliged to remain for a week at least at the signorina's pension. "A few days' rest will do the poor dear good," she thought that afternoon as, her mother having fallen into a peaceful sleep, she stole through the empty drawing-room on to the balcony over- looking the Lung' Arno. "And we might be in a worse place. It is quiet and peaceful. The signorina is kindness itself, and her terms most moderate. Darling mother ! If only I knew what our future is to be. Oh, Fred, Fred, when my father scorned you as poor he little guessed what his own child would come to! wonder where you are—and if you ever think of me "</p>
        <p>The river flowed slowly and silently along. The streets were quiet, the day warm, and in the shady balcony Gwendoline leant her head against the railing and fell asleep. Suddenly a hand was laid upon her shoulder, and a merry voice cried out: "Caught napping. I thought you were gazing at the beautiful Duomo and leaning tower. Oh! if I bad known you were here I'd have called you in, for Mr. Fred Langdale has just been in the drawing-room to bid us all good-bye. He had to run for his train."</p>
        <p>"Fred—Langdale P" Pale as marble Gwendo- line raised a pair of questioning, bewildered eyes to the girl's laughing face. ,"Is he--" "Our show guest, I mean. Mr. Langdale—tall, young, handsome, and adored by all. But he's gone—off to Australia, and we "—putting her handkerchief to her merry face—"are left lamenting. But,..sou are i 11... I'll, get VQ% a elasi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>-.I wrote, aim got' no answbr. But, on! 1 see it all now—you were away, and only that by a lucky accident I lost my train to-day I should never have seen you again." "We were in Rome."</p>
        <p>"And I took your silence for— Oh Gwendoline "—he clasped her hand in both his own and gazed at her, his eyes full of love— "long ago you said you loved me and would have married me' poor as I was, had not your father forbidden it. I am well off to-day, through the will of a distant cousin. I love you, sweetheart. Will you marry me now ? " She raised her head, and laid one little white hand upon his breast. _ _ .</p>
        <p>"Yes-, gladly," she said, simply. "I always loved you, Fred, and vowed that if I could not marry you no other man should call me wife." "My beloved!" He drew her to his side and gazed with passionate and adoring love into her beautiful dark eyes. "Here is the water at last. I'm sure you thought I was never coming back," cried the English girl, gaily, as she stepped out upon the balcony. Gwendoline started away, crimson to the roots of her hair.</p>
        <p>"Don't</p>
        <p>"Mr. Langdale—you!" the girl cried. tell me you lost your train ? " "I must "—laughing. "It steamed out of the station as I drove up." "Oh, those good-byes I"—handing the water to Gwendoline. "Aren't you sorry?" "No. Glad—glad I lost my train, Miss Smith, to find happiness. Allow me "—catching Gwendoline's hand—"to present my affianced wife, Miss Travers." "Well "—Miss Smith gave a little scream "here's a surprise! I congratulate you both. Such an excitement! And now I'm off to tell the news."</p>
        <p>"I won't call her bad rubbish,'" laughed Fred, dropping on to a seat beside the girl al Miss Smith disappeared, "but 1 will say shei • a good riddance. Long may she stay away."._ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"-Amen to that," cried Gwendoline, "l want no one but you just now, dear Fred.' And, sighing contentedly, she slipped her hand into his.</p>
        <p>CURE END. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Lantern Service at Latchford</p>
        <p>The first of a series of magic lantern services in con- nection with the Latchford Baptist Church. was held on Sunday evening from six to seven in the schoolroom and was conducted by Mr. S. Albinson. The subject was "The Life of Moses," which was illustrated by 'views thrown on the screen by Mr. James Adams's lantern. There was a good attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Bold-street Men's Bible Class. The Church and Social Economy.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Men's Bible Class. The Church and Social Economy.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the Rev. J. F. Matthews visited the Bold-street Wesleyan men's Bible class, and gave an address on "The Church and Social Economy." Mr. J. C. Holland (president of the class) occupied the chair. He said he knew that a record attendance like they had that afternoon meant a warm welcome for Mr. Matthews. He, however, hoped for an extension of the same spirit to all their own members Who so ably and willingly served them from time to time as e ell as to friends outside the class, Mr. Matthews, who was cordially received, defined the Church as the great body of men and women outside and,inside the Churches' who were trying to practice the teaching of Jesus. The Social ceremony was the present industrial and commercial regime and the life- conditions involved. The battle in which they were engaged, continued the speaker, had resulted so far in the growth of power on the part of industrial democracy. There was evolving slowly under their very eyes a new state of society. The whole democracy, rightly or wrongly, was an organism moving with life, and instinct with activity. Its outlook was always widening. The well-being of the whole was its new point. In view of this what function had the church to perform ? It was still, said the speaker, that the Church was set for saving men ; for saving him in the "Narrower "sense of that term. They were concerned —the whole of society in fact was concerned—with the abnormal environment in which large sections of the population lived. They were responsible for it. If the Church had failed at all in the past, it was in leaving the people to work out their own Social Salvation. The time had come when more emphasis should be put upon the application of its own teaching to the facts and conditions of life.. Surely it was a curse for any town to own a slum ! The spirit of Jesus in the Church ought to make such ownership impossible. The same applied to the aggregation of the country's capital in the hands of a few men. It was intolerable among a free and enlightened people that the bread and butter of the many should be at the mercy of the few, man was born with a right to live, to eat, and to_worlr_ Nona was born witi. 54 T Int to sponge upon his fellow, whether the peer who drew a rent role from the labour of the many or the Filthy Wafer, that could work and wouldn't. Both were a prey upon the energy, food and labour of the commonwealth. If a man would not work neither should he est. But if amen should eat he ought to be made to %yolk. Society had to do business to send men adrift, and tell them there was no work for them, 'wither food nor place in the scheme of things. So the Church Sectionally and in its totality would have to face the facts connected with the production and dis- tribution of the wealth of the country. It must take the lead in framing a right and a just solution of the problem. And the means? Well, said the speaker, the means lay to hand. The promotion of piecemeal legislation which dealt with the gradual extinction of vast monopolies ; the substitution of the Communal good, for class greed; the nationalizing of great sources of wealth ; in these and other ways, and the Christianizing of all effort local and national, would make for an era of righteousness, peace,and prosperity. For while here it was their business to make it increasingly hard for a man to go wrong and to do wrong, and a delightful and altogether inspiring ideal to induce him to do right. They had, however,to get back to the one point from which he (Mr. Matthews) started. Man was an "X " quantity. After their best schemes had been considered, passed, and tried, after all had been done for man's education and demelopment he was human still and liable to err. His greatest safeguard and sure hope lay in Christ Jesus, who came into the world to save sinners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Temperance Sunday at Legh-street.</head>
        <p>Temperance Sunday at Legh-street.</p>
        <p>The annual temperance sermons were preached at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel on Sunday to large and attentive congregations. In the morning Mr. J. Whitley of Stockton Heath gave an earnest and interesting address. He said that the word "temperance" c‘ as mentioned three times in the Bible, the first being in Acts 24, verse 25, where Paul reasoned with Felix on Righteousness, Temperance, and a Judgment to come, and Felix trembled. From that, the preacher was led to believe that Felix must have been an intemperate man, and that the searching temperance address by Paul touched him to the quick. The word also appeared in Galatians 5, v. 23, and Peter 2, chap. 1, v. 6. Temper- ance had become in our day essentially a woman's ques- tion, for there was saarcely a mother in the land who did not mourn over some member of her family who bad become the victim of strong drink. Drunkenness and kindred evils were on the increase, too, among women themselves, and on the 15th of October last 15 women passed before the magistrates at Clerkenwell on charges of intoxication. Homes in Warrington to-day were dens of immorality and filth through strong drink and he affirmed that if ,they could only get the help of every woman in the land against the drink traffic it would be like the "lightning to the oak." The Government proposed tackling the drink question next year, and while the temperance people must be the advance guard in the fight they must get the moderate drinkers to help them. A minister in Warrington once said that £4,000 per wetk was spent on drink in this town—toAice as much as wls said to he spent on bread—and he had not seen these figures ques- tioned. If that £4,000 were spent on food and other necessaries, all want and distress would he done away with. He thought the present outlook for temperance was very encouraging. 1 hey had the assurance of the Prime Minister that temperance legislation would be one of the first duties of the next assembly of Parlia- ment, and they had also the satisfaction of knowing that a large number of the present Members of Parlia- ment were in sympathy with the cause, while the trades unionists were promoting a temperance organisatron of their own. No question had had so much tinkering at to so little purpose as temperance. They seemed to be always unsettling, but never settling, always grafting fresh branches but never altering the character of the fruit. Mr. Whitley referred to his experience as a Guardian and testified to intemperance being the chief cause of high poor rates and pauperism. The Chaplain of Birmingham Workhouse said that 99 persons out of every 100 who came to that Workhouse as paupers came there through drink. It was estimated that over 60,000 people died every year from theeffects of intemperance. The preacl.er then went on to refer to the result of the recent municipal elections and to quote the opinion of Mr. Dodds" that drink was the enemy, the bane of our family life, the source of almost all our crime, and the greatest hindrance to the' work of our Churches." He , also quoted from the " Examiner" that. "the temper- , liince party was supine and impotent, while the Churches I either Episcopal or Nonconformist never united in a practical way to fight the drink evil." No less than £166,421,911 were spent on chink in one year in this country, and £16,000,000 on tobacco, while less than £2,000,000 was contributed by all the Christian Churches for the spread of the Gospel. For every penny spent on the extention of the Kingdom of Christ, ten shillings were spent on drink and tobacco. The preacher con-</p>
        <p>1 eluded with a strong appeal to every man and woman to undertake the duty which is incumbent upon them to support with all their power the cause of temperance --to throw out the life-line, rescue the perishing, and ! save the dying. 1 The service in the evening Wtl3 conducted ,by Mr. John Griffiths, who also spoke effectively on the sub— ject of temperance.</p>
        <p>-.Cb--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CONCLUEIVE WARRINGTON LVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>CONCLUEIVE WARRINGTON LVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Conclusive evidence is evidence that puts an end to all doubt. 1-Jere is IVarrinaton evidence that must put an end to any doubt you may have about the permanency. of the cures made by Doan's Pills.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Oakley, l, Bond-street, Sankey, Warrington, says :—" I can truthfully say that Doan's pills have done me a lot of good, and I have recommended them to my mates. "I used to be a sufferer from drousy and kidney trouble, my limbs swelled very much. I was subject to, severe pains in the small of my back, hips, and loins. I consulted a doctor, and be advised me to go into art Infirmary. I was in there for a time, and I got a bit better. Ahogether I was laid up for ten weeks, and I got no real benefit -until I tried Doan's pills. "Doan's pills are a splendid medicine ; they have made me well again, and I shall take nleasure in speak- ing about them. (Signed), E. Oakley." After a lapse of more than eighteen months, one of our representatives had the pleasure of seeing Mr. Oakley, who said :—"I have not needed to use Doan's pills since they cured me so thoroughly. It was dropsy that I had, my body swelled considerably, and I was unable to pass the kidney secretions properly, which were unnatural in colour, painful in pass, and contained sediment; Doan's pills, however, caused them to pass naturally. I was treated for kidney trouble at the hospital, where I remained a month, twenty-one days in bed.</p>
        <p>"Doan's pills are indeed splendid. Numbers have asked me about my case, and I always give the medicine high praise. I can't do otherwise, four boxes of the pills cured me." Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence. Of all chemists and sto-es, or post free. direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford- street, London, %V. Doan's are the pills that cured Mr. Oakley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CORONER AND CHIEF CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>THE CORONER AND CHIEF CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>More Unpleasantness.</p>
        <p>Before opening an inquest at the P,lice Station, on Monday afternoon, Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, addressing Acting-Sergeant -thwart said : Do I Under- stand that the Chief Constable has given you order.s not to write the evidence out for me ?—Yes, sir. The Coroner,: I am astonished that the good reiations which have existed between this polic6' force and myself are to be shattered since the new Chief Constable came into existence. if he does not know his business I shall have to teach him.</p>
        <p>During the progress of a second inquest, the Coroner asked a witness what kind of a labourer her husband was.</p>
        <p>She replied that be was a payior's labourer. The Coroner : It only says labourer on ray paper. The police ought to know by this time what informa- tion I want. It is curious that after all these years the Warrington police cannot tell me the information I require. At the conclusion of the inquest,</p>
        <p>The Coroner, again addressing Acting-Sergeant Stewart, said : You have been getting these cases up for me, and you know that Sergeant Barlow got them up before you did, and you Inow that the practice of this Court has been for the evidence ,to he written out and typed for me to read to the witnesses in these simple cases. Acting-Sergeant Stewart: Yes sir. _</p>
        <p>The Coronr : Have you received instructions from the Chief Constable that in future-that- course is not to be pursued? Acting-Sergeant Stewart: Yes, sir. The Coroner : Do you know of any reason why it is not to be done? Did the Chief Constable give you any reason?</p>
        <p>Acting-Sergeant Stewart : He said be did not think it was our duty to do it. The Coroner : I want to be quite clear. You can convey to the Chief Constable my compliments and friy that we both stand on our strict legal right; with each other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>P.S A. Meetings</head>
        <p>P.S A. Meetings</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Farrar presided at a well attended meeting of the Wycliffe Society on Sunday. Mr. C. Rose, of Droylsden, gave the address. God's dealings with riaid, full uf thoughts concerning Him. The beauties of the earth, the daily mercies of God they could see and look upon. Although they could not see God's face and live, they could see and live upon His goodness, which filled their thoughts with gratitude and loving affection. Mr. •W. Booth sang two solos in good style "The Psalm of Life," and Thou didst not leave," and was greatly appreciated. Mr. W. H. Pay-ton presided at the organ. —The weekly meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. Society was held in the Co-Operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday, when considering the inclemeecy of the weather, there was a very fair attendance. The meeting was presided over by Mr. Brown, who spoke on " Christ as our pattern, our own ideal." Mr. Brown, of St. Helens, delivered an address on " P( ssibilities, or seeking and finding," and a violin solo entitled " Nazareth " was played by Miss A. Triage. The orchestra also rendered very valuable service. The lesson was read by Mr. Hedgecock; and prayer was offered by Mr. Heywood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WINTER ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>WINTER ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>An Agonising Complaint Cured.</p>
        <p>Zarn-Buk Cares 7.vhere Cheap Ointments</p>
        <p>Fall</p>
        <p>There are thousands of people now just beginning the annual torture of Winter eczema. Sickly, unhealthy skins soon break down under the stress of cold and rain. It is advisable for all our readers to note the first. symptoms of chaps, cold-sores, or pimple', hr these very often are the first signals of an outbreak of skin disease. In such cases as these the great remedy is Zam- Bilk, a rure, herbal, medicinal balm, which gets right down to the root, of the trouble, and soothes and cures in both acute and chronic cases.</p>
        <p>"I had my first attack eight years ago," says Mr. George Thompson, of Wolseley Bridge, near Stafford, "when. an eruption brae out on my hands and arms, which stvelled and discharged a great deal. When my dxtor had done all he could for me I was still very bad, arid became an in-patient at the Birminghana Queen's Hospital. During the first week in the hospital the erup-- tiors discharged a great deal, and I had to lie on my back with my arms upon a rack for some time. The doctors told me I was suffering from gouty eczema (the severe form (J the disease which is most prevalent in damp and 'cold weather). I improved under the treatment, but when I came- out of the hospital the &amp;ctor told me the disease might b.eak out again any time. It did come' np,rt ma again, and in a most horrible way. I was soon one mass nf sores from head to foot, my eyes were all made up, and my hair came off my head.</p>
        <p>" The doctor s:Jid it would kill me. He said he never saw anyone in sucii a condition. The diSease took the form of lamp discoloured -lumps, which discharged a lot of nasty, sticky fluid. My wife used to wash me and 'dress me night and morning, and sponge me all over. I need not describe the agonies I went through. I had all kinds of remedies, end 1 was bathed with special soaps. In fact, I tried a lot of ointments and medicines; which, however, only seemed to drive the disease in it stead of killing it. I suffered constantly for 'twelve months, and then my shin came off in scales from head to foot like the scales of a fish. It peeled off two or three times, and ever afterwards I had a return of the scaling every 3 ear, especial'y in the winter mouths. While I was suffering very badly I read about Zam-Buk, and got a sample box. I was led to get a full supply, and noticed how the applica- tions began to put the skin into a much healthier condition. The p:eling or so ding by degrees got less, the sores were gradually healed up, and now, after persevering with Zam Buk, I ant entirely free from the distressing disease which torture Ime so long. Nor have I had any return of it since. -My cure is all the ;Imre wonderful, seeing that I am nearly 80, and I am convinced that Zam-Buk has no equal as a pure skin-cure."</p>
      </div>
