        <p>colour Of the satin,.( This embroidery is kin- tinued on the edge of the draped, wide over. sleeve, and has a wonderfully rich and effective appeafance. Above this embroidery comes a vest of filet lace, and above that again a vest of ivory net edged with a binding of gold tissue argl tucked, perpendicularly. Above, again appears another net vest, tucked horizontzAly this time, inserted with bands of filet— lace, and finished with a little tucked neck-band of the net. The sleeves are also of net, and are semi-transparent, being rucked lightly from shoulder to wrist.</p>
        <p>A.SECRET OF SITCES,S.</p>
        <p>Confidence in our own powers is a secret of success, says Miss Jeanie Victor Bates in - the World and His Wife. It is possible to enter the world equipped with characteristics, talents, and attributes likely to ensure . success, and end by leaving it as "nobodies" simply through want of confidence in these same qr.; tides. They who</p>
        <p>are subject to uc osc ill cilia that it must be conquered, else, a 5 time passes, !t will form an ever-widening 'chasm between them and their fellows.</p>
        <p>To-DAY'S RclrE</p>
        <p>RIB DE VEAtr AUX OLlVES.—Parboil a couple of calves' sweetbreads. Place two ounces of butte' in a stewpap with two tablespoonfula of flour, and stir until the mixture is smooth end the flour has taken a good I,rown colour. Moisten by degrees with three-quartrs of a pint of stock, and stir until the sauce boils. Add a dessertspoon- lui musnroom Ketenup enli six olives, pre- viously stoned and cut up small. Season with pep- per and salt, and then add the • sweetbreads. When ready serve garnished with fried crouton. ind</p>
