        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>GIL BE RT SHELDON'S</p>
        <p>LOVE AFFAIR.</p>
        <p>BY FITZGERALD MOLLOY, fluthor of "The Mysterious Palace," "The Devire Head," "Donisthorpe's Destiny," "Just at ! Moonrise," "Two Women," &amp;c:</p>
        <p>The drawing-room windows stood wiae open to the balcony, whose palms were motionless in the breathless July air, whose striped awning shaded the fierce glare of the afternoon sun. The crowd ! inside had been invited by Mrs. Anselem Penkarven ; to her At Home, and was made up of smart people, together with a sprinkling of musicians—singers who delighted in hearing their own voices and : pianists who exhausted themselves in endeavours ' to drown conversation.</p>
        <p>Close by the Moorish arch dividing the great drawing-rooms sat Mrs. Baddley, a woman of , ample proportions and cynical views, who spent her 1 life in regretting the follies of other people; whilst beside her was Mrs. Rhoden Smyth, a simple, I sweet-natured soul, who, whilst living in the suburbs, desired to be considered fashionable. "There," said the former, who was pointing out various people to her friend, "there is Mrs. John, Darla," and, with a wave of her fan, she indicated ! a tall, elegantly-dressed woman, conspicuous by her distinguished appearance and disdainful air. "She is very handsome," Mrs. Rhoden Smyth murmured, appreciatively. "No one knows that better than herself. She gives herself intolerable airs. No one would sus- pect that before her marriage she and her sisters had but one visiting-gown between them, and that was bought at a cheap sale and made by the village dressmaker."</p>
        <p>. "But I heard," Mrs. Rhoden Smyth said, "that she is the daugher of a baronet." "So she is, and her forefathers were Crusaders; but what good was that to her when her own father was a pauper? I come from the same county, and knew them—slightly. The baronet could never pay his butcher; and as for his wife, though a God-fearing woman, she was never seen at church for twenty years—her bonnets were so bad." Mrs. Rhoden Smyth's well-regulated mind was shocked at hearing so distressful an anecdote of the aristocracy. "When a girl," Mrs. Baddley continued, "Mrs. Dark used to cry from vexation because she couldn't attend the county ball. For she knew she was handsome, and that if she had opportunities, she could marry -a wealthy man." "But she did." •</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes, she married a self-made man, as honest as he is vulgar. He called on her father one day about timber that was for sale on the estate, and he met Miiiam, with her pale face and her yellow eyes that always seemed to hold some mystery. He had never talked to anyone like her before, and he was unable to forget her; so he came again and again about the timber, until Sir Jasper began to wonder why he wasted so much time over a transaction already satisfactorily settled. The fact_ was that Miriam always contrived to meet him by the merest accident, either in the grounds or in the house, un,il at last she proposed to him." "She did ? "</p>
        <p>"She saw he would never have had the courage. There was such a commotion when he asked her father's consent. Poor Sir Jasper, a weak-voiced little man, with a bald head and a bilious constitu- tion., referred to his ancestors and burst into tears; whilst his Wife, whose fingers were worn to the bone through sewing household linen, fainted right away. However, Miriam, who wasn't going to lose her chance of being a rich woman, declared that as she had given John Dark her word, she was in honour bound to keep it. But she duly impressed him with the sacrifice she made? and has never allowed him to forget it." "Perhaps she was fond of him," Mrs. Rhoden Smyth suggested. "She was fond of his money. He worships her all the more because she despises him, poor man." ."Despises him ? " "My dear, did you ever know anyone that was benefited who was grateful? He gave her horses and carriages, jewels and servants, town and country houses; in fact, he did everything she desired short of making her his widow; and, as a consequence, she sneers at him and goes about with an air of melancholy as if she_ were_a figure weep- ing on her own tombstone; whilst she -talks to romantic young men 'about her empty life. I know her, my dear, for my eldest boy was crazy about her for nearly six weeks." "I suppose she helps her family ? " said Mrs. Rhoden Smyth, anxious to discover some virtue in this 'much-discussed lady. ' -"She gives-her sisters her cast-off gowns, that I know from seeing them on their backs; but they have no chance of 'catching a husband whilst she is near. Not that they can ever directly trace her interference, or she would hear of it, but her heart- broken pose and her yellow, eyes draw all the men around her; and besides, young married women are always so subtle and so seductiv,3. I know of three lads she has sent to the bad, not that she ever commits herself, for I believe her heart is as cold as a stone. As for my boy-" "Someone is going to sing." . . _</p>
        <p>"What a bother- that 'Our hostess forces us to listen to all this squalling, instead of letting us talk to our friends when we meet them I I really can't stand it, so I will go and have some straw- berries and cream, and an ice atisl some wafe,rs, and a little claret cup. 'Won't ou come, my deir ?"</p>
        <p>Mrs. John Dark, standing in the centre of the room, listened to the song with a rapturous gaze and dreamy air until it ended, when she started in surprise at being spoken to by Gilbert Sheldon, whom she had seen enter the room five mint 39 earlier.</p>
        <p>Gilbert Sheldon was a very young man, who a year. before had won the. Newdigate prize for a poem on "Cassandra." He had been hailed as a genius by a Hale band of undergraduates, and he had accepted as just this estiMate Of himself com- ing from judges so .mature'dand critics so experi- enced. As a consequence, he had turned his back on the professions that he might gain fame as a noet. On leaving Oxford he took rooms near Ha nri3tead H:ath, and published a volume of verse in Vigo-street. - 'Before going to college he had been educated by an elderly tutOr, and had lived with his widowed mother, a gentle, old-fashioned lady, whose views of life were Arcadian.. Those of her son were some- what similar. He had never, been brought into contact with deceit, his • nature was upright and simple, and to him women were ideal creatures,' guileless, preservers of truth, practisers of the virtues, worthy of all worship. - On fist meeting Mrs. John Dark he was struck by her pathetic fcc ard her subdued manner, that linted at the so:-row it stvpressed. Her eyes, Vait he compared to amber, scarcely glanced at ho, and she listened to hin with an abstracted air. He was wholly unconscious that she had really been attracted by his pale, grave face, with its deep blue eyes, pointed chin, and macs of da:dc hair. At their second meeting she gave him the permission he asked, to. call on her. Befoie a minth ha,-1 passed he began -to feel she regarded him as a i id, and she had frequently seemed on the point f 4iv;ng• him her confidence. Once she used the Phrase ‘.‘unmated f:?0111;._" w:iich he did not fully understand until he saw her hush ind, a short, round-figured little man, with a red face, square hands, and a hearty manner. Then a new light was thrown on the fate of this beintiful and suffering woman, and the poet's .heart was fined with misery. His despair became ihnipportable until he wrote a canto _expressing his feelings. This he hoped,to read to one day; it would shew her his sympathy, it would tell her of his love. .</p>
        <p>When he joined ker at Mrs. Penkarven's party, he said: "If I had known you would be here so early, I should have come long ago." "I didn't intend to come at all, nor wonld I be here, only I hoped the crowd might amuse me," she answered, sadly. "And has it ? "</p>
        <p>"How can you rsk ?" she said, reprovingly. "It has only made me feel my own loneliness, my Enner solitude a thousand times more."</p>
        <p>He could not trust himself to reply for some time ; but eventually be said: "Let us.. get away from the crowd and sit down in the conservatory; it was empty when I passed a minute ago." . "I Should like to go with you," she answered, in a low caressing voice, laying stress upon the last word, "but it would be ind:screet ; you don't know how envious, how censorious women are, and he is dreadfully jealous." The poet turned pale at reference to that monster of a husband.</p>
        <p>"I htiye much to say, and vet I seldom have a</p>
        <p>chance ot speaking to you alone. Will you come ! to tea with me on Thursday? You have nevet seen to my rooms, and I should like you to see my surroundings—where I write, where I dream ! of you." "That is wrong of you," she said, with a : reproachful nod of her beautiful head. "How can I help it? You are not angry ? Show me that you. are not. I want to talk to you and to read you a poem you have not seen." "I will try." "Oh, thank you, thank you," he burst out, gratefully. "But I may not be able to manage it," she remarked, knowing the value of suspense in increas- ing ardour. "My sister Agatha is staying with me, and sheavould hardly like me to visit you; she is so severe with me. "What's to be done?" he asked, despairingly, "I will ask Lady Lapthorn to chaperon me," she said, giving him a rrapid comprehensive glance, for dear old Lady Lapthorn was almost as deaf as a post. He blushed, and stammered his expreFsi Ms of delight. "Agatha is coming for me, and I must leave you," she said. "But I shall see you on Thursday ?" "I can't promise; I will try, but you snow I have to be so goarderl ; I am not my own mistress; I am only a slate," she answered, with a resigned pathetic smile that. went straight to his heart. When presently she drove home with her sister, the latter said: "I saw you talking to Gilbert Sheldon." "Everyone in the room shared your advantage," answered Mrs. John Berle, who had' suddenly lost her pathetic manner, which was now sharp and ill- tempered. "Yes; but they don't know as I do, that you are making a fool of him." "I agree with you; for it's only someone who is envious and jealous that could think so." "I am neither one nor the other, but I see you are treating him as you did the others." "My dear Agatha," said the married lady, sweetly, "I am not. to blame if men don't look at you; it's not my fault that you were born plain." "Y( u are 'a heartless woman," replied_ Agatha. "Indeed. Well, as it, must be disagreeable to you to live with such a person, perhaps 3ou had better return home to-morrow." Agatha thought. of her dreary home life, with her dull, pompous father, her melancholy, over- worked mother, and the small irritating economies they practised. There she would have no servants to wait on her, no carriage to drive in, no parties' to attend, and no one to talk with save the curate, who had only two topics of conversation—vest- ments and his cough. She looked straight before her, striving to keep back her tears, whilst she compromised with herself by resolving that though she would not go back next day, she would certainly refuse the next cast-off gown her sister offered her. When Thursday afternoon came Mrs. John Darla declared that she suffered from a racking headache, and sent her sister out to a picture gallery. But no sooner had the door closed upon the latter, than Miriam summoned her maid and put on her most becoming costume. When her looking-glass assured her she was looking her best, she ordered the carriage and leisurely drove to Lady Lapthorn; for she was in no haste to keep her appointment, knowing that delay would only increase the poet's anxiety to see her. Lady Lapthorn, who had a refreshingly simple mind and a keen appreciation of herself, was much sought after as a chaperon by young married women; her deafness rendering her very popular. Through the medium of her ear-trumpet, Mrs. John Dark had already assured her that die air of Hampstead Heath would greatly benefit her • and that ,whilst in its neighbourhood it would be a kindness to call on a protégé of hers whom she was striving to help in his literary career. "I think he has decided talent," she commented. "Poor lad, he wishes me to hear some verses he has written that I may be able ta give a personal opinion of them to the editor of the Pantheon, whom I know; and I would willingly do what I can to help him for the sake of his mother." That she had never seen and cared nothing for his mother, was a mere detail; it was more im- portant to impress Lady Lapthorn with a sense of her kindness. Meanwhile the poet was standing at a window of his sitting-room, eagerly listening for sound of the carriage that had not yet left Mrs. John Dark's house in Cavendish-square. The afternoon was deliciously warm. The sun steeping the heath which he overlooked, the blue sky, a sight of distant woods, the glimpse of a winding river, a church spire just distinguishable through the hazy atmosphere, presented- a scene whose beauty struck him even whilst he longed for a sight of that pale pathetk face with its yellow eyes. The tea-table was already laid and there were roses, red and white, in the deep blue vases, and in the great bowl of burnished copper. Their perfume hung in the air they sweetened, and mixed with the smell, of gorse blown through the open windows carefully sheltered from tLe sun.. All was ready for her, yet time passed without bringing her. From exultation at the prospect of seeing her, be sank into bitter disappointment, from which he was eventually :bused by hearing the sound of carriage wheels, when, jumping from his chair, he rushed to a window to see her at last. And how beautiful she looked, her divine eyes shining like stars out of the pale heaven of her face! He clasped her hands and thanked her for her visit. She could hardly get him to notice Lady Lapthorn, who, seeing him for the first time, thought him "a nice boy,-" and considered it a pity he should waste his time in writing verses. A glance round the room shewed Miriam her portrait, elaborately framed, standing on a little table, a solitary heart-red rose in a specimen glass placed before it. Whilst passing it on her way to admire the view, she set the photograph on ita face before her companion had time to notice it. Tea was brought in, and whilst they drank it, Lady Lapthorn adjusted her ear-trumpet, towards which, however, no conversation was directed. The poet believed himself the happiest man in the world—the world that wore its brightest aspect to celebrate the day he was visited by the woman he worshipped. He could scarcely take his eyes from her face, he longed. to throw himself at her feet, and implore her to leave her miserable bondage and go with him wherever she chose; for where she was would be his paradise. If he did not tell her so in plain prose, lie certainly did in the poem he read to her, whilst his voice vibrated with emotion and his heart went out to her. Miriam knew it was she who was idolised under the n m of some mythical personage; and for a moment the youth and his fervour stirred her heart, So that when he raised his eyes to hers they seemed to him as suns that scorched his blood. When lie had finished, Lady Lapthorn, who caught a word here and there, asked: "Is that all ? " And then, receiving no reply, declared "It was very pretty; very pretty, indeed." Mrs. John Thule, rose up, and said, in a low tone: "I shall never forget this day." "Nor I; nor I," he resp-mded, ardently. • "I must go; I am dining out, and shall scarcely have time to dress. Ali, Loa' I hate the world How happy you must be here, surrounded by all this peace and beauty," she said, sadly. "Miriam," he whispered. Pretending not to hear him, she turned away ,and followed Lady Lapthern downstairs ; but just ohs her friend was getting into the carriage, Mrs. John Darle believed she .had left her handkerchief in the sitting-room. The poet rushed back for it, without being able to find it. As he turned towards the door he saw her standing there. "I have found it ; it liras in my belt all the time," she told him. Then she gave him her ungloved hand, which he kissed again and again rapturously. A rose that had been in her breast fell to the ground; he stooped to pick it up, and_ she hastened away., Three days later she received a note from the poet, begging that she would see him alone, if only for-five minutes. She made an appointment whiCh he—who had -been wandering near her house for an hour previously—punctually kept. The instant she saw him, she noticed some change had taken place in him. He looked as if he had not slept for nights, a hectic pink flared in his cheeks, he trembled from nervousness. She was quite calm, not less melancholy than previously, but far more cold; for, cautious and calculating as she I was, she considered her flirtation with him had gone far enough, and feared that as he was not a man of the world, lie might make a scene. She sat in a great chair in a tiny drawing-room all pink and gold, crowded with costly trifles, and : with flowers everywhere. When the servant who •announced him had disappeared, he went over to Miriam quickly and clasped her hands. His own were burning. "It's an eternity since I saw you," he said, fervidly. "Only three days," she replied, in a matter-of- fact tone. "Since then I have scarcely eaten or drank or</p>
        <p>slept. I could do nothing but Minx ox you, song for you. "Foolish boy!" "I love you. I cannot live without you!" "This is really very wrong." she said, in gentle reproval. "I know that this marriaffe into which you were so cruelly forced is abhorrent to you; I can imagine what you suffer through the companionship of a man whom the law has made your husband, but whose wealth could never buy your heart, your love. Oh, tell me," he cried out, he fell on his knees, "that they are mine atone! "For goodness' sake, get up! I don't know who may come into the room, she said, sharply. "Tell me you will come away from this place that must remind you of your bondage, that you will let me carry you to some land where we shall be happy together." "I won't speak to you until you sit down on that chair." _ _</p>
        <p>"You love me—you love me ? " he pleaded, his voice shaking. "I like you—as a friend," she answered, anxious to pacify him, lest, carried away by emotion, he might cause a disturbance in her well-regulated house. _ .</p>
        <p>"As a friend," he repeated, as dazed as if he had received a blow. _ _</p>
        <p>"I really wonder bow you can imagine that I loved you; I'm sure I mv.:x said so."</p>
        <p>"Not in words, but—" "As a poet, no doubt your imagination distorted mere acts of kindness or friendship into something more," she said, hurriedly. "Of course, you are very young, and bound to make mistakes," she added, endeavouring to soothe him, for she was desirous of keeping him, tame and devoted, in her train of admirers.</p>
        <p>"You have never loved me ? " he asked ono.; more.</p>
        <p>" Never. "</p>
        <p>"Then have deceived myself, I have deceived myself—that is all," he muttered, as he rose, and, without looking at her, made his way to the door slowly and with uncertain steps, for he was blind with pain—with a pain that kills. "Gilbert, Gilbert!" she called out.</p>
        <p>He neither turned nor replied, but went down the stairs and out of the house, feeling crushed by misery. On his way he had passed Agatha, who had returned sooner than her sister expected; but he took no notice of the girl, who was frightened by his woe-stricken face and the vacant stare of his eyes. Rushing 'upstairs, she saw her sister standing on the threshold of the room he had just left.</p>
        <p>"Oh, what have you said, what have you done ? " she asked, excitedly. Miriam answered her by banging the door in her face. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The following night Mrs. John Dark had dressed herself with unusual care for a ball at which Prince Xenophon had assured her he would be present. The Prince was singularly handsome, and those who inspired his ardour were not left long in doubt of its intensity. Exhaling delicious perfume, glittering with jewels and superbly gowned, she went to the little room where she had last seen the poet, and where her sister was now waiting for her. Agatha sat with an evening paper on her knees, her eyes red from tears, her face horror-stricken.</p>
        <p>"Something terrible has happened 1" she burst out on seeing Miriam. "Something too dreadful 1" "Anything that concerns me ? " the latter asked, "Gilbert Sheldon is drowned."</p>
        <p>After the blank silence of a second, Miriam said: "Accident ? "</p>
        <p>"The letter in his pocket leaves no doubt that It was suicide." _ _</p>
        <p>"A letter!" she gasped, startled out of her frigidity. "Addressed to his widowed mother. He was her only child. Oh, poor soul, poor, stricken soul, how I pity her!" "Instead of keeping the news until to-morrow, I suppose you tell it to me now in the hope of spoiling my pleasure ? " said Miriam, angrily. "Pleasure? Surely you don't think of going to this ball ?"</p>
        <p>_ "Do you know of any reason why I shouldn't ?" Mrs. John Dane asked, severely. "Yes," answered Agatha as she rose, her face flushed, her eyes indignant.. "You killed him, you killed him, and you know it, .selfiesh, heartless snd wicked as you are. I will leave this house to-morrow morning and never enter it again." "I don't think the loss will be mine," her sister calmly replied, as she went downstairs to where her husband and her carriage awaited her. ;</p>
        <p>[Tux END.]</p>
      </div>
