        <p>Mr. DEAKIN, the Australian IMPERIAL Premier, whose sense of bis FREE TRADE. importance to the Empire is scarcely less than was that of the late RICHARD SEDDON, does not think that the whole of the time of his English visit was thrown away. Something at least was done of which he approved. He has asked Lord NORTHCOTE to say of the conference on Mer- chant Shipping legislation, over which Mr. LLOYD-GEORGE presided, that "it provel the value of such meetings for the free and full discussion of complicated and important subjects." Some among the Colonial Premiers, and Sir WILFRED LAURIER in particular, we imagine would say this of the larger Conference which, said Mr. BALFOUR in his recent speech, was "so hastily dissolved.' With the exception of SIR ROBEhT BOND, who has since proved to be badly mistaken, Mr. DzeioN, and Sir WILLIAM LYNE, the Colonial representatives at the Co' nference seemed to be very well satisfied with it, and it must have surprised them to learn from Mr. BALFOUR that they had been hustled out and that the door had been slarnmel behind them.</p>
        <p>But Mr. BALFOUR had his purpose to serve, and was, besides, acting under stress of weather. What he promised his rebellious supporters the other day was that as soon as his party returns to power the Imperial Conference shall again be summoned, for the purpose of considering, as it would appear, whether we cannot establish Free Teade within the Empire. Free Trade within the Empire means such a condition as exists within the United States, where no State erects tariff barriers against its neighbour, and where there is, indeed, complete Free Trade throughout the Republic. For the purpose, of establishing Free Trade within the Empire, all our self-governing Colonies will be required to abandon those tariff walls which they have built up against us, and in return we shall continue to them their present right of sending their products into our markets duty free. They may go as far as they like in keeping foreign goods out of the Colonies by means of tariff restrictions ; but they must accept English goods without taxation, JOHN BULL, of course, consenting to place on himself the burden of Protective tariffs in order to please his obliging Colonies. Is this what Mr. BALFOUR means by Free Trade within the Empire, the promotion of which is "not only the most important of all our commercial interests, bat appeals most directly and imme- diately to the heart of every man who feels that he is a member ef a world-wide Empire?" He either means this or nothing. Yet we do not hesitate to say that if Mr. BALFOUR were again in office, and the promised Conference were called,the Colonial representatives would not look at such a scheme for ten minutes. And, as a matter of fact, Mr. BALFOUR knows that they would not. He professes to have been brought to believe in the feasibility of Free Trade within the Empire by what was said at the Colonial Conference. Well,in what was said at that Cooferance, there was nothing so emphatic as the declaration of Sir WILFRID LAURIER to the effect that Canada would not listen to proposals for abolishing taxation on English iMports. There is not one of the Colonies which is anxious, or even willing, to adopt a Free Trade within the Empire system. The phrase does I not attract, or, at any rate, it does not deceive our shrewd Colonial kinsmen. They understan d it as proposing to put the British manufacturer on a footing of equality with Colonial manufacturers in Colonial markets, which is just what it means, and they will have nothing to do with it. This has been made so exceedingly clear in so many ways that any politican who speaks of the Colonies as if they desired or would accept such an arrange- ment has either failed to study the subject or is I bent on misleading his countrymen.</p>
      </div>
