      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>The first team of the Stockton Heath Club journeyed to Formby on Saturday last and after a very pleasant game they beat the home team by 2 goals to 1. The forwards in the Stockton team are certainly improving and the way each player manipulates his stick is worth going a long way to see.</p>
        <p>The second team met Formby Second at Arpley on Saturday, and, after a very hard game the match was drawn each, team scoring 1 goal. Greening scored for Stockton Heath. The half-back line of the team is one of the best in this district for with Rathbone, de Jong, and J. Houghton the team ought to be proud of possess- ing such good players.</p>
        <p>The teams for to-day are as follows :—First team V. Rock Ferry at Arpley. Dr. Selby, E. Pierpoint and P. B. Hawtin, G. Hatton, A. H. Rathbone, and Eric Fairclough ; J. Bainbridge, W. Richardson, W. H. Banks, S. Baxter and H. Dolan; Referee, Mr. W. H. Carter.</p>
        <p>Second team v. Rock Ferry, at hock Ferry. Messrs. C. Stanley Woods, 13. Silcock and Jackson, J. Houghton, H. NV. Guest, and 0. de Jong; H. Meredith, L. Pier- point, C. Smith, N. Cook, and A. Hodgkinson ; Referee, Mr. N. W. Bellamy. For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>Rescue from Drowning. A boy aged five years, son of Mr. Llewellyn Thomas, of 10, Ripley-street, fell into the canal at Bewsey, on Monday afternoon, but he was, fortunately, got out by two men whose names are at present unknown. He was apparently little the worse for his immersion. The House of Lords.</p>
        <p>The debating team of Warrington Catholic Y.M. Society accomplished a splendid performance the other evening at a meeting of the Liverpool Y.M.S. Debating league by defeating St. Mary's, Liverpool,. in a keen debate on the abolition of the House of Lords. The Liverpool team were for, and the Warrington team against, the abolition. The opening and leader- ship of Mr. H. Rigby, with the assistance of Messrs. Wilfrid Rigby, J. Dolan, E. Rigby and J. Black, proved too much for the attacking team, who had to acknowledge the adverse verdict of the adjudicators.</p>
        <p>TABLE, FLOOIt AND HANGING LAMPS. New Designs now showing.—J, S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Penny Popular Concerts. Another exceedingly large audience assembled in Bold-street schools on Saturday evening and a highly enjoyable programme was submitted. The chair was occupied by the Rev. J. F. Matthews, who delivered a short address during the interval, chiefly to the young people present, urging the need for the development of courtesy. Songs were given, with effect, by Messrs. Hannan and Hale and Gilbert. Miss Walker greatly pleased the audience with her recitations, and Mr. W. Pearce gave a pianoforte solo. Mr. W. W. Whittle created great merriment with his humorous sketches and was repeatedly encored. Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt., 'LINSEED COMPOUND,' Frade Mark of Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp;Colds.--Advt. Manslaughter Charge Falls.</p>
        <p>At the -Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday Catherine Kelly, Morris's-court, Warrington, was charged with the manslaughter of her thirteen-months-old child, John Kelly, She pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Rigby Swift, for the prosecution, said a coroner's jury returned a verdict of manslaughter against the prisoner arising out of circumstances which tended to show she had neglected the child. The case was subsequently brought before the Warrington magistrates, and having heard the evidence, the Bench thought it was a case which ought to be sent for trial. He (Mr. Rigby Swift) did not propose ts offer any evidence against the woman that morning.-31r. Justice Bray said he quite concurred in that course, and directed the jury to return a verdict of " Not guilty."—Prisoner was then discharged. " HEALRD " KITCHEN RANGES and " Peveril</p>
        <p>Fire Grates are unsurpassM—lnspection invited. —J. S. GARLAND, 51, 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Advt. Warrington Ruffian Punished.</p>
        <p>James-Leonardy, who was described by Superinien- dent Monaghan at the Warrington Police Court, on Monday as one of the characters in the neighbourhood, was sent to gaol for six months for a serious assault with a knife on his mother on November 9. It was stated by the police that complainant, along with another son, was having supper on the night in question, when the prisoner came home drunk. A quarrel arose between the two sons, and prisoner picking up a knife struck his brother on the head with it, causing a severe wound. In separating her two sons Mrs. Leonardy received three wounds on the arm. Sergeant Evans, who was called in the next morning, found the house in a shocking state of disorder, the floor of the kitchen being spattered with blood. The mother, in giving evidence, said prisoner did not do it wilfully.</p>
        <p>Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. 'COACULINE,' KLINK,"TEN ASITINE.LCements for broken and other articles.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Defrauding the Railway Company. John Walsh, Longford-street was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday with travelling on the London and North-Western Railway from Preston to Warrington without having paid his fare and with intent to avoid payment thereof.—Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—Detective Coward of the London and North- Western Railway Co., said that prisoner had been employed by the firm as a goods porter and he there- fore knew the " run " of the station. Witness watched the prisoner board the train at Preston and on arrival at Warrington he gave up a ticket dated July 7th. Witness followed prisoner along Crosfield-street and then gave him in the custody of P.C. Gibson.—Mr. Steel said that the detective had no authority to give the prisoner in custody. Prisoner heard somebody running after him so he stopped. He told the detective that he had given up a ticket dated July and if it was wrong he would pay for it.—The defence was that the prisoner thought the ticket was available for six months. The ticket had been paid for and prisoner should have taken it back and iot his money refunded. Superintendent Monaghan said that prisoner had been fined ten shilling and costs for a similar offence.— Prisoner was fined twenty shillings including costs or fourteen days.</p>
        <p>nouLTON TOILET WARE and Dinner Sets. See Windows.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNORIR Tait CLOCK ?oil e BOOTS IN LANCASHERE.—AdVt.</p>
        <p>Bookmaker Fined r£s. At the Borough Police Court, on Saturday, John Howard, Hamilton-street,was charged with frequenting Shaw-street for the purpose of betting the previous day.—The Chief Constable said P.C.'s Reddington and Woolacott were in Shaw-street, and, between 12.30 and one o'clock, they saw the defendant receive no fewer than ten betting slips from ten different persons and each apparently contained money. The police had had a large number of complaints regarding street betting, and on Friday strategy bad to be resorted to in order to catch prisoner. They had a strong pair of field glasses and there was no doubt about the facts of the case. This was the first case which had been taken under the new Street Betting Act which gave the police very much more power than they previously possessed. The defendant had been carrying this on for a considerable time. When searched, the prisoner had no less than 43 betting slips on him, and they referred to bets— 84 at sixpence, some at a shilling and two at two shillings. The sum of £5 Ss. sd. was found in his possession in addition to a sporting paper. Defendant did most of his business among poor people —Defendant was fined £5 and costs,ss. 6d.—The Chief Constable wished to take the opinion of the Bench as to what should be done with the betting slips. The Bench had power to confiscate them, but if they did it might lead to some difficulty with the people who had made the bets, although he had no desire to shield them.—Mr. R. T. Fairclough said such people should be punished as well as men like the defendant. —The Bench decided to confiscate the slips. LTNSEBD COnPOUND' for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles. Nd. ljli. Chemists.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Services for Sunday.</head>
        <p>Services for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church : Morning 10.30, Rev. E. .T. Gawne ; evening, 6.30 Rev. Thomas Miller. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church : Morning 10.30, Rev. T. Miller ; evening 6.30 Rev. Sidney Pitt. Golborne-street Baptist Church : Preacher Mr. D. Waddell, Liverpool. Morning subject " Christion Per- feetion" ; evening subject "The mocker of men and the deceiver of women." In the afternoon Mr. Waddell will give an address on the" Life and labours of Mr. W. P. Lockhart, Liverpool." Baptist Church, Lltchford : The Rev. John Francis Matthews will occupy the pulpit at this Church both morning and evening.</p>
        <p>Sir W. S. Gilbert, the humorous partner of the late Sir Arthur Sullivan in the production of the fatuous I Savoy comic operas, was seventy-one on Monday. lie is by profession a barrister, and in his early days he was elected a member of the Northern Circuit, to which he still belongs, although it is very many years ago now since he gaTe up any attempt at an active practice of his callings. The best known of Mr. Gilbert's many clever writings are his "Bab Ballads." These originally appeared in " Fun," when that journal was under the editorship of the late Toni Hood. It is interesting to know--and the statement is made by Sir W. S. Gilbert himself —that the first of the series, "The Yarn of the Nancy Bell," :vas originally offered to "Punch," to which paper Mr. Gilbert was at that time an occasional contributor. It was, however, declined by the then editor, on the, ground that "it was too cannibaliStic for his readers tastes."</p>
        <p>IA young man is allowed his crop of wild oats' but the girl must produce wheat of the best quality from her very earliest sowing, whatever the mental or moral climate that she moves in. —" Black and White."</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt.</p>
