      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WORKHOUSE SCANDAL. NURSE SUES RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER FOR SLANDER. Remal;ii)-3-;1-e Case.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE SCANDAL. NURSE SUES RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER FOR SLANDER. Remal;ii)-3-;1-e Case.</p>
        <p>There were many Warrington faces in the Nisi ;Prins Court at the Liverpool Assizes on Thursday, 'when a slander action in which considerable interest Was taken,was opened before Mr. Justice Pickford and a special jury. The plaintiff was Edith Dinnis, a nurse at the Warrington Union Infirmary, and the defendant Thomas E. Fliteroft, resident medical officer. Plaintiff claimed that she had suffered damage from the defendant falsely and maliciously speaking and Publishing a certain statement reflecting upon her .Moral character arid implying that she was unfit for her occupation The words were alleged to have been uttered on the 27th July to the Workhouse Master and to Nurse Biggin (superintendent nurse) and later to Ilerbert U. Todd. Plaintiff contended she had further suffered damage by other words maliciously spoken and published by the defendant in referring to a complaint made to the lady superiutendent by the phged aeintiff of his conduct. The latter statement was a to have been made on August 16th to the 'nembers of the Workhouse Committee sitting to investigate the matter. Defendant denied 'ravine- SPoken or published any of the words alleged in the first paragraph and in the alternative asserted that if he did so, they were used on the privileged occasion, Pad without malice to persons having a common Interest. He further denied having spoken or published Ile words alleged in the second paragraph and alterna- tively submitted that if he did use any of the words mentioned he did so in the execution of his duty as inedical officer and at a meeting of the Committee held to inquire into plaintiff's complaint. The statement Was therefore privileged and was made bona fide and 'without malice. Mr. Rigby Swift (instructed by Mr. H. Unsworth) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. W. • Taylor, K.C., and Mr. Cuthbert Smith (instructed bY Mr. T. S. Steel) represented the defendant. Before the jury were sworn, his Lordship asked if it Was inevitable that the case should be fought out and after the counsel had consulted, assured that it was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PLAINTIFF'S CASE.</head>
        <p>THE PLAINTIFF'S CASE.</p>
        <p>In opening the case, Mr. Rigby Swift said the action Was brought to recover damages for slander uttered by the defendant about plaintiff—a slander which, from the position of the parties and from the words them- elves, was of a very serious character, making a, very rave reflection upon the plaintiff, and compelling her tecome to court to clear her character. It would not Suggested by the defence that there was any justifica- tion or excuse for such an allegation as was atated to have been made. The plaintiff was a girl, twenty-Mo )'ears of age, the daughter of respectable parents, but ni a class of life in which it was necessary for her to earn her own living. When about eighteen years of age she went as a clerk to a firm of manufacturing chemists in Manchester, and after being there for about two years, she went to undergo training as a hospital ?.urse to the Birmingham Infirmary. Then she was a little time at home with her parents, and in March, this year, she went as a probationer nurse to the War- rington Workhouse Infirmary, to which institution the defendant was resident medical officer. From the time She went to the Infirmary up to the 26th July last no complaint of any sort against her moral conduct i Or the manner n which she had discharged her duties was made. She was under the medical Officer, the superintendent nurse, and various charge linrsen, and not one oi those people made any n,emplaint against her. On the morning of July 27, newever, Dr. Flitcroft, the med'cal officer, in the course ef conversation with the workhouse master, spoke of theplaintifT as being enceiente,and later in the day twice rePeated the statement to the superintendent nurse ,(rNurse Biggin) and the assistant master (Mr. Todd). here was not the slightest foundation for the charge. In the pleadings Dr. Flitcroft denied that he had said anything of the sort, but the three witnesses maintained that be had made the charge clearly, emphatically, and Plainly. He pleaded also that if he did use the words the occasion was a privileged one; such words might be Privileged if they were spoken honestly and bona fide .2* a person who believed them to be true to a person in whom it was his duty to make them, and without any improper or corrupt motive. It would, however, net only be shown that the words were not true, but also thSt he didn't believe them to be true,for the statement he bad made on oath showed that at the time he used lh,e words he had never considered the question. ,L he had believed it to be true it would ,ave been his business to enter it in his report cook, but he did not do so, and he never made any Vggestion for some weeks afterwards to the guardians. -131 no case was it his duty to tell the Workhouse Master anfi the Assistant Master, because it had nothing to do with them. They would tell the jury that the state- raent was not made to them in the form of a complaint -or a beziness communication. It was made, in a conver- sation and -they-did not regard it as a business com- tnunication. He (Mr. Swift) thought he vOould be able to show that it was made from dishonest, improper, and corrupt motives, his sole object being to blacken Plaintiff's character and to divert attention from a very</p>
        <p>6.7 A.,•1•••• • serious charge she had made against him at a meeting Of the Workhouse Committee on the 26th of July—that of having made improper suggestions to her. If the jury believed plaintiff made the statement in order to disarm the charge which the girl had made and to blacken her Character and to elicit the sympathy af these people for himself. there would be an end of the case and She would be entitled to a verdict which would clear her character and prevent him repeating such accusa- tions. But in that case they naturally would not ex- pect the inquiry to stop there. It was necessary for Lim to state the circumstances under which the girl raade the charge which provoked Dr. Flitcroft to make the unfounded suggestions. Before giving the history of the affair counsel asked the jury to consider the telative positions of the parties in the case. On the one side there was a girl, twenty-two years of age, who Was under the control almost entirely of the medical Officer of the institution whose advancement in her Profession was dependent on his good will. He was her instructor and examiner, and the person who could ,grant or refuse her a certificate. All the chances she aad of promotion was in keeping on the right side of the doctor. Under those circumstances one expected the doctor of an institution of that kind to assist and to protect the nurses under his charge. Fortunately in Most of our public institutions there was no ground for complaint in that direction. It would be for the iury to say whether the resident medical officer of the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary did discharge the duty 'Which was laid upon him or whether he had attempted to take unfair advantage of that position. On the night Of the 17th July the plaintiff was alone in charge on the ground floor of 36 patients. a great many of whom were suffering from nothing but old age, and required nothing but to be allowed to sleep out the night quietly. Between tyeelve and one o'clock the defendant came to Make his night round, and in contravention of the rules he came unaccompanied by the charge-nurse. He Went into the store-room, where the nurses wrote and left their reports, and Nurse Dinnis, as was her duty, came out of the kitchen to give any information or assistance he might require. Having read the report, the defendant went up to her and put his arm round her Waist. She wriggled away, and he then lifted her up and sat her down on the table. She kicked, and escaped from him into the corridor. He turned the light out in the store-room, and followed her out into the corridor, and then apparently resumed his round. On the night of the 18th he came alone again and suggested to her that it would be very nice for her if she were to go away with him for a week-end, and said that if she did all he wanted her, she would get on very much better in the hospital. He used every means fair and foul to get the girl to consent to his desires, but she refused, and again escaped from him. On the 23rd he was back again, and asked her if she had considered the proposal he had made to her, and she said, "I said 'no once, and I shall never change that answer." It was perfectly true, and, no doubt, the defence would make a strong point of the fact that on Pone of these three nights did the plaintiff shout or scream or complain, but counsel asked the jury, before ' rejecting her story, to again remember the relative posi- tions of the parties, and also that she was able to pro- tect herself and that her whole training bad been to Make her quiet, to make as little noise as possible, and to avoid exciting those under her care. That she was distressed there was no doubt, because on the 25th another nurse found her crying in her own room. They had a conversation, and, in consequence of that. she was taken to the superintendent nurse, and then taken straight away before a committee of the guardians, before whom on the 26th she told the story. Or. Flitcroft was not content with denying the charges, but he slandered the plaintiff in the words complained of, and made a further statement of a similar nature to the Guardians. Prior to the events mentioned he had not made complaints agninst the plaintiff but since the statement of claim nad been delivered he had on three occasions, the 11th, 18th, and 25th October reported her to the Guardians for neglect of her duties. Plaintiff was prepared to explain these matters which She said defendant would not have dreamt of bringing against her before. No doubt with the object of getting rid of her, he actually brought before the Uuardians amen of eighty years, who said that the nurse had made improper suggestions to him. He had also gone to Nurse Carr, to whom the plaintiff bad first confided the circumstances of the affair, and had told hey "You had better be careful what you say about rae or I shall report you for the most trivial offence and get you sent away from the Infirmary." Nurse Carr however, was a girl of some spirit, and took him before the Guardians for threatening her. At the time defendant made the allegations against 'plaintiff to the Workhouse Master and to the superin-</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; never fails, 1/li, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>tendent nurse he produced a letter which he said had been written by Nurse Dinnis, and said" Look at that. This is a nice sort of a girl to bring charges of that sort against me." What was in that letter the witnesses and the plaintiff did not know, but counsel was hoping it would now be produced In court. They wanted to know where Dr. Flitcroft got the letter from. If it was Nurse Dinnis's letter, what was he doing with it ? Counsel concluded by asking the jury to say that they believed defendant spoke the words prompted by the wicked desire to get out of a truthful charge which was being made against him, and that they would give the girl such damages as would show that her character was clear and that a man like the defendant was not to be allowed to treat her as he thought proper. PLAINTIFF IN THE BOX.</p>
        <p>Edith Dinnis, the plaintiff, was then called and gave evidence bearing out the statement of counsel. On the 17th July, defendant made improper suggestions to her and the following night he tried to bribe her by offer- ing to push her on in the profession and placing other temptations in the way Since she had made the charge against him,be had reported her several times but there was no foundation for his complaints. Mr. Swift: Was there any truth in the report he made against you with reference to the old man Hardy? None whatever.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. raylor, plaintiff said Mr. Mather presided at the meeting of the committee when she complained and the Guardians present included Mr. Jolley and Miss Brdadbent. Miss Broadbent is rather a friend of yours ?—She has been since I reported the doctor. That is what I thought. I think Mr. Jolley is too ? —Yes.</p>
        <p>Miss Broadbent has taken up your case, hasn't she ? No. My father took the case up. But hasn't Miss Broadbent ?—No, she has not. Not at all ?-1 daresay she has helped to get a little evidence together. The fact is slie took a rather strong view about it didn't she ?—Well, I don't know. _ _ _</p>
        <p>You were present at the committee and told your story. Miss Broadbent took a great interest in it didn't she ? Yes.</p>
        <p>And Mr. Jolley, too ?—Yes. Did she introduce you to a solicitor ?—No, she did not.</p>
        <p>How did you come to consult Mr. Unsworth ?—I asked for the address of a solicitor and she gave it me. Did she take you to Mr. Unsworth ?—No I went myself. How did you hear of these alleged slanders ?—Some of the senior nurses who had left-</p>
        <p>Told you of the slanders ?—Told me of the conduct of the doctor towards them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: I think lam entitled to the answer she gave. His Lordship: I think not. Further questioned plaintiff said that none of the three parties to whom the slander was uttered repeated it to her. She heard it from several nurses—it was going about all through the institution. When you heard from the nurses, I suppose you told Miss Broadbent ?—I told my father first. I suppose you did tell Miss Broadbent ?—I asked her if she had heard about it and she said she had. Did you tell Mr. Jolley ?—No, 1 did not. Plaintiff was cross-examined with a view to showing that prior to July 17th the doctor had found fault with her as to the condition of some clothing and for irregular attention to duty, but she strongly denied it. Had he made no complaint to you about your conduct outside the hospital in uniform ?—No.</p>
        <p>Had he said aiaything about your being in the park?— No.</p>
        <p>Is it a fact that you used to *go walks in the park ? Yes. I used to go sometimes.</p>
        <p>Do you mean to say the doctor never spoke of your being with young men in the park ?—He never spoke to me about it.</p>
        <p>Do you mean he spoke to someone else ?—I never heard of it if he did.</p>
        <p>Did he not say he did not think it was fitting conduct to be walking about in the park with young men whilst in uniform ?—He told me he did not think it was fitting conduct for some of the other nurses, not me. Pressed on the point, plaintiff gave the names of the nurses and adhered to the statement that the doctor had not complained of her conduct. He warned her not to go with another nurse because she was gay. Plaintiff had not talked with young men in the park, but she had seen one young man in Sankey-street. Plaintiff also denied that on the 16th July she filled in a report book stating the hours at which the patients' linen had bean changed—before she should have done, and that Dr. Flitcroft found fault with her about the entry. She subsequently heard that he mentioned the matter next morning to the superintendent nurse and other nurses. She maintained that she was not at fault in the matter.</p>
        <p>You told us that up to the evening of the 17th July he had behaved properly ?—lt was on the Friday morn- ing_that he said this.</p>
        <p>his Lordship.—You said the following morning.—It was the Friday morning. The alleged assaults, plaintiff further stated, took place on Wednesday and Thursday. Mr. Taylor.—Hadn't there been some little friction in the Workhouse ?—There was none while I was there. Do you know of none ?—I heard there had been some before I went there.</p>
        <p>Between the doctor and somebody ?—And the nurses. He is rather strict isn't he ?—Yes, Wants things done properly ?—Yes. Further cross-examined witness said she had not heard that the friction was on that account.</p>
        <p>What had you heard ?—That he had been talking to them in an improper way. , Had you heard of any unpleasantness between him and the Master ?—No.</p>
        <p>Perhaps he was a little too strict for the nurses ? Perhaps so. There was a little friction because he would have his own way? You heard that ?—Yes. Miss Broadbent is a friend of yours. Was she only a friend after the alleged assault ?—Yes. I never spoke much to her before.</p>
        <p>I think you have a portrait of her in your room ? It is not there now. I have taken it home. Miss Broadbent is the secretary of some Society isn't she ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>What is the name of it ?—The Brabazon Society. What is its object ?—The patients do fancy work and they have a bazaar to sell it in aid of some fund. His Lordship : What is the Brabazon Society or scheme? _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : I daresay Miss Broadbent will be able to tell your Lordship. His Lordship : I have heard of it, but I don't remember at the moment.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor (resuming cross-examination): Have you never heard there was some unpleasantness or friction between the doctor and Miss Broadbent about that ? No. I have never heard it.</p>
        <p>He did not like the patients doing that work, did he? —lt has been said during the last week or two that he stopped it. Because work was being sent out without being disin- fected ?—I don't know_ why be stopped it. _</p>
        <p>Had you never heard thEit he objected to the epileptic patients making these fancy things ?—No. I had nothing much to do with it. You say you have heard of nothing between the Workhouse Master and the doctor ?—No. Or between the superintendent and the doctor ?—No, not until this action. Have you heard that Miss Broadbent, to put it mildly, would not be averse to his leaving the Work- house? Apart from the question you told us about, do you know that Miss Broadbent would not be displeased if he were to leave ?—I don't suppose she would be dis- pleased if he would leave now, since this action happened. But I am talking of June or July.—No, I never heard of that. _ _</p>
        <p>No gossip about Miss Broadbent and the doctor ?—I never heard any gossip at all. Perhaps you never listen to any ?-11.ardly ever. (Laughter.) Plaintiff was then subjected to a searching cross- examination as to the first alleged assault. She admitted that she did not shout or attempt to call any- one. She was terrified and struggled and told him to go away. Why didn't you go upstairs and tell Nurse Clynch who was in charge there ?-4. didn't want to let everybody know. I was afraid of what he might do. •</p>
        <p>ilVh.at were you afraid of ?—For "one thing, of the lies he would tell about me if I told. Why didn't you run along the corridor to the nurses' home ?—I am not supposed to leave my ward. I was afraid of rousing my patients and I know I was able to resist him.</p>
        <p>So you preferred the struggle to calling for help? Yes.</p>
        <p>Further questioned plaintiff admitted that she after- wards let the doctor out by unlocking the door of the corridor of which she had the key and they parted friends. She did not think the matter was serious enough on the first occasion to inform the Superintend- ent. She thought she would be discharged and that they would take the doctor's word before her's. She did not think he would come again. She did not shout or give any alarm on the second occasion. Did he kiss you on any of these nights ?—Yes. Are you sure he did ?—Certain. How many times did he kiss you ?--E don't know. Subsequently plaintiff said he kissed her three times on the first occason and twice on the second. She did not complain to anyone on the second occa- sion, continued plaintiff, although she considered the matter was getting serious.. She thought she would tell i Superintendent Nurse Biggin fit occurred again. Did you expect him to assault you again ?—Yes,l did. He did not say so outright but he looked as if he was not fooling. Further questioned plaintiff said she believed two Local Government Board Inspectors visited the Work- house on July 18th, or 19th. She did not tell a soul about the matter on the 19th. Plaintiff was under cross-examination when the Court rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning Mr. Taylor resumed his cross- examination. - - - - - -</p>
        <p>Plaintiff said there was moonlight on the 18th July. The corridor and the doors had windows so that any- one passing could see whether there was a light inside. Dr Flitcroft did not tell her anything about changing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 23, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 23, 1907.</p>
        <p>the linen that night, although he did mention that the inspectors had been. He made this communication while the assault was going on?—Yes. The assault seems to have been interspersed with talk about the business of the hospital ?—Yes. I put it to you that he gave you instructions on the night of the 18th to put down the actual times of changing and that you were to change the linen when it was necessary ?—Y es. I suggest to you that on the 17th Dr. Flitcroft came straight into the store rood and sat down at the table? Yes.</p>
        <p>appointed ?—Yee, I felt very strongly about it. How long ago was he appointed ?—About a year. Up to the 26th July what terms were you on with Dr. Flitcroft ?—I was very friendly with him. I took a great interest in him. Was there any friction of any sort between you up to that time ?—Not any friction, but I told him he must be more punctual. Sometimes he came down very late in the morning. You visited the Workhouse constantly ?—Yes. Had you up to the 26th July the slighest wish in the world to get rid of him ?—No. The Society of which you are seeretary has garments made by pour people to give to poor people ?—No. The people in the hospital do fancy work to amuse them and keep them occupied. The patients at the Warrington hospital do a great deal. Before the 26th July did Dr. Flitcroft put any diffi- culties in your way in getting that work done ?--Not that I know of. I agreed with him that it would be much better that the consumptives should do as little as possible. He said it was quite safe so long as I hung the articles up in the sunshine. Dr. Flitcroft did not make any objection to you about it ?—No. He said he was friendly to it, and he helped me at the sale of work last nay. On the 26th July were you at a meeting of the Com- mittee at which Nurse Dinnis made a complaint about the doctor ?—Yes. Did you interest yourselPkn the matter as a Guardian? —I did.</p>
        <p>That you stood opposite him ?—Yes. That he sat there only two minutes ?—He sat long enough to read the report. That he then got up, walked out and went along the corridor and you let him out ?—No he did not. And that you switched off the light in the corridor? —No, he switched it off. His Lordship : Is the light generally on or off ? It is always supposed to be on. Mr. Taylor: On the 18th he came and sat down at the table as before ?—Fle came and read the report. I don't know whether he sat down or not. _ .</p>
        <p>Did you i;:i;i-Wn.-1.-h-e-Ol;Posite side of the table ? Yes. And did he then tell you about the inspector's visit and give you the instructions I have asked about ? No, he told me later on. Did he say the inspectors had complained about the bed sores the patients were suffering from and that that was bad nursing ?—No, he never mentioned any- thing. And that you then went down the corridor with him opened the door and let him out ?—No. Do you allege or not that after the assault on the 17th and 18th anything happened until the 23rd ?—No, nothing at all. You had no complaint to make as far as he was con- cerned ?—No. I thought perhaps it would not occur again and I had no need to make a complaint. I put it that on the 19th, 20th,21.5t and 22nd he came in the usual course and went to the store room and examined the book, or at any rate saw you on the busi- ness of the hospital?—He only came on the 20th. The charge nurse was with him. Questioned as to the events of the 23rd, plaintiff said there was a man dying that night and Father Crotty was sent for. Did the priest come before the assault or not ?—I can't remember. lam notsure. Which do you think it was, priest first and assault after or the reverse ?—I think the prie3t came first. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Were you asked by the Committee to make some inquiries into the matter ?—I was formally asked by the Committee to find out everything I could from nurses who had left the institution. _ _</p>
        <p>At a subsequent Committee meeting was Dr. Flitcroft represented by a solicitor ?—Yes. Did you advise Nurse Dinnis to have a solicitor ? The Doctor had been told by the Committee that he must clear himself by legal action, so I said she had better see a solictor in case he took action against her. Did you give her the name of Mr. Unsworth ?--I did. At the time you recommended her to go to Mr. Unsworth had you heard anything about the slander ? No. Nothing. After she had gone to Mr. Unsworth did you have anything more to do with conducting the action ?—No. In consequence of a communication from plaintiff's father, after the case was got ready for trial, did you lend his some money ?—Yes I borrowed £2O from the bank. I hadn't any myself. (Laughter.) With the exception of lending the father £23 to come into Court with, had you anything at all to do with finding the money or the means of bringing the action? —No. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Further questioned plaintiff said she believed the priest left about a quarter to twelve and the doctor came at ten minutes past. She did not report the affair to Superintendent Nurse Biggin that morning. Why didn't you go to Superintendent Biggin at once. i Here s the third thing on which you had made up your mind to act. Why didn't you go to the Superintendent and tell her ?—I didn't like to.</p>
        <p>-'Continuing her evidence, Niiss Broadbent said that at the first meeting of the Committee when the girl had made her statement Dr. Flitcroft, who was called into the room afterwards, said Nusse Dinnis was a bad girl and none of the other nurses would have anything to do with her. He did not suggest any motive for the charge. At a later meeting, she believed on the 16th August, he made a statement to the effect that the girl had made the charge to shield herself because she was in a certain condition. _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Do you mean the third assault mlde you doubt whether you should do it or not ?—I thought I had better give in ray resignation and go all at once before making a scene like this. Did you tell anyone in the hospital about the assaults before you spoke to the Superintendent ?—Yes, Nurse Carr. _ _</p>
        <p>At any of the meetings after the first charge was made did you say anything about him, either for or against ?—Against him.</p>
        <p>What did ion say ?—I said he was a cad. Did you say anything else ?—I said the Guardians ought to investigate the matter. I asked whether the Guardians would send for nurses who had made similar complaints. I was asked to get all the information I could from nurses who had left the institution as to the doctor's behaviour to them. _</p>
        <p>Do you mean you told her you were going to resign or that you made a complaint ?—I was crying in my room and she asked me what was the matter, and I told her part of what had happened. You did not speak to the Superintendant at all on your own account ?—No. Neither to resign not to complain ?—No. Plaintiff was questioned as to whether she was play- ing the piano and singing comic songs on the morning of the 25th. She denied that she sang comic songs. but said she might have been playing. She did not remember. She told Nurse Carr the doctor had assaulted her, and the nurse told Superintendent Biggin. At Nurse Carr's suggestion plaintiff then went and reported the matter to the Superintendent,who told her she could not help her, but she would take her before the committee in the afternoon. Nfiss Biggin did not take the statement down. Plaintiff gave the account of the affair to the Committee. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Miss Broadbent denied instructing Mr. Unsworth to see other Guardians. Do yoa remember some other Guardians objecting to your action in this matter ?—One of the Guardians objected that I hal told Mr. Unsworth anything about the matter.</p>
        <p>Was that Mr. Brooks ?—Yes, Mr. W. C. Brooks. Miss Broadbent said she did not remember that Father Ryan made a similar objection. She admitted having mentioned the names of three nurses to the Committee and said she heard it was rumoured that they had left the institution partly because of the doctor. You were on good terms with the doctor, until July 2Gth, and then you said he was a oad ?—Yes, after the statement of the girl but very largely from what he himself said at the committee meeting and the way he spoke of the nurses. Had he objected to this work being done by patients for the Brabazon scheme ?—Not that I knew of. He said at the sale of work " If you want more Brabazon work doing I will keep the patients in the hospital longer, to do it for yon." How very accommodating ! (Laughter,) When was it that you discovered him in the act of coming late ?—First I heard that one of the inmates had said she heard the doctor say when' he came very late " Nobody has seen me." I told him and he said "I will turn these old women off the landing." Did you find fault with him again ?-went one morning and found he was in bed at 10.30. (Laughter.) Did you go into his bedroom ? (Langhter.)—No. I asked to speak to him and they said he was in bed. I said "Go and tell him to ge up at once as I want to speak to him." Poor man ! (Laughter.) Who was going with this terrible message ?—One of the inmates—an old woman. That was too cruel ! (Laughter.) You sent this very old woman as your herald. I hope she was a mien old lady. (Laughter.)—She was. (Laughter.) You select good ones? (Laughter.)—She was the only one there. She had to knock at his door and tell him to get up because Miss Broadbent wanted to speak to him. _ _</p>
        <p>The man you charged was not there ?—No. Nor i fact was he ever allowed to ask you any ques- tions so far as you know before the Committee .—No. Nor was his solicitor who was present on oneoccasion and tried to get in ?—I know nothing about that. Who went before the Committee first Miss Biggin or yourself P—Miss Biggin. Were you before the Committee any other time except the 26th ?—Not on the same point. You have taken no proceedings about the assaults ? No. _</p>
        <p>Why not ?—Because I had no witnesses. Neither had he.</p>
        <p>--- -- Who advised you about that ?—My father. Did you suggest to lfr. Unsworth that proceedings should be taken about the assaults ?—Yes. NVhat did he say ?—I don't remember. Plaintiff admitted that she never asked Dr. Flitcroft about the slander. .</p>
        <p>Do you know that no letter was ever written to him about it before the writ was served ?—I know nothing about it. _ _</p>
        <p>Did you know when the writ was served ?—No. Did you know it was going to be served ?—Yes. Do you think it right to authorise a writ to be served upon him without giving any opportunity of asking him whether he had slandered you ?—We knew for a fact that he had slandered me. _ -</p>
        <p>Did you discuss with Miss Broadbent whether you should bring an action ,for the assaults or for slander? No.</p>
        <p>She has talked about it with you ?—No. She never mentioned it to me. - - -</p>
        <p>That would waken him up. (Loughter.) How often have you done that ?—I only did it once. I told him he must be more punctual as he was disorganizing the whole of the hospital. Sometimes he came down at twelve and went on duty when the nurses ought to be going to dinner. Did you spend a great deal of time there ?—A great deal.</p>
        <p>•.0 WV ...1%,• You have been to her house a considerable number of times ?—Since the assault. _</p>
        <p>Not before?—No. Never before. How is it you have been visiting the house since the assault ?—Because I was invited. Have you talked about the assault to her ?—She heard tin the committee. Did you talk about it to her at these visits ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>The poor man had not much chance of neglecting his duties ?—I think he managed to negJect them pretty well.</p>
        <p>And about the slanders 7—No, not about the slanders. Do you mean to say Miss Broadbent did not know you were going to bring an action 7—She did not know until the writ was served.</p>
        <p>I suppose you never went away for a holiday ?—YOS have just been to Constantinople. (Laughter). Oh, he would have a chance of staying in bed a bit longer. (Laughter). I hope you kept them in ordier there—No, I didn't interfere with the Sultan. (Laughter). I don't know why you should not—l was not there long enough. (Laughter). Have you made charges against the master to .fr. Brooks?—I have often talked to Nlr.W.C.Brooks. I have often been to see him about anything I thought was extravagant. That is what the Labour members are for. (Laughter). Have you made a serious charge against the master ? —Only extravagance. About his being drunk, I mean ?—I said I heard it, and it ought to be inquired into and when I told the master so he agreed with me, that it would be better for the matter to be cleared up. You told Mr. Brooks it ought to be inquired into ? yes. Did you tell him to go and ask some cabman ?—No. I went to ask the cabman. _</p>
        <p>Plaintiff said the doctor reported her on October 11th because some of the patients' heads were dirty. She did not deny that they were dirty. That was your fault I suppose ?—To a certain extent. Therefore he would be right to report it ?—Yes. Have you any complaint to make about it ?—Yes, I had no lotion to kill the vermin. Have you any complaint about the doctor making the report ?—I was not on duty when he came round to look at the heads.</p>
        <p>You admit it was your own fault ?—Yes. Is there, in your mind, any blame to be attached to him for making the report ?—No. His Lordship: If you had not got any lotion how was it your fault ?—I was in charge aud I suppose I would be to blame. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Plaintiff then denied the truth of the allegation that she had made improper suggestions to the old man, Hardy, an inmate, who was an ex-soldier. Hardy and Dr. Flitcroft were very great friends. They were very often together in the ward and she believed the doctor had put Hardy up to making the suggestions against her.</p>
        <p>Did you gat any information from—him ?—He said it was not true, and if it was he would not say so. (Laughter.) Are you liable to pay anything else to Mr. Unsworth? —No. "A CONTEMPTIBLE LOT OF GIRLS."</p>
        <p>Do you suggest the doctor was irregular in not going to the ward with the night charge nurse ?—He ought to be accompanied by the charge nurse' Re-examined by Mr. Swift plaintiff said Miss Broad- bent was a Guardian and a member of the Visiting Committee. She was present at the Committee meet- ing on July 26th when plaintiff made the charge against the doctor. . . .</p>
        <p>Nurse Carr' one of the probationers at the Infirmary, deposed to finding the plaintiff crying in her room, eliciting her account of Dr. Flitcroft's behaviour towards her, and communicating it to the superinten- dent nurse. About a week after this the doctor sent for witness and another nurse and told them, "I have come to tell you that whoever speaks disrespecfully of me, I will take proceedings. A more contemptible lot of girls I have never come across. For the least trivial thing I will take you before the Board, if you don't do your work accurately, and have you dismissed."</p>
        <p>Before you made the charge at that meeting had there been any friendship between you and Miss Broadbent ?—None whatever.</p>
        <p>Had she ever treated you any differently from any other nurses ?—No. I had very seldom spoken to her. Had you before July 26th ever heard a suggestion that there was any trouble between Miss Broadbent and the doctor ?—No. I believe they were very good friends.</p>
        <p>Further questioned plaintiff said she heard defendant was represented at one of the Committee meetings by a solicitor but she was not. She did not know that the Committee passed a resolution that she should be summarily dismissed. She had been advised from beginning to end by her father. As to the doctor reporting her, she had asked for lotion but did not get it. She was before the Committee but was not reprimanded. That was not the first case of dirty heads there had been while she had been at the infirmary but he had never reported her before. She denied being hysterical as had been suggested by the defence. Do you know any reason why you should not go into the Park in uniform ?—None at all. _ _</p>
        <p>Or why you should not talk to young men in the Park ?—No.</p>
        <p>_ But you in fact say you never have done ?—I have not.</p>
        <p>Further questioned : She asked the doctor about the statement she had heard that he had reported her about the entry in the book, and he said it was all lies. Long before the alleged slander she had heard there had been friction between the doctor and some of the nurses. Nurse Stitch had told her something about friction between the doctor and another nurse.</p>
        <p>What VMS it you heard from Nurse Stitch about this nurse ?—That he had tried to assault her in the infirm ward while she was on night duty. Plaintiff said she had no relations in Warrington and she did not knox anyone in the town except one young man.</p>
        <p>MISS BROADBENT'S FVTDENCE. AMUSING PASSAGES. Miss Const9.nce Broadbent, The lioHies, Latchford, was the next witness. She said that for three years she had been a member of the Board of Guardians and of the Visiting Committee. She had been connected with the Brabazon Society for twelve years—six years as secretary. His Lordship: Is that something established by Lord Meath ?—Lady Meath advances the money to start it. There are branches all over the country, and it is a very good thing for the people. Before Dr. Flitcroft was appointed, I believe there was no resident medical officer ?—No. I think it was your desire to get a resident officer</p>
        <p>For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. If 1, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>witness about the allocation of the nurses, and said that Nurse Dinnis ought to be moved from the male hospital because of her condition. Mr. Taylor: Are you quite sure he didn't say that one of the nurses had told him so?—Absolutely certain. BURGLARY AT WIDNES. Mr. Taylor: You have had disputes with the doctor, 1 ? At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, Frederick haven't you ?—Yes, we have had disputes, usually about Ford, the possessor of a police court record, was sent food. That is a very quarrelsome subject. (Laughter.) In to take his trial at the next Liverpool Quarter Sessions Jul 3 last you wanted to resign because you could not os, ,s remises on the 10th inst., on a charge of burglariously entering Mr. and stealing a • Samuel get on with the doctor ?—Yes, that is so. There was silveerr ton Geneva watch, value £l, and 6d. in money; so much unpleasantness I could not stand it any longer. , also with breaking open a Corporation slot meter and SOLICITOR'S EVIDENCE, taking therefrom 7s. in copper, the moneys of the Mr. C. H. ITnsworth, solicitor, gave a history of the ; Mayor, aldermen and burgesses of the borough. legal proceedings in the case. He did not interview or 1 WIFE WOUNDING AT WIDNES. write to the defendant before issuing the writ because I he thought it would be useless. He had instructions I At Liverpool Assizes on Monday, Michael Qainn (55 ) from the plaintiff's father to clear her character at all labourer, pleaded guilty to having unlawfully wounded costs. Miss Broadbent had not said she would find the his wife at Widnes on the sth of September. Mr. Riley costs of the action. 'prosecuted, and Mr. Madden defended. On the sth of Mr. Taylor: Have you reason to think she will ? September prisoner, who had been out of work for some I rather fancy she would if I could not get it in any time, left the house to draw his club money, when his other way. She is certainly under no obligation, wife remarked "You had better spend it." He Hasn't she given you some reason for thinking that it returned home in an hour and struck his wife with a is possible ?—No. lam relying upon what I know of her hatchet. The prosecutrix was said to be reluctant to character. jgive evidence against her husband.—His lordship,taking into consideration the fact that prisoner had already been in prison for two months, sent him to gaol for twenty-one days. THE CUPEL EXPLOSION AT WIDNES. CAUSE OF THE ACCIDENT. Mr. S Brighouse (coroner) held an inquest at Widnes on Monday afternoon, touching the death of Martin Cain, aged thirty-three years, G 9, Nelson-street, who died at the Widnes Accident Hospltal,from Severe burns caused through an explosion of a cupel containing molten metal, at Messrs. Bolton's copper works, on Thursday night.—The Coroner inquired if Mr. Skinner H.M. Inspector, had been notified.—The police had done so, but the Inspector had not visited the works.— Only evidence of identification was taken, the Coroner expressing his intention of postponing the inquest until Woods, another injured man, was able to attend.—Mr. Glover, who appeared for the works proprietors, explained that it was the duty of Cain to regulate the flow of water. If this was neglected the pipes burnt and burst, and this was the cause assigned for the accident.—The inquest was adjourned until the 3rd prox., at twenty-five minutes past ten o'clock. GENTLUNIAN AND 1113 DOJCOR. At Runcorn. on S iturday, D. Carruthers, of Halton sued George Jepseu Knight, of 58, Park-road, South- port, gentleman, for £53 Us. Gd. balance of an account for professional services. Mr. Knight was formerly a well-known accountant in Runcorn, but retired some years ago. In 1830 Mr. Knight had a very serious illness. Dr. Ca,rruthers attended him night and day, and had to engage a specialist. Defendana had made two paymetits of £lO, the last being in 1897. Mr. Knight now pleaded tho statute of limitation, which Mr. A. Browne, plaintiff's solicitor, characterised as dishonourable and ill-requitted conduct after all Dr. Carruthers' kindness and attention.—His Honour said he had no alternative but to give judgment for the defendant. WIDNES SEXTON'S REMARKABLE RECORD. Mr. John Palmer, who has been associated for fifty years with the ancient Parish C"iarch of Faraworth, Widnes, as sexton and parish clerk, has just been pre,. sented with £75 and an illuminated address by the members of the congregation. Still hale and hearty, the veteran succeeded, half a century aeo, an eccentric worthy named Parr, who held the office for a long period. Mr. Palmer is a dignified official of the old- fashioned type, and he is a veritable link with the days that are past. Many past vicars and curates assembled at the presentation to express -their appreciation of his services. and Canon Wright Williams said it was true of the parish that " Vicars may come and vicars may go, But Palmer stays for ever." During his fifty years' term of °Mee Mr. Palmer has officiated at 6,398 baptisms, 1,385 marriages, and 6,761 funerals.</p>
        <p>THE DEFENCE, A CONIPLErE DENIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. raylor submitted that there was no evidence in respect of the alleged slander uttered to the Workhouse master. If, however, his Lordship decided that the question must go to the jury, he submitted that the occasion was privileged. His Lordship said he would take the opinion of the jury as to whether,assaming the occasion was privileged, there was malice on the part of the defendant. He ! would probably ask them if they found for the plaintiff to separate the damages as to the different incidents. Addressing the jury Mr. Taylor said it was a very responsible position he had to take on behalf of the defendant and the case was by no means an ordinary case. Although technically the question of slander was the issue which the jury had to settle, it shrank into nothing as compared with the other result of an adverse finding—which would be to find that defendant criminally assaulted the girl on the 17th, 18th and 23rd July. That would mean absolute rain to a professional man. If he was guilty no language would be too strong in which to characterise the man and therefore one was not putting it too high in stating that defendant's professional life was absolutely at stake. With regard to the slander defendant totally denied having said any of tha words imputed to him by the Superintendent Nurse and the Assistant Master, and he merely told the master that there was a rumour. The latter staternent,he submitted, was privileged and uttered without malice. It was not suggested that there was really a conspiracy that these people had joined together in an organized band to bring an action against the defendant and ruin him,but it was suggested that there was a certain feeling of hostility against the man. The attitude of the Committee in immediately mina,' upon the doctor to clear his char- acter was not a fair one. The facti of insinuations having been made about other nurses supplied the source from which these people got the colour and tone of mind which lei them to think it was very likely there might be something in the charge of assault. He suggested that Miss Broadbent was biased against the defendant and was prepared to get him out of the hospital. Nothing of the sort that she alleged was said by the defendant before the Committee and members of that body would be called to state that the words were never uttered. The vital part of the case den3nied upon the alleged assaults and he submitted that the girl's story needed the gravest scrutiny beiore such an improbable story could be accepted. The story bora on its face the marks of improbability of an extreme kind. Did they think a virtuous girl, after such scenes had been gone through to her horror, would not have gone at once and complained to the matron or superintendent? The defence was that the assaults never were committed,but the plaintiff, having been forced by subsequent events to tell some story, invented these things.</p>
        <p>WIDNES TRAGEDY. WII,FUL \KHMER VERDICT AT INQUEST.</p>
        <p>i The adjourned Coroner's inquest on the body of Rose 1 Ann Byrne, of 18, Brown-street, Widnes, which was hold lin the local Police Court on Tuesday, .has left the ! circumstances surrounding her death enshrouded in ; deeper mystery than ever.—The body was discovered on INovember 3rd lying on the pavement directly under an open bedroom window of her husband's house, and it wits at first surmised that she had fallen over or thrown Ik herself from the window. Later, on account of infor- t "nation vouchsafed by neighbours, her husband was I arrested and charged -with her death. He has been in ; custody ever since, and was present at Tuesday's pro- ceedings.—The iury,however, after hearing the evidence, returned a verdict of" Wilful murder," but said there was not sufficient evidence to -connect prisoner with that finding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>John Frederick Avvbery, the workhouse master, said that on July 27th,the defendant came into his office and said," This is a nice girl to make allegations against my charioter," adding that he was given to understand or he had been told that she was enceinte.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Taylor, witness agreed that there had been a want of harmony between the doctor and himself. They had differed slightly as to the necessity of reporting some "case to the coroner, and witness had some correspondence with the coroner on the subject. His Lordship: Is this one of the matters we have been reading ahout in the papers lately ? Witness: It was a subsequent case your lordship read about.</p>
        <p>His Lordship: It was about the Chief Constable of Warrington. ' Mr. Swift: The case in which the uhief Constable of NVarrington and the Coroner wrote letters to each other—(laughter)—has been disposed of at these Assizes in the other Court. I prosecuted, or rather, ray lord, I did not prosecute. (Laughter.) His Lordship: I merely saw there been a discussion in Warrington. I didn't know there had been a case. Mr. Awbery admitted writing a letter to the defend- ant in which ho said" I don't need to be taught any- thing by you regarding reports to the Coroner. If any similar death occurs again I shall report it again. Re yor.r requests for samples and prices, I get nothing unless instructed by the Guardians to do so." Cross-examined further as to the statement made by Dr. Flitcroft, the master said the defendant had never actually said that the plaintiff was enceinte, but merely that he was given to understand. Witness had the im- pression that this statement and tho letter had been gleaned from one of the nurses. ASSISTANT MASTER'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Herbert U. Todd, the assistant master, said that two or three days after the case was first brought before the committee, the medical officer had asked him what his opinion was. Witness replied, "I don't know what to think," whereupon the doctor remarked that she was in a certain condition, and added," She is a bad woman. I have a letter in my pocket which will prove what she is."</p>
        <p>SUPERINTENDENT NURSE CALLED. Mary Biggin, the superintendent nurse, said tha defendant had told her that he did not think it was fair that she should have taken Nurse Dinnis's com- plaint to the Committee before giving him a chance of hearing what the allegation wae. The day after the subject had been before the Committee he spoke to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DEFENDANT'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>DEFENDANT'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Thomas E. Flitcroft, the defendant, was called and said that at the time of the alleged assaults there was no rule about the doctor being accompanied by a charge nurse on his night rounds. He had to exercise his own judgment. Nurses Stitch and Downing left the institution because of an alteration of a rate and Narse Cox said she left on account of family reasons. Before July 26th he thought there was a little too much inter- ference by Miss Broadbent in the hospital. He also disagreed with her because she allowed epileptic inmates to do work, using sharp instruments, and because con- sumptive patients were allowed to do work which was sent out without being disinfected. He considered that dangerous. He, however, could not stop it and the practice was still allowed. On the 17th July on going to the ground floor ward he was met by Nurse Dinnis who went with him into the stores, He sat down at a table and looked at the report book. After making some general remarks as to whether everything was all right he left her. He then visited another ward and was afterwards let out by the corridor door by Nurse Dinnis and bade her "good night." He did not assault her, kiss her, or make any propositions to her. He would never dream of doing such a thing. - Nothing improper occurred on the 18th. He weat on his rounds each night up to the 22nd July and saw the plaintiff in the usual course. On the 23rd he concluded his round about moo sue Father Crotty came to his room about 12 o'clock and left at 12.20 or 12.30. He emphatically denied having assaulted plaintiff. The first he heard of the assaults was at a committee meeting on July 26th. He believed it was Mr. Jolley who read out a statement to the effect that he had assaulted Nurse Dinnis on July 17th, 13th and 23rd,that he had asked her to go away for a week-end and offered to make her a senior. Defendant was so frightfully annoyed that he said " It's a damned lie." His request to be confronted with the girl was not acceded to and he told the committee he had been badly treated and walked out. He told Mr. Awbery that anoth-ir nurse hal told him there was a rumour in the place that Nurse Dinnis was in a certain condition. Mr. Awbery said it ought to go before the committee and defendakt agreed. He told Mr. Ai-vbery that he considered it was part of his duty as master to know. He had heard the rumour from Nurse Gorse. Defendant denied uttering the words attributed to him by Superintendent Nurse Biggin or to the Assistant Master. On August 2nd, after a committee meeting Mr. Jolley told him the committee, on the advice of the clerk, had decided that the doctor would have to clear his c'naracrer. As to how, Mr. Jolle said rested with defen.lent. He said " Have Ito put the girl into court ?" Mr. Jolley said " That's what it amounts to." Defendant denied uttering the alleged slanderous statement before the committee. Defendant was under cross-examinetion when the hearing was aijourned until to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>JOHN Moon.FlELD.—Your letter raises questions which, we think, would be better discussed and settled in the Trades and Labour Cauncil than in the columns of the" Examiner."—En. " E x."</p>
        <p>Good Templary at Burtonwood. Sir,—Perhaps your Temperance readers will be pleased to hear of a unique service held last Sunday in the Church Hall, Burtonwood, the austensible object of which was the preparing of the ground for opening a Good Templars lodge in the village. Contingents of Good Templars collected from the surrounding towns and villages and formed a procession headed by the Earlestown Salvation Army band. After parading through the village they returned to the Church Hall to hear a sermon by the Rev. G. H. Lunn, a Church of England Clergyman and a P.D.C. of the Order, supported by the Vicar. The most interesting point about the matter was the fact that the audience consisted largely of Dissenters of various denominations that the instrutmental music was provided by the Salvation Army baud and that the preacher wore his Good Templars regalia and all these joined heart and soul in a Church of England service. Last but not least I may commend the hearty greeting given by the Vicar of Burtonwood to the Salvationists and in conclusion I may say that gatherings like these truly demonstrate the brotherhood of Christianity and the rallying power of Temperance.—l remain yours ttc.</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>To Fight the Trade.</head>
        <p>To Fight the Trade.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am quite sure my Temperance friends will not mis-interpret my feelings when I say (were it not for my regret for a personal friend who was defeated at the late :Nlunicipal Elections), I could almost be glad at the results, as it seems to have aroused an awakening of many of our friends who were apparently dormant. And thanks are due to the" Examiner" for the lead it is giving. I have read the various letters on the subject with pleasure and interest. But why a new Temperance Electoral Association ? Wtiat is the matter with the " Warrington Temperance Federation ? " The last ' meeting of its Committee, which I think I attended, was adjourned, to be called together when necessity arose. The Federation is under the presidency of the Rev. J. F. Matthews, reconstructed and brought up to date as the result of a paper read by him at a Temper- ance Conference. Here is the machinery at hand, and it has undoubtedly done good work in the past. It has beeu broad enough in the past to include names like that of tha Rector of Warrington, the Revs. Davies, Powell and Byrne of the Church of England, Revs. John Yonge, J. F. Matthews, Dunnico and others of the Free Churah, whilst the Roman Catholic Church was for sometime worthily represented by Mr. Dalton working as a co-secretary with Mr. William Roberts. It will thus be seen it was neither sectarian nor political'in its construction and did good work at a time when it was evidently more difficult, and temperance principles no quite so popular in the town. Why not call this Association together, again. The name of your Association will not win elections. It is the material constituting the Association that tells, and the influence they can bring to bear. There are many of us who have done our best in the part will gladly stand on one side, or follow in the ranks, to those who may be younger more fervent or enthusiastic in the work, and may thus do better in the future. My gratitude is to those who paved the way before me. My hope in those who are corning after us to successfully achieve what we have failed to perform. I am, yours very truly, PATRICK O'BRIEN,</p>
        <p>125, Wilderspool-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>POLICE COURT PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Immediately after the close of the Coroner's inquiry Alderman W. W. GOes:tge attenied the Police Cour t</p>
        <p>gr. Knowles prosecuted on behalf the police, and only called sulicient evidence .to justify a remand for another week.</p>
        <p>A remand for another week was granted. A lame crowd of townspeople gathered around the court expecting to c act, b-.. —.- disappointed. The gruesome production of a portion of :qrs. Byrne's skull by Dr. Eustace, who conducted tho post-mortem examination' proved the main factor in dispelling the theory of a fall from the window. There was ample evidence hero of a huge triangular fractnre, which could only have been done by a severe blow. The crack in the skull extended for two inches above the left orbit, and in a jagged downward course to below the nose. Great care had beau taken by the doctor to arrive at his conclusion, and a plaster cast was also instrumental in enlightening the jury on the point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>"THE GARDEN OF EDEN" AT ST. HELENS. At a mAeting of StHeleos Park Committee, on Wednes- day, Mr.Pagh complained of the way that children were treated in Victoria Park. He said they were not allowed to go near the fountain, and he wanted to know if the caretaker had instructions not to allow this, and how they arrived at the decision to which children should be allowed and which should not. Was it on account of age or dress? it seemed to him that the caretakers received instructions from someone else besides the committee on this matter. They stood about with big stick., as though they were standing outside and protecting the Garden of Eden.—Mr. J. Phythian said there was a very strong feeling about this matter in the town that the men should not go about with big sticks, and that the borough engineer should make inquiries and see what could be done. The Chairman said that some people were too thin-skinned, and complained very readily. Matters like that must be left to the caretakers to use their discretion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BOUGHT ON HIRE.</head>
        <p>BOUGHT ON HIRE.</p>
        <p>PIANO-MAKERS INSTITUTE A ;NEW SYSTEM The hire purchase system has been a fruitful source of sarcasm on the part of County Court judges, " gags " from pantomime comedians and whole sermons of advice from people whose notions of the system are rather hazy. "Respectable firms with a name at stake deal fairly enough With the hire purchase customers." said Mr. Henry Nfeyer, the genial manager of Messrs. Waddington and Sons,the.old-astablished English piano makers, whose liandsoale Premises in the Central Half Buildings, Renshaw-street, Liverpool, gave evidence of much prosperity when our representative called last week. " Speaking for onrselves," he continued, "we have never pursued the custom or wanting the customers moneyand the instrument. Our new system of surrender values is evidence of our desire to treat well those of our customers who have fallen on hard times. Under the system adopted by any other provincial firm the hirer, or thp would-be buyer of the instrument may hay paid £36 out of £4O and still have the piano or organ taken from him through making defatilt with but one pay- ment. But under the Waddington system the hirer has at least 25 par cent. or ss. in the pound returned to him in cash should he wish to return the instrument. We prefer the hirer to find someone to whom he can transfer his rights under the agreement. This generally pays him even better than receiving the amount of the surrender money we refund. In certain cases we return even more than ss. in the £ of what has been paid to us, but that sum is the least we refund, even if the hirer has only been Paying 10s. per month. It is by such honourable methods of business that we have built up the largest trade of its sort in the pro- vinces. Paying advertising bills and canvassers com- missions does not appeal to us. The fact of our being the only piano makers in the north selling direct to the public, becoming known to the public, has, of course, greatly helped us to extend our trade iu Lancashire. We make the bast piano that 69 years experience, well-paid British labour, seasoned timber, unlimited capital at ceaseless study of our art can assist in producing. Our factory in York has been enlarged three times within five years or so,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>STEKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STEKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY iCHOOL TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.—The half-yearly gathering of the Sunday School Teachers' Association connected with the Rural Deanery of Frodshaiu was held at Stockton Heath on Saturday afternoon. There was an attendance of over 200 teachers and clergy in St. Thomas' Church Room, pre- sided over by Canon Blogg, Rural Dean.—The Rev. E. W. Evans (Vicar of Helshy) was re-appointed secretary, and Mr. John Littler treasurer, the latter gentleman presenting a most satisfactory balance sheet. The committee was constituted as follows : Revs.J. Collins (Stockon Heath), W. Scholes (Runcorn), and G. E. Briggs (Christ Church, Latchford), and Messrs. T. Clam (Runcorn), Banks (Latchford), and Burrows (Frodshatn).—The Rev. J. H. Thorpe, of Stockport, addressed the gathering on the spiritual advantages of Sunday school teaching to the teachers. gZi</p>
      </div>
