      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>STREET GAMBLING ON SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>1 John Hennigan, Henry Trollop, Henry Johnson I Joseph Keenan, Martin McDonald were summoned at Newton Sessions for gaming with cards at the top of Clarence-street, Earlestown, on Sunday afternoon, October 27th.—M1 except Trollop are youths.—P.C. Simpson he and P.C. Hunt watched them playing " banker" for more than half an hour, and when they showed themselves all the defendants ran away. Trollop was the only one caught, but he knew them all. —llennrgan and Keenan, having been previously con- victed, were fined 10s. each and costs; the others is. each, and "more next time," said the Chairman.— Mark O'Brien and John O'Brien were summoned for a similar offence about the same time, near Havannah Colliery.—Pined Is. each and costs. CONGO ATROCITIES.</p>
        <p>Under the ansyices of the local Free Church Council a public protest meeting was held in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Earlestown, last Thursday even- ing, when Mrs. J. H. Harris (returned Baptist Missionary from the Congo), gave a lengthy address on the barbarities curried on in the Congo Free State, many of which were too horrible to speak about. Views were then shown from photographs taken on the spot by missionaries. The methods used in forcing the natives to get rubber without pay or reward included the shooting down of men, women,and children, cutting off hands, arms, and feet, and other terrible offences.— The Rev. A. Harvey moved that a protest be forwarded to his Majesty's Government against the continuation of these outrages, and to secure just government and freedom for the native races of the Congo.—The Rev Simon C. Hall seconded, and gave his own personal. experiences of the West Coast of Africa. He denounced King Leopold in no measured terms, and said he would bring forward the subject at every meeting he attended, wherever possible.—A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. S. Harvey for presiding, and to Mrs. Harris for her address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ASSAULT ON A WORKMATE.</head>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A WORKMATE.</p>
        <p>Before Colonel Wood (chairman), Messrs. W. Coiling- wood, and E. T. Evans at Newton Sessions last Thurs- day. David Barcroft, of Sankey-street, Earlestown, was summoned by James Hatton,Earlestown,for assault- ing him on November Ist, in the Viaduct Works Earles- town.—Mr. Knowles prosecuted, and said plaintiff was a wheelwright at the London and North-Western Waggon Works. He and others received instructions to move a waggon,which they proceeded to do by means of bolsters —things which in other trades were called wooden horses. While they were doing it, Barcroft came up and with foul language found fault with what they were doing. Complainant was standing with his legs between some of the bolsters, so that he could not move. Some of the men expostulated with Barcroft for his interfer- ence and bad language, and plaintiff pat his hand out and said "Do get out of the way !" Barcroft then struck him several blows in the face, badly injuring him. He was a much older man than Barcroft and, standing as he was, was unable to move out of the way. It was a most violent and unpro- voked assault, and the plaintiff suffered very much, and had not yet recovered the use of his right eye—Plaintiff corroborated and added : lam 59 years of age. I have never been in a Court before. I have not recovered from the injury to my eye yet.He is a young man ; I don't know whether he is under 30.—John George Mullins, the Chairman o? the Earlestown Co-operative Society, and a waggon builder in the employ of the L.&amp; N.-W. Railway Co. since 1876, supported plaintiff's evidence.l was working with Hatton as described. Barcroft came up and used the language complained of. While he stood on a bolster that we had moved, Hatton pat his had against him and asked him to get away. It was the action of a man who wanted to get on with his work. He used no violence. Mr. Kelly for the derence said the defendant admitted having hit the plaintiff, and regretted it. There were discrepancies in the description of the push given by Hatton to Barcroft, and he (Mr. Kelly) suggested that if it was merely a push it was a very violent one; in fact it was a blow, and upon that the defendant did what he much regretted since. He struck Hatton, and for that offence he had already been tried by the works officials and dismissed from his employment, and did not know where he would get work again.—The Bench considered the assault a serious one, and fined defendant .23 including costs. A CYCLIST RUN DOWN.</p>
        <p>At the Newton Petty Sessions last Thursday, Joseph Bullock was summoned for furious driving at Winwick on October 24.—Mr. Knowles, prosecuting, said a I gentleman named Vaughan, living at Didsbury, was riding a bicycle and was knocked down by the defendant driving a light fish cart at a great pace. Mr. Vaughan was thrown off his bicycle and considerably hurt. The defendant was under the influence of drink. It was a good wide road, and the gentleman on the bicycle was only about 2 feet from the kerb when he was knocked over.—Walter Henry Vaughan, solicitor, of Didsburv, deposed that he rode a bicycle, and belonged to the Cycle Touring Club. He was that day riding along the Winvvick-road, and was on his proper side, only two feet from the kerb. He heard a vehicle coming along behind him with a great noise. He rang his bell, and as the defendant drove up to him, called out to warn him, AGI,« "- bicycle and threw him forward on his face. He went to the Swan at Winwick, washed the blood from his face, and rested. His nose bled very much, and he did not recover from the shock for some days.- -John Lewis, carter, of Warrington, said it seemed to him that defendant was under the influence of beer and not fit to drive. The cart wheel struck the bicycle wheel and threw the rider on the path, Defendant did not try to pull up, he was plucking at the reins and so urging the horse on.— Mr. White, said the case for the defence was that the cart wheel did not cause the accident, but Mr. Vaughan looked round, swerved, and so caused himself to fall. Had the cart struck the bicycle, going at the rate alleged, the bicycle must have been damaged, which it was not.—Defendant was sworn, and said he was not driving more than eight miles an hour. When approaching Mr. Vaughan, he (defendant) and his mates shouted to him, asking if he wanted all the road, Mr. Vaughan then lost his nerve, swerved, and his pedal caught the berbstone, That was what upset him.—Frank Coggins, butcher of St. Helens, one of the men in the cart, said the cart wheel did not touch the bicycle.—The Bench found that defendant was driving furiously, and to the danger of the public. He was tined 40s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TREE PLANTING</head>
        <p>TREE PLANTING</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Stockton Heath Committee. Trees iYilf ally Damaged.</p>
        <p>There is still considerable interest in the tree planting movement in Stockton Heath and a satisfactory year's work was under review at the annual meeting of the Tree Planting Committee held at the Hall CatV on Tuesday evening. Mr. C. Plinston, who presided, said unfortunately the spirit of hostility had not quite died out as evidenced by the wilful destruction by some individuals of some of the trees which had been planted at the desire of and the expense of the committee. A coincidence was that the great proportion of the losses had been again in Walton and Whitedeld roads. The cause of their deficit had been the acceptance by the committee of an offer which they thought it unwise to refuse for shrubs and young trees to be planted on the Ship Canal banks. The preparing of the beds and the planting of the trees and shrubs had caused them to expend rather more than they might otherwise have done last season, but in the course of a short space of time he thought events would show that the committee were warranted in accepting the offer. They had now under their charge nearly 1,000 trees besides several hundred shrubs, evergreels and creepers but, although that large amount of work had beau done there was a considerable amount still to be done. LBut they would rise to the occasion, and ,before long from the Cantilever Bridge to the Stag Bridge there would be a scene unrivalled in the vicinity of Warrington (Applause.) Toe Hon. Secretary (Mr. A. Fawcett), read the annual report which was adopted on the motion of Mr. Grant seconded by Mr. Ray. Mr. A. Fawcett submitted the statement of account which showed that the expenditure amounted to £llO 19s. 4d., and the receipts £BB 14s. 4d., leaving a deficit of £22 ss. Re had received a further subscription of £5 from Lady Greenall. He added that he had received a letter from Mrs. Lyon, in which she said that Mr. Lyon would be very glad to let the society have what trees they wanted. A subscription of £2 had also been sent by Mrs. Lyon. (Applause). Mr. Bindloss had also subscribed kI, and the other members of the committee would endeavour to raise amongst them a further sum of £lO. They would, therefore, see that there was a sum of .£lB practically raised towards the ' £22 ss. their present deficit. (Applause). Mr. Hickman proposed and Mr. Bindloss seconded the adoption of the balance sheet, which was carried unanimously. The members of the committee were re-elected, with the exception that Messrs. Grant, McGregor, and T. Baxter were appointed in the place of Messrs Tandy, Edden, and A. Broady. Mr. C. Plinston was unanimously re-elected treasurer, and Mr. A. Fawcett hon. secretary. Some discussion took. place as to the selection of the tree-planting day, and it was decided, on the motion of Mr. Woodcock, seconded by Mr. Bindloss' that it be held sometime during Pebruary,the actual date to be left with the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Woodcock suggested that two or three trees might be planted near the Police Station, and that the dead trees should be replaced. Mr. Rickman thought they ought to replace the dead trees, but he would most certainly oppose the planting of trees in new streets, because he did nt.t think . the inhabitants of the village had shown their enthusiasm by their presence that night to warrant the committee spending money in that direction. ' The Secretary explained that an estimate had been received for 40 trees to replace those which required to be replaced, and it amounted to £ll 10s.</p>
      </div>
