      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE. THE LOVELY-LANE SCHEME DISCUSSED.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE. THE LOVELY-LANE SCHEME DISCUSSED.</p>
        <p>r 41; .</p>
        <p>4 4;</p>
        <p>Ouestion of Price. The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening unJer the presidency of Councillor J. Fulton- Smith. There were also present :—The Mayor,Aldermen Bennett. Evans, Ellison, Tinnion ; Councillors Barton, Ball, Dickenson Evans, Dr. Joseph, Lord, Nield, Pemberton, W. B. Roberts, Sankey, Toole, and Wilkin- son : Revs. C mon Stevenson and N. Wilson, Miss Williams, Messrs. C. Sawkins Burgess, T. P. Bradshaw, R. T. Vairclough, A. .1. Jolley,C. E. Parker, E. Plinston, J. Upson, G. Walker; J. Nfoore Murray (director), R. B. Roberts (assistant director), John Fairhurst (treasurer). The CHAIRMAN said he had much pleasure in wel- coming Councillor Roberts among them, and he was sure he would prove a very useful acquisition to their ranks. (Her, hear.) He wished him every success as a member of the Committee. • THE FUTURE OF FAIRFIELD SCHOOL. A deputation from the managers of the Fairfield School having met the School Management Sub-com- mittee it was resolved that, subject to the mistress-in- charge agreeing to the arrangement, the conditions as they at present exist be continued until the end of the current educational school year (31st July. 1908), and that the subject he hraught up again before then with a view of deciding what shall be done in the matter after that time. Alderman BEYSETT. on behalf the Fairfield School managers, now expressed thanks for the way they had met tham. They felt that the existence of the two schools met the wants of a Three number of the inhabi- tants, end they honed when the matter came forward again, the state of things would be such as to warrant the permanent continuance of the present arrangements. NEW NITSTR.FSS AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. On the re,ornmerviatinn of the sub-committee appointed to censjder the applications received for the vacant post of assistant mistress at the Secondary School and Technical Institute, it was resolved that Miss Emily Mary Can vin. 8.A.. he appointed assistant mistress at the Secondary Sehool and Technical Institute, at a commencing salary at the rate of £llO per annum her engagement to be determinable by three month's notice. THE TRAINING OF TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S INDEPENDENT ATTITUDE. It Was noted that tho following resolutions bad been passed et the Conference of Representatives of Lanca- shire Education Authorities held at Preston on the 14th Octoher, with reference to the system of bursars and student teachers: (a) That the maintenance allow- ance to bursars he £lO per annum where necessary (b) that it is desirable that student teachers should serve one year in the ele—entary school heft) e -entering a training college: (c) that the Board of Education be recrested to withdraw Regulation 40 of the regulations of the preliminary education of elementary school teachers which v..tricts the Milnloyment of the student teachnr in no elementary sehool to not more than eight meetings per week; and (.1) that the total payments made in the cortrse of two years to a bursar and stivient teacher should not exceed the amount pre- vionsly paid hy an authority to a pupil teacher in the course of a two years' apprenticeship, whether the stnrient teacher al tends the elementary school eight or any other number of times per week." Canon STEVENSON, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Sehool Management Sub-Committee,said that at prPsent there were three alternative courses for pupil teachers. Some authorities were taking one and (Abets another,so that they were losing any unanimity of plan ihroughont the count ry.lle could not help thinking That thc, Buarl of F,ducation would have been wiser to hare thoneht out what was the best course, and laid that down for rational adoption. To the Warrington Committee it bad seemed wiser te work upon the old system and retain ti e two years' pupil teachersbip. That vas probably more economical, while it seemed likely a pupil teacher would zeta better grasp of the -working of a school than a bursar. In a Year or two they would he able to judge which was the hest system The feeling of the School Management Sub-Committee was that it would not he judicious to take a hasty step before they knew the bursary plan would he successful. That ens ant the view of the majority of education authorities—in fact, it seemeel as if Warrington was going to t e in the position of England to the rest of the world. England was the only Free Trade country and Warrinetcn seemed to be the only pupil teacher education authority, and they were going to stick out against the rest. (Laughter.) 'Mat, did not matter a bit if they were right. (Laughter.) There was an element of uncertainty about the whole future which made the position very difficult. Father WILSON seconded the adoption of the minutes. Me. ,ToLtsv said he was pleased the sub•committee bad stuck to their guns in this matter, although War- ;ton was in the minority. They were prepared to welcome changes which would boreal rerorium, out. Were was no necessity to change for the sake of making a 'change. He did not suggest that the Board of Educa. tier, were altogether unwise in allowing a considerable amount of discretion on the part of local authorities, as many reforms had been initiated in tbat way. The minutes were adopted. • THE LOVELY LANE SCHZIVIE. PROPOSED SCHOOL AND RECREATION GROUND.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor having reported as to the amount of land which will be needed for the purposes of a public elernent,ry school, it was resolved by the Sites and Buil:lines Committee (s) that, subject to the approval of the Board of Education, not less than three acres of land in the Lovely-lane site be purchased for the purpose of erecting thereon a public elementary school; and (b) it the Town Council be recommended to purchase, con-liti :many upon the approval of the Board of Education being received, the site in Lovely-lane containing seven acres, for the joint purposes of a public ele‘ineet.ry school and a public playground. Mr. It. T. FAIRCLOUGH, in proposing the adoption of the Tninutes, said the sub-conorntttee thought they ought -to have some definite expression of opinion from the committee with regard to the amount of land they should look for in purchasing a site for a school. He thought the ideal school was the one where they could have a howling green or lawn in front,and a play ground -at the back which ought to be utilized for the benefit of the children after school hours as well as during school hours. (Hear, hear.) At the present time they saw boys kicking footballs about the streets and on waste land. lie thought the sub-committee ought to be instructed -vrh-n looking for sites to purchase land that would not only he adaptable for schools but also as open spaces. 'The Sites and Buildings Sub-committee had met the Open Spaces Sub-committee, and they perfectly agreed with this view, but he (Mr. Fairclough) would like to know the opinion of the whole committee. Mr. WALKER seconded the adoption of the minutes. Alderman BEENETT cordially agreed with the recom- mendation. They were working on sound lines in trying -to cow&amp; the two ideas of the school and an open apace in proximity, lie thought the Lovely-lane site on its merits was a good one, but, although he believed there -was no real risil,he would prefer a site more remote from the Infections Diseases Hospital. The medical authori- ties, he supposed, were satisfied about that, and the sub- committee were satisfied too theft, there was no risk. The general policy of building the schools in the immediate neighbourhood of a suitable onen space tended to incr )ase the health of the school and the agreeableness of the surroundings to the general public who used the open space. He hoped in any future school they built they would try to provide bath accommodation. Alderman ELLISON also expressed his approval of the 'idea suggested by Mr, Fairclough. Councillor PEMBERTON said be agreed too. They out.Tht to have all the open spaces they could. But he did not agree with the price they were going to give for the laud. There was not a word about the price in tlya resolution. One of the things he objected to in public life was that every now and again they were asked to vote blind-folded. There were not many men in busi- mesa or private life who would give any price asked. clairne 1 to know something about the value of this land and the price was exorbitant. Anybody could go and lease a piece of land for two cottages there at the same rate that the committee were giving for seven acres. He did not think that was fair, because the , landowner thero owned many hundreds of acres besides. The laying-nut of a public space was bound to be an attraction, end the landowner was bound to let more of his land. If they voted for the resolution that night they had no other opportunity of saying anying about it. Whatever price was given they were committing themselves to it by that resolution. The CHAIRMAN said Councillor Pemberton was anticipating. The resolution did not commit the com- mittee. The matter bad first to receive the sanction of the Board of Education, and after that it was in the option of the committee whether they took the land or -not. They were not now committing themselves; they bad the first offer. The DIRECTOR explained that before they purchased the land they would have to pass a resolution stating the price they were going to pay and then it woul. have to be confirmed by the Town Council. They now asked for the Board of Education's approval of the site before going any further. Couucillor PEMBERTON : "Be purchased" it says. The Diarcrou : You cannot purchase it without a Tesnlution stating the price. The CITAIRNIAN said they had to make a start and this was the first step. it was non-committal. Councillor NIEL° said he took it they were asked to approve the principle—whether it was advisable to adopt the two schemea or not. If there were any insuperable dynctiits they were not forced to carry out the principik He heartily approved of the idea of provid- ing a recreation ground wherever possible. Mr. UesoN said Councillor Pem,-;erton seemed to know the price of the land. He was perfectly ignorant of it. Would the objects of the comtnittea be frustrated if he knew the price? Councillor PEMBERTON : 6,200. Councillor WILKINSON said there was no doubt they load the option of buying the land at a price from Lord</p>
        <p>, d Pr irift knoNk of it. if he k Conn Conic "bad the t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>Newton. Seven or eight years ago the Corporation bought some back land adjoining, the present site for the extension of the Isolation Hospital at Md. per yard. The land they now proposed to purchase was at the rate•of I:2d. per yard. One half the seven acres was front land, and abutted on Lovely-lane. Front land in Lovely-lane was being sold at 31 ao 31. per yard. There was no private street-making to be added in , respect of the actual school site; and he thought they were making a very good purchase. The price of the Beamont school site, incloding street_making was 4s. Sd. per square yard, and of the Bolton school site, also including street making, Os Old., per square yard. The Lovely Lane site would work out at 39. 8-13. per square yard and there would be no street makii.g. Alderman TINNION thought the principle advocated a very proper one. In a 'cry short time it would tie necessary to build a school in the neighbourhood, and three acres was the saiallest possible space they could devote to a school. There was a feeling in the ward that they should make provision for open spaces and recreation, and the land under notice was admirably adapted for the purpose. The Corporation would have to make some streets but the cost would int be aoy- thing like as large, he thought, as in the case of the other Council schools. Councillor EVANS said when the sub-co:mai ttee were seeking a site in the neighhourhool they decided this was the only one that would be, in e, ory sai:able for the purpose. The front land woall have to 00 pur- chased by the Street Improvement Committee of the Corporation for the purpose of widening the road, and then it was not desirable that the Workhouse Hospital and the infectious Diseases Hospital should he hemmed in with cottage property. (Hear, hear.) The site was one of the best, and certainly it would be one of the most useful. In the opinion of the sub-coaraittee school built on the site would save the expense of another school being built in Bewsey. Mr. JOLLEY agreed with Councillor Pemberton's view that whatever might have been in the hands of the sah- committee the words of the resolution bound them to the purchase of the land, subject to the approval of the Board of Education. While he agreed with Councillor Pemberton on that point he dit.t not agree with what he said afterwards. If the resolution did not bind theth it ought to do. If the price bad been a higher one than suggested, he (Mr. Jolley), would have suggested it would be a good purchase in the interests of the town. Cotinoillor BALL said Councillor Pernherton's point was that seeing they were buying a large piece of land ought they not to get it at a smaller price? He knew the price of land had gone Ito considerably and thought the committee would be wise in pressing forward the resolution. At the same , :le they pa-1.4 'I, C.mncillor Pemberton justice in naderstanding what he was getting at. The CrrAirtmaN said Couacillor Pemberton might rest assorNi that the resolution did not, in any way commit them. The question wanld he discos9ed•agaln, and could not be finished with until tha T "%via Council had given their sanction. The minutes were then carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>"HELPLESS AS A BABE."</head>
        <p>"HELPLESS AS A BABE."</p>
        <p>Woman's Fearful Sufferiog from Rheumatism. "Chas, FORDE'S bile beans Saved My Life."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Elizabeth Cole. of 14,- Argyle-r Brettenham-road, Upper ,Edmonton, London, has been restored to lasting health by the original and genuine CHAS. FORDE'S bile hearts, after years of fearful suffering from rheumatism. "Three years ago I cnrnmenced to suffer from terrible pains in my body," Mrs. Cole t ii a London journalist. "I became thin and emacia- ted. Several doctors gave me medicine, hut nothing did me any good, and finally I weat to the hospital. There they tried and tried again to give me relief, but failed, and at last I was told that my case was quite hopeless. "My fingers were swollen and twisted into all sorts of shapes. My feet, too, were all out of shape, and it meant excruciating pain to put them to the ground. People, .I have since found oat, spoke of me as dying, and not without cause. I was nine months in bed, awl during the whole of that time I was unable to do a hand's turn. I was as helpless as a babe. " While in this awful'. condition. I tried Cans'. FORDE'S bile beans, After taking 'Cans. FTE'' for a short time I could sit up it hel, a thug I had not done for nine months. Of ciurse I c rn- tinned to take C Cans. FORDE'S' ; the swellings in my hands and feet disappearel, the terrible pains went away entirely, and I could 'walk about in comfort. I was so delighted that I went back to the hospital and showed the doctor what CHAS. FORCE'S bile beans had done for me. " Re said, 'Mrs. Cole, you surprise me;l never expected to see you on your feet again.' I regard my cure as simply marvellous, and am convinced that Cans. FORCE'S bile beans saved my life." I,,,pang 7nnr nelrt supply insist unnn seeing "Cans. FORM'S " on the box label. No pills or beans are genuine without this distinguishing mark, " Chas. Foyle's" —tly7 e the kind .you have always blaght.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTTS A.C. v. T,TVERPOOL PENIBRO;KE</p>
        <p>-HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cln-b—v-lsited Knotty Ash on Saturday last, for the first inter-team fiKtitre between these clubs. The Cyclist section. a.f,tbe Pembroke Club laid trail over about eight mile, in the neighhenrhood of Knowsley, a rood portion being on the marls and a fast run resulted in the finishing ori!er being Pembroke: 1.0. A. Millington : A. T 3 trgess ;6. C. E. Howarth. (late of Sefton) ; 7, W. Sablick ; 11, M., Carrigan; 12,H. Allen. Warrington : 3, H. floittrao% : -1, J. T.Xtnllhall ; 5, TT. K. Jepson ; 8. A. Tilley ; 9, G. Watmough ; 10, M. Tavlin; 39 points etch in a count of si as in the Liver- pool and District Senior. Counting fire as in the Northern .Tunior the result 27. 29, gave Pembroke the victory by the narrow margin of two points, this makes the r3turn ran at Warrington on January 25th a ve-v interesting affair, seeing that the Northern Chamnionships are five.] for Fehrntr. 15th, and bearing in mini that the clubs floishei sth and 6th, resnectively last yti,ar in a field/of 42 clubs at Haydoltk Park. Millington who is right at thc top of his form, won by about 150 yards, he is a ranch improved runner and as he takes care of himself should be heard of later on. Holbrook finished close up to Tinrgess (whs had made the pace hot and held a substantial lead until two miles from home) now heads the Warrington points list with 20. followed by Mull-tall 16 ; J;-pson 16 ; and Wat- mon eh 15. Tilley deservedly earned the gold centre medal given by the Warrington Club for the first novice home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>WHiLEY Gutt.D.—At the meeting of this guild on 'Monday evening Aidermtn Bennett gave an address on "Heroes and Hero-Worship?! He took a type of popular hero the Right Hon. Joseph Chamberlain. sketching his career both as a mnnicipal reformer and as a statesman. He showed what great improvements had been effected in the city of Birruingha n, mainly owing to the initiative of Mr. Chamberlain and how whole-heartedly the paplace regards I him as their beau- ideal. in State affairs he was praised as a specially capable Colonial Secretary and one who hnd great ideas on the question of the unity of the Empire. At the conclusion the Alderman was warmly thanked for his paper. Mr. Roicollar presided. PRESENTATION. —At the conclusion of the meeting-of tne Wesley Gaild on Monday evening a presentat on was made to Miss Florrie Bennett, Grange Mount, on the occasion of her marriage. Mr. F. Wright, senior superintendent of the Sunday School, made the pre- sentation on beh tlf of the officers and teachers of the Sunday School, members of the choir, and the olicers' of the Wt•sley Guild. He spoke of Miss Bennett'a devoted service as a Sunday School teacher for several years, and her efficient help as a member of the choir,and also her regular attendance at the meetings of the Guild. All would feel the loss of her precene and services, but at the same time they nnited in wishing her every happiness and prosperity in her futu. e life. In the name of the subscribers he begged her accept- ance of a pair of very handsome candlesticks. Miss Bennett suitably responded.</p>
        <p>HORLICK'S MALTED MILK. Horlick's Malted Milk, which is largely spoken of as an Ideal food for all ages, is no doubt known, at all events by name to most of our readers. In dealing with a food, the question of ease of digestion, qoick- ness of assimilation and nourishing value is often not sufficiently considered—it may therefore be useful to emphasize the fact that according to the analysis issued by Messrs. Horlick, the food contains within itself all the constitutents as required by nature (in their proper proportions) for huilding up and strengthening the constitution and for forming bone, nerve and muscle, and in pelecting an article of diet this, coupled with ease of digestion and assimilation is one of the most important points to he sonsidered. Messrs. Horlick have however borne all these things in mind and the food which they put on the market as Horlick's Malted Milk embodies all these points and contains all the essentials to support life. , The food is packed in three sizes which are sold at Ils. 6d., 28. 6d. and lls, by all Chemists sod Stores, and when the value of the article as a food and 111111,1er up ,of the constitution is considered, it is a remarkably cheap article of diet—as calculator' upon the basis of a 2s. 6d. bottle, a " drink " costs under 11d. and a full meal for an Invalid or convalescent less than Bd., so that both on the score of cheapness and dietetic value the- food is of a very high standing, and our readers who have not already done so would do well to give it a trial, for which a free sample will be sent on applica- tion to Messrs. Horlick's Food Co., 34, Farrington-road, London, F.C. or Slough, Bucks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>The Exkiminer, Saturday November 23, 1907.</p>
        <p>the suspicions I undoubtedly have respecting the political honesty of these pioneers of sosial liberty. If we are to judge the sincerity of these vote-anglers by the size of the print on their electioa posters, then it is evident that they are sincere, but Mr. Editor we have been had ' before with these Election dodges. Trade Unionists have for many years been aaitating, striking, and using the contents of their war chest to get this reform, hut if we are to accept the philosophy of these Howley Wardites, it is evident that the aristocracy of labour have been wasting their time and their cash.</p>
        <p>What a blessing it would be if anly these wiseacre,3 would give us the benefit of their wisdom. A tomb in Westminster Abbey, or a statue in Bank Quay Park would be poor recompense, and such names as Shakes- peare and Charles Darwin and Bill Adams would fade into nothingness before such modern heroes. I need not intrude further upm your valuable space, but I should be pleased if someone who is familiar with the philosophy of election platitudes would kindly pour the oil of intelligence into the brain of yours truly, ANTI-HUMBua.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TE VICE O 1.41311.1 R.</head>
        <p>TE VICE O 1.41311.1 R.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman. . Readers of this column are invitej to assist ill in &amp;king it interesting and helpful. to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one sidc. of the puper , only, and mica cony to -,nakc their letters and notes short awl to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, bat the real name of the writer should be , for,olmied in confidence to the Editor of the column. ; must be atldresse to .Tob.n Workman, " Examiner" Office IVarrintiton and should -be sent in not l.ater than :Wednesday in each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MR. PARKER AND JOHN WORKIIAN.</head>
        <p>MR. PARKER AND JOHN WORKIIAN.</p>
        <p>After waiting a,. fortnight for John Workinan's answer to toy question it is disappointing to read a letter which meanders round the question, loses itself in a fog, and like me of Jerome's German trains never arrives anywhere. If the Editor eannit or will not answer my question why does be not frankly say so and be done with it.</p>
        <p>I will put it once more. In John Workman's pro- posed reconstructed Socialistic Suciety how are we, as individual workers to be remunerated? Is it to be solely according to our needs, or are efficiency and industry to be taken int) account. and if they are, how are the two principles to be harmonized ? Until this question is answered I must respectfully decline to reply to my opponent's new questions and continue a corresp,ndence which has become unprofitable. Sinet! writing above I have re-read John Workman's letter and I dimly see his meaning through the. fog. But are we to understand from his last sentence that he is going to answer my query in his next? It may be so, but may I point out that the question of the " right of subsistence "is not the one raised by me. C. E. PARKER, RAILWAY AND LAND MONOPOLY.</p>
        <p>In Answer to " Perplexed " it is somewhat strange that after reading my last three times he should be under the impression that I consider that the reward of a scavenger for a week's work is rightly measured by twenty. one shillings. If he will kindly read for the fourth time; the paragraph beginning "The equity of this will he acknowledged,provided there is equality of opportunity for everyone to exercise his power to produce," &amp;c., he will see that I clearly indicate my conviction that monopoly (especially the land monopoly) prevents the scavenger and every other Worker from getting the full reward of his labour through. its power to claim part of the produce. Until these monopolies are destroyed or effectually controlled, there can be no equality of opportunity, no full reaping of reward. Perhaps I can best make my meaning clear by an illustration familiar at present to our minds. The recent published earnings of railway servants revealed the fact that many were receiving a wage upon which it was impossible to maintain a family in decency, far less in respectability. The railway directors p Anted out that the earning of the whole capital invested in railways wls only about 31; per cent. and to give the advance asked in all grades would not only cause a large depreciation of the capital which they held in trust, but would make it impossible for them to rlise any further capital for necessary extensions. In their pos;tion as trustees they were perfectly right. Bat of the 2,1,282,801,009 invested as capital in the railways of the country hnv much of it is stored labour, real capital?</p>
        <p>Suppose a valuation were taken of all rolling stock, rails, roadways, stations at the cost of making we should find that this capital on this valuation was less than the nominal capital by .at least one third. How has the excess capital which is not represented by any tangible assets come into being? The following figures iodic Ite the price which Railway Companies have had to pay for laud and compensation in various froms to landowners. _</p>
        <p>Per mite of track.</p>
        <p>London and South-Western London and Birmingham Great IVestern London and Brighton 8;000 Taking these linee as average and at 31 per Cent. the Railway C .mpanies have to pty on a supposed capital of §.,6,150 per annum for lvery mile of track £214 10s. If we add to this Parliamentary and other expenses necessary to acquire possession of the land, the cost in fritQrest, or more accur:ttely rent, would excee I :£250 per annum ner mile. We read of one noble lord who was paid :V20,000 'merely to induce him to withdraw his opposition to a railvay bill. Se far we have only dealt with capitcti, but the land qiitinnaTtq thA ravonut, and dirniniQbact it at °very mint:- The Railways are the largest coal consumers. Trace the course of the coals from the pit to the engine fire-box. No pit can be worked util a royalty is paid to the owner of the soil under which the minerals rest: As soon as the coal reaches the bank it starts on its journey to the railway over land for which privelege tolls in the form of way-leaves have to be paid all along the journey. It travels in wagons the cost of which in wood and ircn has been raised through private land ownership, this apart from the labour put upon it. These are propelled by engines, every ton weight of which has mid its toll to the same interest. The same applies to a greater or less event to everything used in the construction or working of a railway. Thus we sea how both capital and charges are swelled by what has to he paid for right of access to nature _ . . _ - _</p>
        <p>I qThite realize that were land nationalized no industry would hive a free right to exploit urture for its iudividuil benefit, the-increased valle of the right of access would come t.) the State to be by it for the benefit of all. The differeace wool] he that instead of this increase being abs wbr.d by those who do nothing for it, it %valid come as an increlse to the reward of Ltbour, and be aster) towards the realization of our ideal, viz. that the worker shoal' get the full reward f his toil.</p>
        <p>In regard to those from whom the w•-irkers borrow the resnits., cf st re i lai)our, the only trne c yital, it is for the workeri to say whether they care to employ such c tpital and on what terms. If it is tt.)t required, or can he got oa more favourable terms from others, then to illusfrate. the small capitalists wh) put their savings into the Warrington Sivinqs B Ink will have to be content to lend it at a lower rate or lock it up. As I have said before labour should e.nploy capital not capital labour. _ _</p>
        <p>I tiust sufficient has :been said to indicate to " Perplexed "that what the community are prepared to give, or are obliged to give, in exchange for any service at the ipresent time is little indication of what they would have to give when the principle of equality of right to access to nature's storehouse was recognized and regulated by the State in the interests f all. Labour fend when I speak of labour all grades are meant a-nd not me: ely wage earners) will never receive its rightful reward as bug as it has to pay a tax at every point of activity to those who hold the land. Free Labour of this, aud it bec ones master in its own house. Capital is useless unless hired. Whether any particular f ,rm of capital is worth hiring and on whit terftri is for ' the employer, labour, to say. The rate of hiriag or of interest-will depend on the number of the workers anxious for its aid. • With equality of opportunity of access the probability is that, if we say at the present time that capital can be hired at 5 per cent capital lam ,me cheaper because it could be pro- duced at less cost and therefore be more plentiful and could he hired at a lower rate.</p>
        <p>No economic movement affecting land, capital, and labour will make any permanent progress until there is a clear appreciati n of the difference between the three factors.</p>
        <p>We want to begin at the beginning ad uaderstand the ineaning of the terms as they are to be used. Without this the only end of argument is confusion. Loose meanings are the mother of confusion as well as the last refuge of the feeble reas mer.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER, Belfast, November 19th, 1907. • . THE LIVING WAGE.</p>
        <p>This eplstle is perhaps like some of our local j expresses, somewhat !ate, hut I feel in a reminiscent mood, and my thoughts are for the moment wrapped up co to speak in the recent municipal elections in Warrington. Those of us wh had occasion to pass in the vicinity I of Howley Ward during the recent stirring of plitical mud could not help but notie the appeal made to the electors by the red party and the blue party to vote for the living wage. One can quite imagine the recipient of fourpence ; an hour wizing on this until the letters would be: stamped on his brains (if he had any) and arguing ' with himself as to whom he should honour with that portion of his rapidly diminishing store of private property,the cross at the ballot box. Seeing that one of these advocates of the living wage has entered upcn his political career one natur- ally asks " What about the living wage ?" Would it be impertinent to ask how this much desired reform is to be brought about. Perhaps lam too inquisitive, but the spirit of inquiry is too strong within me that lam willing t diminish the contents ef my ink bottle in order to obtain information which shall drive away</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FROM A CRITIC.</head>
        <p>FROM A CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having heard that a colgisn called the" Voice ,of Labour," appears weekly in 'The "Examiner " I purchased last weeks issup. After reading the I column, I fail to discnver th faintest "whisper," of Labour, but I notice the "roar " of the Socialist agitator.</p>
        <p>—Do you not think that is unfair? Why not state clearly the contents of the column. In reference to your statements, you speak of the ideal this and the ideal that.</p>
        <p>What is wanted is, "immediate "improvement, in the life of the working man. If you are a Trade Unionist, (as I hope you are) do you not think that an Actionist Labour Party,would do good in Warrington, consisting of the trade unionists of the Conservative Labour Party, the Progressive Labour League and others who are aiming at practical reform.</p>
        <p>To my mind it would meet with far more sympathy than a wrong-named Party whose only objects seems to be whether under Socialism "all the cards in the pack will be aces " and other such idealistic piffle. CURRENTIS, ONLOOK ER.—Hope to insert your ,contribution next week.—J.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS,</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS,</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. By " Ovalist.'</p>
        <p>Warrington first team was seen at home on Satur- day for the first time since October 5 with the excep- tion of a Monday match with Barrow, but the spectators had nothing like the treat they expected at the expense of euch a moderate side as Rochdale Hornets. The homesters won, certainly, but the victory was no credit to them, and as a matter of fact the team which played the best football lost. War- rington have seldom given a worse display of combination, and if the Hornets had not had the misfortune to lose Schofield early in the game they might have had the uncommon pleasure of beating Warrington at Wilderspool. They were certainly the smarter side and held the lead up to this accident, but afterwards the home lot's superior weight and strength decided the issue.</p>
        <p>I cannot bring myself to say much about the match, there was such a scarcity of good football shown. The Warrington halves were helpless whea it came to handling the hall, and after Brooks' fine dis- play the week before it was amazing to see him make mistake after mistake. In fact he did nothing credit- able all afternoon, and the only two men on the home side who played right up to form were Dickenson and Thomas. Even Tilley waS unreliable, Taylor was slow at centre, and the wings —well, least said soonest mended. There is a conspicuous want of pace about the Warrington back division at present, and neither Dickenson nor Taylor is running as well as formerly. Too mach kicking is also indulged in. The forwards are fortunately playing a strong game and keeping the team's head up.</p>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets are a team who have been unlucky all through the season. Several matches have been lost by narrow margins, and their record of only two wint4 in ten engagements does not do credit to the side. They played very smlttly at Warrington on Saturday, and a surprise victory was quite on the cards until Schofield got hurt. Baxter did great service, particularly on the defence, but for a county half-back he applied his boot to the ball far too frequently. There were some smart threequarters behind simply starving for pisses. Farrar at left c3ntre played splendidly and was quite the best back on the field. The Hornets have got a good capture in the ex-Brighouse lad. The forwards, though on the light side, stuck up to their bigger opponents most pluckily and were never really beaten for possessiop to any extent. ,</p>
        <p>The Northern League table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For. Agst. Per. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'ge. Oldham fitinslet Wigan 9 7 1 1 23 31 139 10 9 47 83.33 Barrow Batley . Warrington Hull ll 7 4 0 25 27 131 17 13 73 63.63 Broughton Keighley lO 7 3 0 23 21 109 15.12 66 60.00 Hull K. R..... 10 6 4 0 26 27 133 10 16 68 601)0 Sal ford i fax Wakefield T ll. 6 4 1 27 30 144 19 20 98 59.09 Dewsbury ll 5 6 0 15 18 84 18 19 93 45.45 Leeds York Bramley Bradford lO 4 6 0 16 17 83 15 25 105 401)0 Leigh Widnes lO 2 6 2 9 20 78 20 26 118 30110 Runcorn Swinton St. Helens ll 2 8 1 13 17 77 20 50 190 2212 Ebbw Vale ll 2 8 1 8 10 46 20 40 166 22.72 Huddersfield Rochdale lO 2 8 0 10 15 65 25 24 122 20•)0 Merthyr . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>A stiff task awaits Warrington to-day at Broughton, but I should not be surprised if memories of the cup- final there stimulate Warrington to some of their best football. They Will be strongly represented for once, in fact Tsherwood and Fish are the only w)table absentees from the picked team. The prospective beneficiare is suffering from a sPvere cold. Kenyon and Brooks go on the wings, and with Jenkins at half the back division should be more effective as an attacking force. ,</p>
        <p>The selected team is : Tilley, full back ; Brooks, Taylor, Dickenson, and Kenyon, tbreequarters ; Lees and JetikinF, half backs; Thomas, A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Shugars, Belton, and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington" A" team did well to beat Rochdale " away by 18 pints to 5 points on Saturday seeing that they had to release Cartwright and Jordan at the last minute. As Wigan "A " lost at Oldham —where Warrington secured two invaluable points the Championship of the Combination becoMes once more a very open event. Much interest will be taken in the game at Warrington to-day with the Rangers' smart second string, especially as Fish is billed to tarn out once more. I hope he will begin to nay serious attention to football, • otherwise his dazzling career will soon eni like a farthing dip. There is good football in him yet, if he will only get rid of hi 3 superfluous weight and train properly far the game at which he has no superior.</p>
        <p>The " A" team will be: Jordan, full back ; Fish, Cartwright,Landor and Harrison threequarters ; Lloyd and Daintith half backs; Jolley, Hewitt, Forster, Morley, Holbrook and Preston forwards.</p>
        <p>The new full back from Wiles has n)t yet been captured, but hopes are entertained that he will soon be seen spirting the Wartington colours He is a man I with a reputation as a custodian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Her Seventy-fifth Appearance</head>
        <p>Her Seventy-fifth Appearance</p>
        <p>Athe Warriogton B;ton0 Pjlte C )tl 1. )-1 T.lll- - Margaret M'Laughlin was charged with disorderly behaviour in Scotland-road and also with ass,Lulting Alice Thompson, the wife of the licensee of the Alexandra Vaults, Scotland-road. On Monday Mrs. Thomps'm heard a crash outside the vaults and went to see the cause. She accused M'Laughlin of throw- ing a jug at a window and pris nett thereupon struck Mrs. Thompson a blow in the face. • Stipa: intendant Monaghan said that that was prismer's sev.3ntylifth appearance and she was sent to gaol for two months.</p>
      </div>
