      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>MY DEAR . I expect you are wondering what Com- petitions are to be announced this Christmas. Well, as November is drawing to a close, I think it is now time to give you particulars. \ First, then, I shall offer a ss. casket of Fry'k I Chocolate to the sender of the best article for a poor child. This may be any article of clothing. To the sender of the 3 next best I shall give 3 2s. 6d. boxes of Fry's Chocolate. Also, as a first prize, a 2s. 6d. fancy box and 2 is. 6d. boxes of Fry's Chocolate to the best in the following Competitions:— For the best Short Christihas Story, about ; 200 or 300 words. For the best Joke. For the best Conundrum, and for the best piece of Poetry.</p>
        <p>To all those w- ho send any contributions, if they are not prize-winners, I shall give a little fancy mirror. All efforts must be sent me by the 14th of December.</p>
        <p>Now, I do hope all will try and send me some contributions, and do your best to win a prize. Mirrors have been sent to the following members for contributions received:</p>
        <p>J. Brearley, Ethel Collett, Elsie Talbot, Doris Talbot, W. Elliott, Francis Hayward, Doris Kivell, G. Kivell, Edith Croker, B. Farrar, and L. Talbot.</p>
        <p>Leslie Padley writes and asks me if old left- off clothing will do to send for the poor children. Well, Leslie, I am very glad to receive anything, but you know a little poor child thinks much more of a nice new article. Still, I shall be very grateful for anything.</p>
        <p>With love,</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>MY FAVOURITE PET.</p>
        <p>My favourite pet is the dove. A little while ago I kept a pair. They were very pretty little things, and I fed them on mixed corn, which they ate very heartily. One frosty morning, when I went to feed them, to my surprise they had got out, but they had not gone far. I went over to them, and found that they were very cold, so I took them in and gave them some warm food, which I am sure warmed them. On another occasion, in the summer, I went early one morning to feed them, and to my surprise saw the hen-bird in the nest with an egg under her. In the evemng I. saw that the cock-bird had got in the nest, and the hen- bird had got out and was eating its meat. The next day there were two eggs. I then had to wait a fortnight, and at the end of that time, to my delight, the eggs were hatched and a nice pair of young doves were showing.</p>
        <p>HA.RRY COPP,</p>
        <p>COMMON THINGS. The sunshine is a glorious thing, That comes alike to all, Lighting the peasant's lonely cot, The noble's painted hall. The moonlight is a gentle thing, It through the window gleams Upon the snowy pillow where The happy infant dreams. It shines upon the fisher's boat, Out on the lonely sea, And where the little lambkins lie, _ _</p>
        <p>Beneath the old oak tree. The dewdrops on the summer morn, Like diamonds, deck the grass; The village children brush them off, That through the. meadows pass. Blithe robin on the pear-tree sings Beside the cottage door; The heath flower fills the air with sweets Upon the pathless moor. There are as many lovely things, As many pleasant tones, , For those who sit by cottage hearths, As those who sit on thrones.</p>
        <p>ELIZABETH BREARLEY.</p>
        <p>PER.</p>
        <p>The people of Dublin tell this story of a poor man who used to sweep the crossing of one of their principal streets: As he had swept the street for years he became a well-known char- acter to the hundreds who crossed and re- crossed where he daily laboured. He was weather-beaten and poorly clad. A prominent lawyer in the city, in his practice, came across a certain legacy for which no heirs were found. The name of the testator haunted his memory and became a torment to him. It tormented him because he said to himself, "That is a familiar name to me, but I cannot place it." At length it came to him—" That is the name of the old street-sweeper; I wonder if he could be of the same family? " He studied the case up and found that he was. He was the heir whom the court wanted. The facts of the case established, it became the duty of this lawyer to make known to . the old man his good fortune. The old man was hard at work when he went to him. There, in the middle of the street, he told him his story, and the old man stood, broom in hand and mouth wide open in astonishment.</p>
        <p>This is the way the story ends: "So carried away. with the pleasure and excitement of his good fortune was the old man, that, uncon- sciously, he dropped his broom where he was standing and followed the attorney to enter on his new career." The broom! He had always been careful of that before. The broom! that had carried in it his livelihood. But he dropped it in the middle of the street, and forgot it, and left it where•the wheels of the traffic would break it to pieces. Do you wonder at hie treat- ment of the broom? He is a rich man now.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending in a piece of poetry, which I hope to see in print, as I have not written for a long time.</p>
        <p>MUD PIES. Tell me, little ladies, Playing in the sun, How many minutes Till baking is done. Susy gets the flour, All of golden dust, Harry builds the oven. Lily rolls the crust. Pat it here and pat it there, What a dainty size! Bake it on a shelf of stone, Nice mud pies. Now we want a shower, For we need it so; It would make the roadway Such a heap of dough. Turn them in and turn them out— How the morning flies; Ring the bell for dinner: Hot mud pies. GWENDOLINE KIVELL.</p>
        <p>••• • 10:•;4# , . • • t • • -', UNCLE • • E D. • .I.ievtvi•:;-;•• • CHILDREN,- A CROSSING SWEE -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MY FAVOURITE PET.</head>
        <p>MY FAVOURITE PET.</p>
        <p>I am going to tell you about my favourite pet. It is a dog. We do not keep him inside, but in a kennel. Sometimes, when the men selling goods come, he will bark at them until they go back, very much annoyed at him. His name is Pat. When quite young he fetched a large lump of beef into his kennel, and when we found him he had nearly eaten the whole of the meat. To-day I have just been promised two pigeons, but I have to wait for a place to keep them in. Not long since I had two rabbits, one named Jack and the other one named Topsy ; one a black and white one and the other all black, quite glossy. I have now five fish. I had two given me from one of my cousins, who had been fishing, and three last Saturday night my mother bought me. I must close now, with love, from your loving nephew, R. WARBURTON. GIVE. Give as you would if an angel Awaited your gift at the door; Give as you would if to-morrow Found you where waiting was o'er; Give as you would to the Master If you met His searching look; Give as you would of your substance If His Hand your offering took.</p>
        <p>Aunt: "Has any one been at the preserves?" (Dead silence.) "Have you touched them, Jemmy ? " Jemmy: "Pa never 'lows me to talk at dinner."</p>
        <p>; You can get a very good idea of "natural selection," in its practical workings, by viewing a celery glass after it has been once around the table.</p>
        <p>A Parisian musical dictionary defines a shout to be "an unpleasant noise produced by over- straining the throat, for which great singers are well paid, and small children well pun- ished." ._.....-4.-........</p>
        <p>A manufacturing wire-worker, in an advertise- Went, invites the public to come and see his in- bisible wire fence.</p>
