      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>VERY DANGEROUS.</head>
        <p>VERY DANGEROUS.</p>
        <p>Not long ago an Episcopal bishop was a guest at a dinner party in Baltimore. "By the wFy." said one of the guests, a woman, "do you know that there are times when it is dangerous to enter an Episcopal church?" "What is that, madam?" said the bishop, with great dignity, straightening_hiniself up in his chair.</p>
        <p>"I say, there are times when it is positively dangerous to enter the church," she replied. "That cannot be," said the bishop; "pray explain, madam." "Why," she said, "it is when there is a canon in the reading desk, a big gun in the pulpit, and when the bishop is chargitig his clergy, the choir is murdering the anthou, and the organist is trying to drown the choir." A hearty laugh went round the table at the bishop's eixpenee, and he acknowledged that at such a time he could well imagine it dis- agreeable, at least, if not dangerous, to be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>INVISIBLE ENEMY.</head>
        <p>THE INVISIBLE ENEMY.</p>
        <p>Henry Drumniond, in his fascinating little book on Tropical Africa, tells a curious story of the white ants which belong to that country. He says that they are the most secretive creatures in the world; even when they are attacking whole forests they come up under cover, building dirt tunnels up and down,tree trunks, to shelter them while they work. So unless one breaks into their laboratories with strong hand, he may witness wholesale desola- tion wrought by them, without even seeing a white ant.</p>
        <p>One may rise from his chair at night, and go to bed; get up in. the morning and see it stand- ing there apparently unchanged. But let him take his seat on it, and presto! he and the chair are in a heap on the floor. - What is the matter? Why, the white ants have come in the night and eaten all the inside out of the wooden legs, rounds, and frame; not a nick appears on the outside, but the chair is a mere shell by daylight. Worse than this, unless one is keenly on the alert, his whole house will be. thus bored through and through, and, without warning, some fine day, when he thinks he has goods laid up, its walls will tumble about his ears. All this time he hae not seen an ant!</p>
        <p>The right kind of boy with a peadlhooter can take a man's mind off his business troubles and politics quicker than anything else in this bleak, cold world.</p>
        <p>In a work, entitled, " Pomology," no fewer than nine hundred and forty-two kinds of apples are enumerated as now cultivated in Great Britain.</p>
        <p>The longeet stretch of telegraph wire in the world is across the Kistna River' in India, the span being six thousand feet in length.</p>
        <p>One San Francisco paper in the morning re- ported the sad death of a deaf-and-dumb girl. Its eve,ning rival, not to be outdone, gave also her last words.</p>
        <p>200 YEARS A SECRET.</p>
        <p>GRANNIE OINTMENT FOR SKIN TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>For 200 Years the Secret of Grannie Ointment has been kept in one Family. Its curative action is wonderful in all cases of WOUNDS. CUTS, BRUISES, BAD LEGS, and all SKIN TROUBLES. It at once soothes and heals, and cannot hurt the most delicate skin. In many cases of very long standing it has quickly cured, even when doctors have despaired. Froe for 14stamps. GRAN NIE COMPANY, NORWICH.</p>
