      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday November 23, 1907. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday November 23, 1907. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>Matches for November 23rd. Warrington St. Paul's v. Monks, Hall. Warrington Central v. Latchford. Warrington St. Barnabas' v. Penketh. Fairfield v. Burtonwoorl. Bartonwood Social v. Palatine. Walker's Brewery V. Fairfield Reserve. Arpley v. Central Reserve Warrington St. Elphin's v. Orford. Monks Hall Reserve v. Park Juniors. WARRINGTON CUP. SECOND ROUND DRAW. Matches to be played on December 7th. Halton Villa v. Burtonwoocl A.F.C. Penketh v. Warrington Albione Thelmall United v. Winnington Park. Halton A.F.C. v. Oughtrington Park. Lostock Gralaru v. 'Warrington St. Paul's. Castner Kellner v. Warrington St. Mary's. ' Sutton Commercial v. Bethel Mission. Orford A.F.C. v. Lymm.</p>
        <p>Results for November 16th.</p>
        <p>Latchford 4,, WaAington St. Pauls 7. Monks. Hall 2, Fairfield 3. Burtonwood A.F.C. 1, Lymm 0. Warrington Central 3, St. Barnabas' 0 Palatine 0, Per eth 0.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAINING COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC SHOCKS.</p>
        <p>Science, which is explaining everything, has now accounted for the queer little ner- vous shiver which comes upon one now and then ,without apparent cause. It is generally put down as "someone walking over my grave." Actually we are' told it is the effect of electricity. This great power is constantly being generated everywhere, and when the positive and negative parts of the power meet they produce a shock on any living thing. There is a good deal of electricity in the air, and when the human body is made a meeting point the sen- sation is liable to be felt about the region of the spine. Some people are liable to shocks now and then in special parts of them, in the ankle, for instance, or very commonly around the base of the brain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Countess of Sefton Opus New Wing.</head>
        <p>Countess of Sefton Opus New Wing.</p>
        <p>BISIIOP OF LIVERPOOL AND RELIGIOUS EDUCATION</p>
        <p>The Countess of Sefton on Monday afternoon opened a new wing to the Warrington Training College and the occasion was one of great interest in the history of the institution. The extension consists of the premises formerly occupied by the Clergy Daughters' School which has been removed to Darley Dale. The training }college authorities have acquired two acres of land and 'the buildings thereon, the object being to protect the college from interference with its use for educational purposes.and to prevent the deterioration of its property by being surrounded by buildings which would shut it in on the North-east and South sides. With the approval of the Board of Education, the price paid was £3,375, but it will be nebessary for development of the 'property to incur an expenditure on it of about £2,500, making with the expenses, estimated at £225 a total sum of £6,100. A contribution of £5OO has been given towards this amount by the Hon. Misses Wilson-Patten, and the Board of Education have authorised a loan not exceeding £2,600 repayable by instalments, to be raised lon mortgage of the whole college property, which is 1 valued at £lO,OOO in case of the amount required for purchase not being otherwise provided. 1, There was a large attendance at the opening ceremony ' The Lo rd Bishop of Liverpool presided and he %I-a. supported on the platform by : The Principa (the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson), the Countess o"' Sefton, the Mayor of IVarrington and Miss SnAthurst The Venerable the Archdeacon- of Warrington (how • secretary of the college), and Miss Spooner, the Rev. • Canon and Mrs. Penrhyn, the Rev. Canon and Mrs • Willis Mrs. Stevenson and Mrs. Ferriman (Lady Super-. intendent). The following ladies and gentlemen accepted the invitation of the committee and Principal of the college:—the Rev. Dr. Dickson, the Revt.T.W.and , Mrs. Barber, the Rev. H. Baud Mrs. Blogg, the Rev. W. and Mrs. Bracecamp, the Rev. G. C. and Mrs. Briggs, •the Rev. F. H. and Mrs. Burrows, the Rev. J. Collins, the Rev. L. J. T. and Mrs. Darwall, the Rev. T. and Mrs. Lee-Jones, the Rev. H. A. and Mrs. Lester, the Rev. H.V. and Mrs.Pigot, the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, the Rev. P. and Mrs. Stewart,Mr. and the Honourable Mrs. Willis, Colonel and Mrs. Mrs. Edelsten, Captain Crosfield, Alderman and Mrs. Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Atkin, Mr. and Mrs. Bowden, Mr. and Mrs. Burgess, Mr. and Mrs. Carruthers, Miss 'Coxon' Mr. Darby, Mrs. Davies, Mr. and Mrs. Fair- cloup, Mr. and Mrs. Fulton-Smith, Miss Grayson, Miss Jenkins, (Edge Hill Training College), Mr. and Mrs. McGregor,Mr. and Mrs. J. Moore Murray,Mrs.Percival, Mr. and Mrs. Piorpoint,Mr. and Mrs. Rossiter, Mrs. and Miss Rylands, Mr. and Mrs. Vere-Davies Mr. and the Misses White, Mr. and Mrs. J. White, Air. Woodcock. In addition there were present the Resident Staff and the Resident and Day Students of the College. Canon STEVENSON gave a highly interesting history of the Clergy Daughters' School, \which dates from 1697. The extension, be pointed out, comprised a large class room, nature study room, and dormitory accommo- dation for 46 students in additiain to two or three me,»bers of the staff and servants. That had enable d them to relieve from the hostel at Orford thirt y students, and to lessen the aumber in the old part of the College, and therf3 was now unity of the whole College. , Many people might be afraid of putting their hands into , their pockets on behalf of training colleges at the present time. They were threatened with what seemed to be a measure of grave injustice. Those colleges, which had been built and supported by the Church of England, it was said were to be thrown open to those who were not members of that Church. They did not • see the necessity, or justice of that. It was sometimes , said the Government gave them such a large grant that ! the college ought not to be confined to one denomina- tion. Although the Government grant was certainly large, the friends of that College bad spent £20,000 upon it during the twenty years that he had been Principal, , averaging £l,OOO a year. There was no better defence for their colleges than to be able to shcw people they were spending money upoh them. That was an argu- ment the man in the street could understand, and they would then with great justice be able to claim some 1 measure of consideration as to whom they should admit l into them. (Applause.) The COUNTESS OF SEFTON then declared the huildings open and was presented vi ith a lovely bouquet by Miss A. Holme, the head student. -</p>
        <p>The BISTIOP OF LIVERPOOL, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Countess of 6eiton, said they were to- day in the face of apparent disaster an,' catastrophe, but the laying of foundation-stones and the enlarte- ment of their training colleges showed that they believed that the future was with them, as regarded the elementary schools and training cnlleges. On September 21st the Attorney-General for Ireland,speak- ing in Liverpool, expressed an opinion that the Govern- ment did not wish to destroy the Church of England or voluntary schools, nor interfere uith the right of the parent or its instructor to select religious education which they liked for their own children. "'hose state- ments tilled them with hope, but he much regretted that Mr. M'Kenna, in Wales very recently, declared he</p>
        <p>could not substantiate them. In a rely to a letter which he (the Bishop) wrote to him last week, he said he could neither confirm nor deny the statements !nada by Mr. Cherry. That meant he supposed that the Church of England had very little to expect from the drastic Education Bill which they were threatened with in the coming session. It was their duty to have a clear and definite policy, to show a united front in advocating it. That policy could be summed up in a very few words— the right of the parent. They maintained that every parent hada light to decide as to the religious education of his or her child and to demand that the child</p>
        <p>should be taught in religious mattel7, in schools hours, by teachers who I,elieved what they taught. The other day Dr. Macuamara admitted that this policy was just and symmetrical, but impracticable.. He (the Bishop)maintained that if it were just the English nation should make it practicable. Speaking of training colleges, he said that the Church of England had done nobly in building, maintaining,and enlarging its training colleges. If their Nonconferinist friends had shown some of their energy and liberality in providing colleges and teachers for their own schools, there would to-day he no grievance whatever. In August of this year like a bolt from the blue came Mr. al'liennit's famous circular. The meaning of that circular was that it for- bade them practically to build any new training colleges, although they had not sufficient accommodation to provide for all thoseXhurch students who applied for admission. They eould not be allowed to open any hostel in connection with their training colleges for Nonconform ' ists nor open any hostel in connection with day training colleges for their.owu students. The result would be that the places in colleges built by Church of England money for the training of future teachers for their Church school s already insufficient for themselves, might be at any time taken up by Nonconformists, Roman Catholics, Jews, and Agnostica. That was why the Principal, rightky in his opinion, expressed a keen sense of injustice in connection with that famous circular He ventured to think the circular struck a blow at religious instruction everywhere,because young students at present were drawing back from the religious educe. tion,knowing that the qualification was no longer needed for entrance into their training colleges, and w ith- drawing fr3rn the religious examination. They were in danger of having a large number of less keen studenta joining their training colleges, having no religious iustruction themselves, who would go forth to teach others in the future, lie was one "ho strongly believed in a common Christianity and in simple Bible teaching. But he wanted it to be Christianity and Bible teaching and it was because there was a e!eat danger of their raising up teachers who had a Christianity of their own, who put their own interpretation on the Bible, and not, the interpretation of the great society which their blessed Lord founded, and which they called the Church, that he trembled as bethought of some of the effects Mr. Nt'Ketnot's circular would have on religious teaching of this land. ilek desired not only that the child should be trained in body and mind, but in soul also for the world to come. (Applause.) The MAYoR OP WARRINGTON (Alderman Smethurst) seconded the resolution, which was unanimously carrier. The COUNTESS OF SEFTON briefly replied and expressed her complete sympathy with the work of the College. On the proposition of the RECTOR OF WARRINGTON (Canon Willis), seconded by Cgiun PENRHYN, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Bishop. Tea and coffee were served in the second year school- room and the whole of the college was thrown open for the inspection of visitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ROMANCE-KILLING MACHINE</head>
        <p>ROMANCE-KILLING MACHINE</p>
        <p>An ingenious machine for measuring the emotions has been designed by the Italian physiologist, Professor Mosso : Its principle depends on the fact that emotions influence the blood pressure in various parts of the body. If, therefore, the variations in blood pressure can be measured, it is easy to arrive at the strength of an emotion. In construc- tion the instrument resembles a balance, and upon it reposes the person whose emotions are to be tested. An unpleasant sensation will usually diminish the quantity of blood in the brain; a pleasant produces the reverse effect. Mental effort can be similarly mea- sured, but it is said that emotional activity registers itself much more strongly than in- tellectual exertion.</p>
        <p>TOBACCO SMOKING AND CONSUMPTION. Does smoking save people from consump- tion? This is a question which has been raised by Dr. J. C. McWalter. He tells us that "the persons whom I had noticed in the out- patient departments of the Dublin hospitals as non-smokers and suffering from phthisis (consumption) were of the age of from sixteen to twenty-two years, and males." To him, further, it seems that no social habit of con- sumptive patients is so remarkable as their general abstinence from tobacco. He does not think that the inhaling of tobacco smoke has any direct germ-killing effect, but it does not seem unscientific, remarks Popular Science Siftings, to hold that the fumes may have an effect on the membranes of the breathing passages such as will make them a less suitable soil for the consumptive germ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SPIRILLA.</head>
        <p>SPIRILLA.</p>
        <p>• Spirilla corkscrew-like threads of exces- sive minuteness are parasitic organisms, like bacteria, bacilli, and micrococci. They -are of different kinds—some harmless, some deadly, writes Sir E. Ray Lankester in the • Daily Telegraph. One is egmmon in the mouth of the healthiest of us; another causes one, of our most terrible diseases. They can be distinguished by the microscope, though much alike. What microscopists call "dark- ground illumination" that is, illumina- tion by horizontal rays of light, obtained by a prism attached below the glass slip on which the object is placed for examination with the microscope—has been found at the Institute Pasteur to be a very ready way of shewing the spirilla in fresh blood or sputum. The spirilla are alive, and are seen when highly magnified, shooting rapidly across the field of view with a corkscrew action, like brilliant silver threads. The detection of the microbe which causes an infective disease is often the first step to the control of the , disease, or to knowledge which enables man to avoid the disease altogether. Some strik- ing examples of this have occurred of late years. I THE " WEATHER-PLANT " AT KEW.</p>
        <p>As bearing on the question of the "weather-plant" (Abrus precatorius) as an , indicator of meteorological conditions, it may be worth recording, writes a corre- spondent of the Westmtnster Gazette, that , some years ago Professor F. W. Oliver, Quain Professor of Botany at University College, London, carried out a thorough test of the plant at the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew, and the result was published in the Kew Bulletin. This report stated that no connection had been proved between the movements of the leaflets and any subse- quent changes in the weather. These charac- teristic movements were, it was further stated, entirely due to changes in illumina- tion, which immediately caused the plant to alter the position of its leaflets, and a small cloud passing over the sun was quite suffi- cient to bring them from an almost vertical position to the horizontal. The Abrus pre- , catorius is only one of several members of the same family so affected by alterationk light rays.</p>
        <p>HYGIENIC USE OF YELLOW GLASSES. Now and then the use of yellow glasses has been recommended for spectacles to persons with highly sensitive eyes. Motais, a French opthalmologist, of Angers, read a paper on this subject before the Paris Academy of Medicine, and recommended the use of</p>
        <p>• yellow or orange-tinted glasses, from his ex- perience of fifteen years. The French jour- nal, Cosmos, discussed Motais's paper as follows: These glasses give a remarkable illumina- tion. The sky objects are lighted up with warm tints, very, agreeable to the eye. Be- sides and despite this luminosity, they pro- duce a quieting effect, so that with tints proportioned to the intensity of the light or to the retinal sensitiveness, the MOST SENSITIVE EYE MAY BE PRESERVED. They are most agreeable, the inienser the light, and are consequently recommended to travellers in high latitudes or on the snow- fields of mountain regions, where they are ex- posed to the, blinding rays of the sun. They also modify, in summer, the brilliancy of the sands on a sea beach. In the mountains, on an automobile excursion, their illumination enables the traveller to regard the widest views without fatigue. Irritable eyes, even when they have normal visual poAers, will find it advantageous to substitute the agree- able impression of yellow glasses for the gloomy tint of blue or smoked glasses.</p>
        <p>THE SARGASSO SEA MYSTERY. Ever since Columbus discovered the Sar- gasso Sea on September 14th, 1492, the mys- tery of that strange waste of the Atlantic Ocean has fascinated men. Its wonders dis- tracted the attention of his sailors from their fears, and gave them a fresh topic. "They began," wrote the Admiral in hia journal, "to see many tufts of grass which were very green, and appeared to have been quite recently torn from the land." The masses of vegetation, turning the surface into greenery through which the waters seemed to ooze, became a new source of terror to the sailors, whoise murmurs grew louder; for were they not likely at any mo-</p>
        <p>mean.t to be I DASHED TO PIECES UPON HIDDEN ROCKS? Even to-day, comments the Globe, scien- tific men are not fully agreed upon some ; phases of the, Sargasso Sea, but the best opinion seems to be that this part of the Atlantic is a basin or pool on a vast scale, . with its great Equatorial Current moving • west, and its Gulf Stream flowing east. Thus, a circular motion of water is engen- : dered, and as the Sargasso bea is the centre of the whirl, all flotsam and jetsam are 2aturally attracted to it. A writer in Har- . per's Magazine says that: " Kuntze, who has seen the weed 'in situ' ;nit the AL•ilalean . coasts. says that it is torn</p>
        <p>from nib grutrres -us- storms, iwunea into - gasso by currents, and quickly decays and sinks. He has no faith in the fructification of the drifting plant or in the growth of its fragments. Dr. Krummel tells of the enor- mous lnnotb of o:mat-lines on which the weed grows'.• 'ftTe says tne weed, atter -rescuing tne Sargasso, becomes heavier as it grows older, and gradually sinks, making room for fresh supplies. His approximate calculations tend to shew that it takes about five and a-half months for the weed to • drift to the eastern part of the Sargasso Sea; and he believes that sit recent researcnes, up TO me time ne wrote, tended to disprove the hypothesis of a marine origin, and to support those who welt that the algte came from tha</p>
      </div>
