      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>HIGHER EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>HIGHER EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Dean of Manchester on German Enterprise.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was filled with an interested audience on Wednesday evening, the occasion being the dis- tribution of prizes and certificates to the students of the School of Art, Secondary School, Central Technical Institute and Branch Technical Schools by the Dean of Manchester (the Right Rev. Bishop Welldon, D.D.) The students and their friends mustei ed in strong force and there was also a fairly large representation of the Education Committee. Prior to the meeting Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac. gave an organ recital which was highly appreciated. The items were :—Minnetto, Salome Chanson de matin, Elgar ; Three Dances (Henry TIM) German; Stiindcben, SchuLert ; Corona- tion March, Godfrey. The meeting was presided over by Councillor J. Fulton-Smith (chairmin of the Education Committee) and amongst those present on the platform were : The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Mr. H. Roberts (eeputy-chairman), Revs. Canon Stevenson, N. Wilson and W. Bracecamr, Councillors Evans, Nield, and Skelland, Captain Crosfield, Messrs. E. Plinston, A. J. Jolley, J. Moore Murray (director-of education). R. B. Roberts (assistant direotor), E. S. A. Robson (head- master of the Secondary School and Technical Intsitute) C. Mawson (headmaster of the School of Art), and members of the staffs of the various school.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN having announced the receipt of I apologies for non-attenance from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and Alderman Bennett, congratulated the successful students. He also congratulated the teachers on the success which had attended their eilorts,and said they were finding out every day the value of the solid Information and training given in the Elementary school, which reflected itself in the success of the Secondary and Technical schools. The committee had been aiming specially at the co-ordination of the educa- tional system,and the more the various branches worked in unison the better would be the result in fitting the children to take their places as useful citizens. The Chairman concluded by extending a very cordial welcome to the Dean of Manchester. He likened War- rington to Manchester's "little brother," but said he was sure no welcome given to the Dean, even in Man- chester, could be more hearty than that accorded by the people of Warrington. (Applause.) A song, "The White Squall," was then effectively interpreted by Violet Brown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR then submitted the annual report as follows :</p>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The School year which has just closed has enabled us to see how far our expectations of the school have been realised. When the Committee established the school four years ago it was intended to provide a general education for the children of those parents who wished them to stay at school until they were sixteen years of • age, but at that time the regulations of the Board only allowed of a special scientific training. The Committee used every efkleavour to get the regulations changed as they felt that the work of the school would not be as successful as they would desire. After refusinr, the request of the Committee for a more general curriculum, the Board of Education itself introduced regulations in</p>
        <p>the following year which made it possible for the Com- mittee to carry out its original intentions. The more general education has been given for the last two or three years and we are now able to see some result from this change. The following are the successes at the University examinations :—Matriculation Examina- tion of the Northern Universities, one scholar passed in the First Division and five in the Second Class; London University Matricul4ion Examina- tion, two passed in the Second Divaion ; Senior Oxford Local Examination, one was successful. At the Preliminary Certificate Examination of the Board of Education the results were most satisfactory. Hitherto the results have not come up to.s expectations</p>
        <p>but this has been because of the want of Poliminary</p>
        <p>training on the part of the candidates. As, however, -a greater number of the candidates have been for at least three years in the Secondary school, this cause has been removed. 21 candidates were presented for examination and of those 20 passed, three obtained four distinctions two obtained two distinctions and four obtained one, distinction. Thus nine out of 20 successful candidates obtained distinctions. These results compare most favourably with those of other towns.</p>
        <p>Such sUccesses as these show that the school can take its place and bear comparison with the results obtained in other schools of a similar character. But the enabling of scholars to pass examinations is not the only function of the school and it is pleasing to know that scholars who have left the school after three or four years attendance have been able to obtain good posi- tions in public and private offices. SCHOOL OF ART. Last year reference was made to the falling off in the number of students at the School of Art and it was pointed out that the stress of competition compelled boys to turn their attention to subjects of a more utilitarian character. Last year the number of entries was greater but the number of subjects studied was less than in the previous year. Students chose those subjects which were of the most importance to them in their daily work and as a consequence the higher and perhaps more artistic subjects did not receive much attention. The results in those examinations which took place were an Improvement on those of the previous year only nine out of sixty-seven failing as against 25 out of 68 in the previous year. The feature of the result is the number of first classes obtained, these being 22 as against Hin the previous year. In the examination made by the Board of Education, of the works sub- mitted for the Art Class Teacher's Certificate, the fol- lowing pupils works were accepted, viz. : MODEL.—Dobson Clark, Sydney Clatworthy and Jane E. Pearson. LETTErtnio.—Dobsort Clark.</p>
        <p>_ During the year Miss E. Mottram, a former student of the school, was successful in obtaining the Associateship of the Royal College of Art. At the end of the school year gr. Aylward, the Head Master, resigned the position of Head Master, and the good wishes of all his old students and the Committee will go with him in his retirement. The Committee after carefully considering he qualifications and claims of a large number of applicants, unanimously appointed Mr. C. Mawson to the position. Mr. Mavvson is with us noW for the first time, and we extend to him our greeting, and we look forward with confidence to the fulfilment of the expectations formed of his abilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Last year attention was called to the introduction of the Grouped Courses. These have been arranged so that the student may take up the study of those subjects related to his trade subject. It is exceedingly difficult to make a boy understand that however well he may be able to draw, he will not be able to design an engine unless he knows something about the quantities of materials and about mechanics and mathematics. When a boy who is engaged in an engineering works comes to the Technical Institute, the only subject he wishes to take is Machine Construction, and similarly the boy who is engaged in a Chemical Works wishes to take Inorganic Chemistry. After a time it Is found that their knowledge of Mathematics and the other subjects related to their trade subject is so poor that these boys are only able to take the most elementary stages, and in time they become disgusted with their progress and probably leave the class. Some have stuck to the one subject, and having arrived at a certain standard have traded upon their qualifications much to the detriment of the Technical Institute. Many boys having obtained a certificate for one parti- cular subject, when tried by their masters have been found wanting, as their knowledge of the subject has been mainly empirical. In this way the employers have been led to judge the resu:ts of the Technical Institute training and have placed them at a very low level. It is hoped that this will be avoided in the future by making it impossible for boys to take only one subject but to give them such a general education in the subjects connected with their trades that they can be recommended by the Institute with confidence to any employer. It is too early to see the benefits of this change in the Science side of the Institute but in the Commerical side the benefits are already to be seen in the results of the examinations.</p>
        <p>Opportunity is taken now to publicly express the thanks of the Committee to those employers who have so generously aided them by their active support and interest in those of their employees who attend the Institute. It is a pleasure to be able to state that all the large business firms in town now make use of the reports on the work of their employ&amp; forwarded to them and this can only result in advantage to the students, the Institute, and the trade of the town. In the Science Examinations, the number of students successful in passing In the first class is not so great as last year. This is probably due to the fact that the number of advanced candidates was greater this year than last. There is no falling off in the quality of the work being done in the Science classes, in fact, there is a considerable gain, as the students in the classes are far more sincere than formerly and the work is being done more thoroughly. In the Commercial Examinations the improvement recorded last year has been more than maintained. In the year ending 1909 41 per cent, of the scholars took the intermediate or advanced examinations. The improvement is still more marked when the figures are compared with those of 1903 when only 33 per cent. of the students were examined in the higher stages. When the results of the examinations are compared there is found to be steady advance, the number of first class successes having increased considerably. Two students obtained the first place in commercial correspondence and business methods and book-keeping, respectively, and two students were placed third in arithmetic and I first year's technological course respectively. The num- ber of students who obtained distinctions was 15 Is , Against 10 f9y the previ9es year. The following</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
