      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OLD AGE PENSIONS,</head>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS,</p>
        <p>UNIVERSAL SCHEME ADVOCATED. Socialist M P.'s at Warrington</p>
        <p>age. The principle underlying it was to deal with the THE DRINK QUESTION. recipients of relief in a manner which indicated that they were regarded as coming to the Poor Law because --- of de-merit or ill-doing and to give them the relief in Progressive Labour League's Debate. such a SCURVY AND DEGRADING WAY as would spur them on to more sustained efforts in the MORE SUPPORT FOR THE " EXAMINER'S " ranks of industry. He believed the best of the poor PROPOSAL. never went near the Poor Law and would not. (Applause.) Dealing with the question on the basis of ' a ss. pension for all who has attained their sixty-fifth year, Mr. Barnes said he sometimes had a contempt for his own class that they should be pottering about with that paltry ss. a week demand. When he considered the great power they possessed and which if used as it ought to do would win for them all that equality of opportunity which had been the dream of poets and prophets of all time, he stood amazed at their moderation. Estimating the number of people over 65 years of age at two millions the cost of a as. pension would be £26,000,000 a year. Mr. Barnes, how- ever, showed that for various reasons there would be large deductions from that number. He maintained that the country could well afford the expense if the burden of maintaining the aged poor were transferred from the shoulders of those who were least able to bear it, and put on the wealth of the nation. A stupendous amount of wealth was produced in the country every year—about 1,800 millions—and the extra- ordinary thing was that those who did all the work got the least and</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the Progressive Labour League held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening, the subject of debate was "The drink question." MI W. H. Sherburn presided over a fair attendance. Mr. J. 11. GRIFFITHS introduced the subject: "1 don't stand here as a politician," he said in opening, "I stand here as a red hot teetotaller—nothing more nor less. I stand here because I believe there is no question which affects the working classes to such an extent as this. It is the most momentous problem which now facea us and faces our legislators as well." Mr. Griffiths was glad to say there had been a decrease of male drunkenness in the land, but he had to deplore the fact that there was an alarming increase in female drunkenness. The feminine fraternity was going down at a terrible rate; immorality was on the increase. Nlother- hood was a word which was rapidly becoming obliterated. They had been stunned during the last twelve months by the cry of the children. He would be disappointed if in the next Licensing Bill there was not something put forward to stop drunken mothers carrying children into the stifling atmosphere of the public-house.lf they visited some of the back streets of Warrington they would be disgusted by what they saw. I they visited many of the slums in Warrington they need not read the articles of Mr. G. R. Sims on "The Black Stain" to prove that the cry of the children was a stern and awfnl realit y The drink bill last year amounted to 160 millions. Of that 19 millions went to manufacture' 35 millions went to the national exchequer and 111 millions to the brewery shareholders, who included six bishops and 170 Church of England clergy. It was an alarm- ing fact that the money was going into the hands of those who were so unscrupulous as not to allow the temperance party to have the slightest look in if they could avoid it. They had evidence of that recently in Warrington. What were they going to do to remedy the evils. They expected much in the way of legislation, because they had been promised much. One of the premier thoughts with them was the reversal of the Act of 1901, which was one of the most retrograde measures ever passed, it conferred a vested interest in licences. They required Sunday closing, and thus release 20,900 of their fellow-workees who were now engaged on a Sunday in supplying the wants of drunkards. They also required election day closing, and he believed if that came about there would be far different results. He would be dissappointed if that was not included in the next Bill. They wanted the abolition of barmaids. It was said they had no right to throw 27,000 young women oat of employment without their consent. There was no doubt that young men were attracted to the public- house because there was a sweet young woman behind the bar, and young women were tempted to take posi- tions as barmaids because of the company they met. He thought the public-house was not elevating for a young woman, and on the expiration of seven years all women behind the bar, with the exception of the licensees' wives and daughters, should terminate their engagement. During the seven years no more barmaids should be engaged. He would go in for the prohibition from public-houses of children under the age of 14. One of the most crying evils was the grocers' licences. and until they were wiped out female drunkenness and the cry of the children would be ever with them. They should have proper control and supervision of clubs of all sorts. The day had come for direct local veto, and that ought to apply to clubs as well as to public. houses. He was dead against the nationalization or municipalization of drink, which would never bring about the desired result. If they municipalized drink the day would come when the public weal would be sunk for the public financial benefit. The I.L.P. embodied drink in their programme of municipalization , and nationalization because they wanted e,erythinff put under such control. Very little about the nationaliza- • tion of the drink traffic was heard from their platforms. , The Public-house Trust, Mr. Griffiths contended, was ! nothing more than a delusion, and he was opposed to disinterested management. He was not downhearted about the results of the recent local elctions, and , believed that in time they would have in Warrington a creditable temperance council. There had been such organ- izations in the past but they had been dropped because I the men who professed to oe stalwarts had turned their backs on the movement and solo them to "the trade." ' The day must come when the Christian Church must rise up and shake off the trammels which bound her to a trade against which the whole instincts of humanity , were crying out. In conclusion, Mr. Griffiths moved, , "That this meeting rejoices in the promise of the Prime Minister to introduce a great and comprehen- sive measure of temperance reform' as the first business !of the next session of Parliament, and expresses its earnest hope that whilst including provisions for the early termination of vested interests in licences, the closing of public-houses on Sundays and election days, with shorter hours on weekdays, the non-engagement of barmaids, the abolition of grocers' licences, the exclu- sion of children from publio-houaos, and the, offective control of clubs, the Bill will, above all, include the power to prevent by local direct vote, the issue ar renewal of liquor licences, untrammelled by any pro- ! vision for municipalization, trusts, or so-called dis- interested' management of the liquor traffic ; and this 1 meeting further pledges itself to support the Govern- ment by all the means at its command in promoting a Bill on those lines." i Mr. JOHNSON, who said ha tril-C not, a teetotaller seconded. He voted for a publican at the recent elec- tion because there were other questions before the public. 1 Mr. O'BRIEN supported the resolution, and gave con- vincing illustrations of how the question affected , labour.</p>
        <p>THOSE WHO NEVER WORKED and whose boast it was that they never did any work, waxed fat and fared sumptuously every day. The wealth of the country obtained in a social manner should be treated more than it had been as a social asset,and used for old age pensions amongst other social purposes. The people who were in possession of the immense incomes never earned them. In taxing these fortunes there would not only be moral justification for the course, but they would be doing something that would be of advantage to the wealthy themselves, that would bring to them a sense of havieg done something with their money through Governmental institutions to relieve their poor old fellow citizens. (Applause.) MR. WALSH II.P. AND THE RIGHT TO WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. STEPHEN WA.LSH, M.P., in seconding, said the attitude of the Labour party was one of protest, warn- ing and demand. They protested against the nation any longer continuing to act on the old theory of indiviCualism because that theory in its results was already self-condemned. In the midst of wealth passing the dreams of avariee, one in 32 of our popula- tion were in the workhouse, and large masses were con- stantly out of employment. lie believed family life was the basis on which the State rested. Every member of the Labour party, every fair-minded mall or woman knew perfectly well that the cant, clap trap and misrepresentation which had been current during the past few months were due to the fact that the people who had indulged in it had recognised the truth of Dean Swift's saying 'Throw plenty of mud and some of it is sure to stick." Not a single statement they had made about it being the object of the Labour party or of Socialism to break down family life was true, and it only showed to what depth of degradation ordinary party politics could descend when professedly-decent people, honourable citizens in many respects, leaders of thought and of parties Could</p>
        <p>DESCEND TO SIiCH A HELLISFI DEPTH as they had done during the past two or three months. The Conservative and Liberal leaders said they could not recognize the right of the workless citizen to be provided with work by the State. He did not believe t'aose leaders represented the feelings of the people A ho in the past had voted Liberal and Tory. They were not faithful leaders. They did not really represent the people and had no real conception of the people's needs. The one thing that amazed him was that after that long coarse of befoolment by both political parties the people could be induced at election times to give them an extension of their confidence. The reason why, after a life of unremitting labour, one million and a half of our people were practically destitute and we were faced with the recurring periods of unemployruent,was because we had allowed the land and the means of production to become monopolised by a comparative few. With the exception of one or two specially favoured industries, we were told that to-day we were passing through a</p>
        <p>PERIOD OF DEPRESSION. Why was that? Not because there had been over- production in reality but because there had been uneer- consumption of the goods produced. No serious harm would overtake the nation if there were no further development of machinery for many years, So grea was the productive power Of labour that what we wanted was not so much an increase of that power as a more equitable distribution of the goods produced. They talked about the Labour party. being Socialists and engaging in plunder and pillage and overturning the pillars of Society. As a matte, of fact they were the true Conservatives. The right to work was upon the statute book. The law of 1601, which had never been repeated, laid an obligation on every municipality to provide the necessary materials whereby the unemployed might be set to labour. That law had never been set in serious operation because the gradual rise of capitalism and disappearance of home industry, made it to the intereat of the capitalist to set it aside. If we were a Christian nation, if our Christianity was anything more than mere lip service, if we really felt that the develop- ment of the nation rested upon Christian ethics and tenets we must say that each man and woman coming into the world had a God-given right to labour. (Applause.) If we recognised that no man liveth to himself, and that the highest happiness and develop- ment of the individual could only be promoted by the mutual interest of the whole community, we were bound to see that our poor dispossessed brother should not be treated as Ishmaelites any longer. (Applause.) Continu- ing, Mr. Walsh said they must first insist on the munici- palities providing dispossessed labourers with work sufficient to maintain them in a fair standard of decency and comfort. Mr. Walsh strongly denied that the Labour party preached confiscation or spoliation. They preached the doctrine of bringing the people back into their own by Constitutional stages, not by violence and rapine, or by setting class against class. They preached no revolutionary theory. They said that by the orderly process of evolution and the ccnstitu- tional developments which seemed to belong to the particular genius of the British people, they hoped to bring about the changes that would set the people once again in the enjoyment of their own. By the gradual freeing of the land and the breaking down of monopolies which sat so tightly on the shoulders of the labourer, they were hoping to gain greater freedom and bright- ness and to hasten the day when old age should not crouch at the gate a suppliant for the means whereby to fan a flickering life, but should sit in the highest happiness surrounded by honour, love, and troops of friends, at the hearthstone which they loved so well. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. S. WRIGHT disagreed with the opinion that drink was the primary cause of all evil. He believed the first cause was slumdoni, which drove people into the public- house—force of circumstances over which they had no control. Improve the dwellings and surroundings, and reform the man, then they would have no difficulty in reforming the drink traffic. The reason why temperance had not made greater progress was that men preached but did not act. He was against pressing Local Option upon the people until they were ready for it. He believed that if local option were enforced now the result would be that in Warrington there would be more public houses. He further contended that the nation had a right to the returns from the drink traffic because it was a monopoly. Mr. J. H. MORTON said his knowledge of Warrington had convinced him that local option would be a failure. He believed that drink was one of the chief causes of slumdum. What was needed in Warrington was a strong temperance party to educate public opinion. He did not know a town which was so much in the hands of drink as Warrington. Temperance sentiment ought to be so strongly roused that it would be impossible for a clergyman to append his signature to a brewer's nomina- tion paper. Mr. J. MORTON said there was a lack of temperance enthusiasm in the town. He had been looking out for temperance organizations ever since he came to Warrington, but they were always eon cat when they were wanted. The suggestion advanced by the " Examiner" would, if carried out, do a great deal to help forward the temperance movement. It would create a temperance sentiment not only amongst those of all shades ot politics, and would make it almost impossible for even the Tory party to have the impudence to put forward a candidate connected with " the trade." If such an organization were formed he was sure many members of that League would take up the work with energy and enthusiasm. He believed the true cause of poverty was not drink or slutudorn, but the competitive system of industry by which labour was ground down to bare sustenance, and drink came along as an aggravating influence. No solution of the slum question could be found until they brought about a true modification of our industrial organization. The CHAIRMAN said that if a man could keep his hope in temperance reform after having worked in Warrington for a few years he had a good share of hope. He (Mr. Sherburn) was not, however, without hope in the matter. He was glad to think that if an organiza- tion was formed, that League would do all in its power to help it forward. He had watched elections and had been grieved to find how many hundreds of votes in a ward could be influenced by a very little point, and that very often not a straightforward one. He had noticed it in the recent elections. If there was one thing more than another that temperance reform would do,it would clear the befogged intellects of men so that they would not be carried away by such claims as seeing the brewers and publicans as the champions of education and righteousness. He believed that industrial conditions were one of the primary causes of poverty, but it must be borne in mind that an average of 6s per week came out of the workingman's wages for drink. If men s intellects were cleared they would not be satis- fied any longer with the slums in which they were placed. He was glad there was a ntav movement in favour of a strong organization being formed In Warrington and he would do his best to help it forward. He would be thankful to the brewers and publicans who were successful in the recent election if the result of their victory had been to awaken temperance sentiment. He would not pin his faith so much on legislation, because he believed more in personal influence and in educating the people. Mr. GRIFFITHS in reply maintained that drink.was the primary cause of poverty. Local option might .be inoperative in Warrington but in other parts of the country it was being demanded. Nlnnicipalisation of the traffic had proved a failure abroad. He was glad to think that in drink-ridden Warrington public senti- ment had been roused since the November elections and be hoped that any organization that was formed would be a success. The resolution was carried with one dissentient.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks to Mr. Griffiths and the Chairman concluded the meeting. -</p>
        <p>It isn't the bird with gay plumage That sings the sweetest song. It isn't the loudest Councillor That gives us Right or Wrona. It isn't the colour of shining gold That makes the metal pure; But the finest thing fora cough or cold Is Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>QUESTIONS. PENSIONS FOR ALL.</head>
        <p>QUESTIONS. PENSIONS FOR ALL.</p>
        <p>Questions were then invited.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. MARSH asked what classification the advocates of old-age pensions, of which he was in favour, could devise to distinguish between the thrifty, the drunkard and the improvident. Mr. BARNES said they proposed absolutely no qualifi- cation at all. They believed none was desirable or possible. The thriftless and the drunken were those who were a charge upon the rates now. Poor Law relief was generally given to those brazen-faced people, the ne'er-do-wells who would tell any tale and suffer any indignity in order to get a share cf what was going. When they came to deal with old-age pensions on a universal plan, they were considering something not in the nature of a new expenditure so far as the drunken and dissolute were concerned, bat a new expenditure on the people who never bothered the Poor Law now. The universal plan the Liberal party believed to be the best, and they would give pensions to the thrifty and thriftless alike.</p>
        <p>Mrs. SIMPSON (Board of Guardians) said there were many married men in the town, fathers of eight or ten children, who were seeking daily employment and could not get it and the children were starving. What could be done now for the men and families who were in that condition?</p>
        <p>WATAH. said the Labour party in 1908 brought in and passed the Feeding of Necessitous School Chil- dren Act and if any children were going to school underfed now,the disgrace attached to the municipality. There was the law and it rested upon Warrington people to see that not one single child suffered physically and mentally for want of food. As to the unemployed, the leaders of the Liberal and Tory parties did not recog- nize the responsibility of the State or municipality to provide work for the dispossessed labourer. That the Labour party condemned as being utterly opposed to the highest functions of the State and they were bring- ing forward an Unemployed Bill and intended to press it.</p>
        <p>Questioned as to the course which the Labour party intended to take with regard to old-age pensionu, Mr. BARNES said be was inclined to think that Mr. Asquith, who with all his faults was not a man to make rash promises, would next year make a start in the way of old-age pensions. What the Liberal party had to do was to make it absolutely manifest to him that it was not a question of setting aside a paltry two millions, but that he must make provision in his Budget next year for pensions on an adequate scale. The resolution was put to the meeting ond enthusias tically carried.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A barber in Washington, so the New York " Evening Post" relates, recently had his eyes opened t,) one of the resul,s of the American tariff by an , experience on board ship when returning from a ' visit to Europe. One day he was having his hair cut, and noted with his professional eyes that the clippers used by the ship's barber were of American make. , "Bought them in New York, I suppose ?" he : remarked. "No, I didn't," was the reply; " I got them in Liverpool." It was then explained that their price in England was equivalent to a dollar and a quarter, whereas the lowest sum for which they could be obtained in New York was two dollars. The Washington practitioner has now become a Free Trade propagandist, and telling the story to all his customers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S WEDDING. DATE AND PLACE DEFINITELY FIXED.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S WEDDING. DATE AND PLACE DEFINITELY FIXED.</p>
        <p>We have much pleasure in announcing that the wedding of Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and Miss Elliadi will take place in Paris on Monday, December 2nd • Captain Crosfield will be the Members" best man" for the occasion and Miss. Truda Crosfield will also be present.</p>
        <p>The announcement last week by our neutral con- temporary that the wedffing would take place on November 28th was premature. We have it on Mr. Crosfield's own authority that December 2nd has been definitely decided upon.</p>
        <p>---</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>LATCHFORD ELECTION. Vigorous Party Fight. A most determined contest is taking place between. the Liberal and Conservative parties for the representation of Latchford Ward, and the whole town awaits with interest the result of the poll on M,nday next.</head>
        <p>THE LATCHFORD ELECTION. Vigorous Party Fight. A most determined contest is taking place between. the Liberal and Conservative parties for the repre- sentation of Latchford Ward, and the whole town awaits with interest the result of the poll on M,nday next.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Bibby, the Liberal nominee, is being strongly supported, and is at the same time sparing no effort to retain the Ward for Liberalism. He has this week issued some effective posters and circulars, one Of which draws attention to the fact that his Tory opponent, Mr. Henry Singletln was returned to the Widnes Council as a Liberal, opposed Alderman Forshaw ,C) in Warrington, and worked against the election kof Councillor,. Steel (C) in November 1906, and now stands as a Conservative staunch and true. Another publication gives 12 reasons why the electors should vote for Mr. Bibby, and details the public services he has rendered, particularly as a Friendly Society official.</p>
        <p>The addresses of both candidates will be. found among oar front page advertisements. The poll will open at 8 a.m. at the two booths, one in Wash-lane schools, and the other in St. James Schools.</p>
        <p>We hope the Liberals of Latchford will vote solidly for Mr. Bibby throughout Monday's eventful fight.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " A Royal Divorce." ThouGh it has been running for 16 years this famous play still continues to draw large audiences. There is something about the personality, of Napoleon which seems to possess as much fascination for the people of to-day as it inspired terror 100 years ago. "A. Royal Di vrce " describes in vivid fashion the closing scenes in the active history of the great conqueror and his divorced wife the Empress Josephine —divorced for affairs of State but still faithful to Napoleon and the only woman he ever loved. There is a strong company in the play at the Royal Conrt Theatre this week. Mr. Frederick Victor has made a careful study of the chief part, and the result is a most convincing Napoleon. The strong features, the heavy brows, the forelock, the qui&amp; step, staccato commands are all there "to the life." As the Empress Josephine, Miss Beth Elliott, a young actress, not only makes up well but-acts with, , power the sad and tragic role ,of the divorced wife of Napoleon. In her scene with Marie Louise, Napoleon's second wife, she is specially dramatic and earns a very hearty round of applause. Mr. Tom Robertson makes an excellent and suave Talleyrand, while Mr. W. T. Ellonby, as the bom- bastic General Ausereau could not be improved up in. The scene at the inn of Genappe is a famous one, but we thought that Mr. J. J. Jordan rather overdid the part of Grimand, the drunken innkeeper. He could have been much more articulate and still be far from sobriety. A. .word of praise is due to Miss Clara Earle, who makes a piquant and saucy, Stephanie de Beauharnais, cousin to the Empress Josephine. She took the honours as the soi disant Augereau in •the fourth act. There are five acts altogether and all are capitally staged. The tableaux of Napoleon at Waterloo are excellent, and a credit to the stage- management, while praise may very fairly be given to the scenery so charmingly , painted- by W .T. Hemsley. The whole production is one which d ies • justice to this famous historical play. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. I Next week Mr. Augustine Knight will present the ' modern romantic play "The Assassin," which should ' prove a strong attraction to those who like something ; sons ational in the histrionic line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>I THE CROSSLEY CONCERT.</head>
        <p>I THE CROSSLEY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The question whether the Warrington public is I being satiated with amusement pure and simple—but ; mainly simple —is unavoidable after one has noted the reception accorded to high-class concerts like those submitted by the Backhaus and Ada Crossley concert , parties. Neither was supported in a fashion likely to : gratify either the promoters or the artistes, indeed on Thursday evening there was a wilderness of space in the front part of the Hall where one would have ' expected a strong muster of musical enthusiasts. A contributory cause is doubtless to be found in the fact that the concert clashed with several social engage- ments of some note. Fortunately the cheaper seats , were well patronised, so that in numbers at any rate the audience which Madame Ada Crossley and her ; fellow-'artistes made their bow to was not a poor one. j And as far as appreciation and enthusiasm go there i was nothing lacking. The concert was one jof the most enjoyable of any given locally in j recent years, and the promoters of the tour are to be ; congratulated on having got together an exceptionally ! strong and attractive party of singers and instru- mentalists. With the exception of the two ladies, the artistes were new to Warrington people, which in itself was a feature of interest. Madame Ada Crossley is one of the leading contraltos of the period, and her welcome after so long an absence and the earning of so many additional laurels, was particu- larly cordial. She sang with delighful expression j and fulness of tone. " Caro raki, ben ' brought her her I first encore, to which she complied avith a perfect I rendering of " On the banks of Allan Water." Again with "Through Love to Light" (Cuthbert Wynne) she gave an impressive display of her vocal ; powers, which was -followed with an attractive encore piece "Christmas Bells," the produc- tion of Mr .S. Liddle (the famous composer and ace inapanist) in the success of which both. shared. The tuneful air, the ringing accompani- i merit, and the singer's tasteful treatment of this pretty ; song resulted in a series of recalls to which bath i bowed their acknowledgments. Miss Evangeline I Florence is quite an old favourite at Warrington, but I her finished vocalisation was never better appreciated i than in "Ah die assorta,' and the sweetly pretty I encore song about "the gap in the hedge at Kilmer°. " What does little Birdie Say" and" Cuckoo" both pleased with their simplicity and dainty rendering. I Mr. John Harrison has rapidly advanced to the front j rank of English tenors, and few vocalists are now so I popular. He had a hearty welcome to Warrington, I and in both the recit and aria" 0 Vision Entrancing,' ' and " 0 flower of all the world," he evidenced the possession ; of a powerful, yet clear and , melodious voice, while his technique as a 1 singer left nothing to be desired. Mr. Harrison twice respooded to encores with: "Two Bright Eyes" and "Mary of Argyll," the latter of which "brought down the hmise."Equally warm praise is due to Mr. Hamilton Earle who proved himself a baritone of unc.inamon ability. Mr. Earle has a voice of con- 1 siderable scope and very pleasing timbre, but it is in enunciation and expression that he , excels. We never heard any vocalist so articulate ani yet so musicianly. He was at his best in " When the Stars were young" (T?ubens), a charming song most expressively sung, though the j rollicking " loghterman Torn" and "Four Jolly I Sailormea " aroused the most enthusiasm among the I audience. Much interest was taken in the young I pianist, Mr. Percy Grainger, who has the distinction I amonc, his compeers of being English. Mr. Grainger I created quite a furore with his dazzling interpretation 'of the florid "Hungarian Rhapsody " (No. 12), (Liszt,) and was enthusiastically encored. His "Irish Dance" set every toe a-dancing, while in "In the Springtime " (Gricg) he gave an exhibition of subdued playing and light fingerwork wholly delightful. Mr. Grainger possessss inspiration, power and technical capacity I which should take him far along the path of fame. The violinist, Mr. Leon Sametini, created a good impression. There is no doubt of his ability, but the selections he gave and the tone of his instrument were alike disappointing in some degree to the critics. Mr. Sametini's easy style suggested that he was capable of greater performances. A word in conclusion is due to Mr. S. Liddle, who accompanied with the distinctive skill and taste of the true musician.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-23_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WAR OR PEACE.</head>
        <p>WAR OR PEACE.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL SEAVIGE LEAGUE DEBATE.</p>
        <p>"Does universal training as proposed by the National Service League tend towards the promotion of peace ?" was the subject of a debate under the auspices of the National Service League which took place in the ; Co-operative Hall on Monday. There was an exceed- ! ingly large attendance and the platform was occupied by Mr. H.Roberts (who presided), the Rev. W.G. Edwards Rees (vicar of Pendleton), Mr. Charles Stevenson, F.C.I.S. (Secretary of the Manchester District Peace and Arbitration League), Captain Crosfield, and Alder- man Snape, Liverpool. Mr. H. ROBERTS said that for the first time in his life , he found himself forced to remain neutral, although he should have liked to express an opinion. The order of the meeting was that the two debaters (the Rev. Mr. Rees and Mr. Stevenson) would be allowed to speak for 'twenty minutes, and after Alderman Snape and Captain Crosfield had spoken the debate would be thrown open uto the public. • The Rev. W. G. EDWARDS REEs,who was in favour of the question, said that the objects of the National Service League were to secure the peace and safety of •, the Empire, the improvement of the moral and physical condition of the nation, to bring about the adoption of Ia universal military training in order to be in a posi- tion to defend the country in time of invasion. To meet these requirements they must be in a sound • physical condition and the best way of attaining that , was by a system of three or four months training under ' . canvas. He was in favour of the proposals of the National Service League because the League was an I advocate of peace. He hated war and he hated it more , than anything else in the world. He hoped the years of peace would be long ones and that nations ai well as individual men would encourage that system of ' military training as proposed by the National Service League and thus by degrees become advocates of peace. Men in England were thirsting for war whereas in [:other countries war was never spoken about. He was I not there to defend France, Germany or any other 1 country, or their system of military training, but botti I in Germany and in France the system was on a sounder [ basis. He was there to defend the proposal of the National Service League and that he meant to do. (Applause.) England's present system of military training was not in a better condition than it was a ; good number of years ago. It was the men who had experienced the horrors of war that hated it. If a r war broke out there would be a rush to kill the enemy by *the men who were thirsting for blood. He was pleased to say that a great deal of interest was being taken in the advancement of our universal system of military training. If this interest continued we would. be able to defend our country and defeat any enemy that dared to come against us. He hoped war would be discouraged, and that England would remain a peaceful , country. He had great pleasure in moving the resolu- tion. (Applause.) Mr. CHARLES STEVENSON. who was against the resolu- tion, congratulated Mr. Rees on his able address, but he did not agree with him in all his points. One of the points he did not agree with was that Mr. Rees said the objects of the National Service League were to secure , the peace and safety of the Empire, and the improve- ment of the moral and physical condition of the nation, etc. He hoped that they had those objects at heart. The greatest man that ever trod this earth showed love for his enemies, and he believed that all men should love their enemies. Mr. Rees hated war and so did he. He hated war as much as anyone else hated it, if not more. Some of the English soldiers Were men who had been driven or who had ran away from home, and men who bore bad characters. He did not see the reason why children should be made to learn how to hold a rifle and how to shoot straight. That would be the thing that would make them thirst for war above everything else. If a man wanted to join a barracks he would join and make a good soldier, whereas if a man was made to join a barracks he would not make a good soldier at all because he had been forced. Although England had a good army and a strong one they never could beat the Scots. He was a Scotsman and he was proud of it. (Laughter.) We had enough men in the standing Army to-day, and the National Servicse League would provide the nation with more than it. was pre- pared to keep. He strongly opposed the resolution. (Applause.) Captain CROSFIELD said that the question under dis- cussion was of very great interest and he hoped the proposal of the National Service League would be carried with unanimity. England and not only England should be prepared for war. Because a child learned how to shoot he did not see why he should shoot Ater children for practice. A fireman who has been practising squirting on a big building, would not go to a big warehouse and put a light to it. Neither would a child become a murderer if he learned to use a rifle. All children of a scund.phygical condition ought to learn to use a rifle and then in time of need they would be able to defend their country. Alderman SNAPE said he did not think it was neces- sary to have such a very large reserve force in hand as, was the proposal of the National Service League. He did not like to see so many armed men about. One night a burglar entered his house and the neighbour sent for the policeman who came and took ()barge of the burglar. He did not like to see a man get into I,t-outa.. IP ...,uvay wort the things -sae priaeu above all other things in the world he would not bother about them if he thought trouble would ensue. ' He urged them "to do unto others as you wished them to do unto you." (Applause.) Mr. J. ASHWORTH said that the question under debate was more than an important one. He hated war , and he did not like to see war being carried\on. England was a peaceful nation and he hoped it would continue to be so. He had great pleasure in supporting the resolution. (Applause.) Mr. BRERETON FAIRCLOLTOH said they had heard a good deal that evening about the last war and various other subjects. He did not consider the National Service League would inake us thoroughly prepared for invasion. He was a man of peace. He believed in Alderman Snape's motto "Do unto others, &amp;c." He did not think that the National Service League would carry out its proposals even if they got the wish and support of the public, Mr. W. MASON, in supporting tha resolution, said he hated war as much as anybody else. If they wanted to have peace they must be prepared for war. Bays should be taught to use the rifle whilst at school and when they became eighteen they should join some corps of some description and when emergency arose they would be ready to fight and defend their homes and their country. (Applause.) Mr. LEVI was the next persou who handed his name to the Chairman and said he opposed the resolution. He was very pleased to see such a lot of ladies present. He would like to know whether the ladies would like to see their sons taken away" from them and made to fight. That's what the National Service League- intended to do. (Rats!) He strongly opposed the resolution. •</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. G. EDWARDS REES in replying said that the National Service League did not intend to take the - sons away from their mothers and make them fight. What the National Service League wanted to do was to be prepared for war and then they would have no fear of invasion. The Boers attacked our fortresses because we were not prepared for war, and we would be again attacked by other nations if we were not prepared for an attack. He said that a lot of people were emigrating and gave figures to show what an enormous 'number of people emigrated from England because there were so many bills about the place stating what a nice place such and such a place was and when they got there they found it was nothing of the sort. Ile asked them all to support the resolution.</p>
        <p>The CfrAIRMAN rose to speak, and began to give an opinion on the question at issue, but he was informed that he was there to govern the meeting, and not to give an opinion, The CHAIRM kN thereupon put the resolution, which was carried by a good majority. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
