        <p>CONGO REFORM tk,•OUIATION. A PUBLIC MEETING To PROTEST AGAINST THE COR GO ATROCITIES WILL BE HELD /N PARR HALL ON WEDNESDAY EVRNING, NOVRMI3EIt 2775, AT 745 PROMPT. CHAIRMAN C. E. PARKER, Efq. SPEAKERS: THE EIGHT HON. LORD MONESWELL, THE REV. J H. HARRIS (late Congo 801010 Missionary). Supported by his Worship the Mayor, Rev. Canon Willis, Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, Rev. W. Braceeamp, Nev. G. C. Briggs, Rev. H. P. Firth, Rev. J. H. G, Hollingworth, Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Rev. B. Percival, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Rev. John Yonge, Rev. Sidney Pitt, Rev. J. T. Milne, Rev. J. S. Hughes, Nev. T. Miller, Rev. W. Mellor, Rev. 3- Bugloss, Rev. F. Vaughan, Rev. E. J Gawne, Rev. H. Well, Rev. J F. Matthews, Henry Robert., Esq., J.P., J. Longiand, Req.. R. J. Guest. Req. J.P., John Ransome, Esq., Charles Jackson Holmes, EN, Req., Alderman A. Bennett, Councillor James Ball, Councillor Fred Stott. At 7.16 an exhibition of LANTERN BLIDES of scenes In the atrocity-ridden districts will be givsn by the Rev. Harry Wall. Collection to defray expenses.</p>
        <p>COUNTY BORuUGH OF WARRINGTOY. MUNICIPAL BYE-ELECTION, 1907. TO THE ELECTORS OF THE LATCHFORD WARD. LADIES AND lIENTLEMEN, I have been requested by the Conservative Ward Committee, and by others, to become a Candidate to fill the vacancy which has been created in the represent ttiou of the Latchford Ward on the Town Council by the eleva- tion of Mr. Ellison to the Aidermanic Bench, and I have agreed to do so. If honoured by your confidence I will do my best to serve you faithfully and diligently, and will endeavour to merit your continuei support. I am well acquainted with every part of the Ward and its needs and reqeire- meas. I would support most vigorously a well-considered and complete scheme for dealing adequately and in a final manner with Warriegton Bridge, as this, together with the question of the hallway ()roaming, is vital to the Latchford Ward. Warrington is now a large and important town, and ought to deal with thete pressing matters in no cheese-paring spirit. I should like to see Victoria Park more fully developed. and shall do what I can to bring this about as soon as possible, consistently with due regard to a proper economy in the town's finances, and to no undue pressure upon the rates. In Educational Matters I am most firmly in favour of the right of every parent to decide as to his own children that they shall be taught in his own faith, believing that this right ought not to be taken away and given to any public teuly of men whatever. I hold very strong views on this question. In adaition to the special interests of the Ward, I will do all I can for the interests of the town generally. I ant in savour of a reduction in the price of Gas to the consumer,and of any movoment which oan be devised for tho hitter la,using of the working classes and the Improvement of the conditions under which so many have unfortunately to live. It is doubtless well known to Alderman Bllison's many friends in the Ward that it was due to the dis- inttreeted action of the Conservative Patty that he obtained the distinction which has been conferred upon him, and I hope that this will be aocepted as evidence that the party to which I belong may be trusted to act firmly and impartially in public a/aire. Soliciting the favour of your votes and Support, I remain, Ladies and Gentien.en, Your obedient Servant HENP.Y SINGLETON. Bridge-street, Warrington. November 14th. 1907.</p>
