        <p>GARNETT'S LOCAL NEWS. NEW DEPARTMENT" to suit all Coming Events i The Stockton Heath Tree Planting Committee have classes. Call and look through the announced that a concert in aid of the funds of the new premises adjoining the old show- Society will be held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on Thursday next. There is a grand array of artistes and we are sure the concert will be a success. There will not be a meeting of the Junior Liberal Association on Monday evening owing to the election in Latchford Ward. I The nineteenth annual dinner in connection with the Warrington Caledonian Association will take Iplace at the Lion Hotel next Friday. A. sale of work under the auspices of the Spiritualist Society will take place on Wednesday and Thursday, December 4th and sth. Tee sale will be opened on Wednesday by Mrs. C. E. Parker, whilst on Thursday the ceremony will be performed by Couucillor Fulton. Smith. I The annual meeting of the Warrington and District i Nursing Association will take place n the Co-opera- tive Hall, Sankey-street, on Friday evening. The ' chair will be taken at half-past seven. A recital will be given in Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Monday by the Rev. J. Holden, 11. A. of Bowdon Downs Congregational Church. He will recite "The Sky Pilot" (Ralph Connor). Mr. Holden is creating the greatest interest by his " Recitals " and every- body is delighted. From all we hear, this will be a literary and elocutionary treat which should not be missed. " The Dream of Gerontius." We wouid remind our readers that the Musical Society will present Sir Ed ward Elgar's " Dream of Gerontius " at the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening next. The greatest interest is being taken in the enterprise and everything augurs for a creditable and successful performance. Golborne-street Church Pastorate. We understand that the pastorate of Golborne-street Baptist Church has been offered to the Rev. R. E. Letheren, of Northampton, whose decision will be made known on Sunday. There is reason to believe that the reply will be favourable. Parish Church Parochial Tea. As is announced in our advertising columns the annual Parochial Tea in connection with the Parish Church will take place on Thursday next. Tea will be partaken of at 6 p.m.' and after tea dancing will be indulged in from Bto 12 There will be no tickets sold after Wednesday. Congo Atrocities. A public meeting to protest against the Congo atrocities will be held in the Parr 'fall on Wednesday next. The chair will be taken at 7.43 by Mr. C. E. Parker and amongst the speakers are the Right Hon. Lord Areal-swell and the Rev. J. 11. Harris (late Congo Missionary.) Wycliffe Young People's Society. At the weekly meeting held on Monday evening in the Memorial Hall under the presidency of Mr. Wylie Mr. James Gould gave a paper entitled "The Housing Problem of our great cities. An interesting discus- sion followed, in which Messrs. Miller, W. Brown, W. J. Barnes, R. Wylie, T. F. Wallington, and Eccles took part. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Child Overlain. An inquest was held at the Police Station on Monday on the body of Annie Miller, aged 15 months. Catherine Miller, mother, said she put the deceased to bed at about 10.30 p.m. on Saturday. Witness retired to rest later, and when she awoke at four o'clock next morning she found that the child was . dead. Dr. Smith, police surgeon, said he had made a post-mortem examination of the body, and he thought death was due to suffocation from overlaying.—The I jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death.' PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. ' Gallant Rescue Rewarded. There was another pleasing incident at the Borough 1 Police Court on Monday, when the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) made a presentation of a certificate on vellum to Mr. William Griffiths, 51, Fairclough's- L avenue, for saving life. On Saturday, July 20th, a boy of Ii years' Harold Harsley of Clifton-street, Latchford, would have been drowned while bathing in the clay pits in Latchford, but for the conduct of Griffiths who jumped into the pit and got the drowning boy out. Agent for HARRIS'S BACON, ASHLEY. Sankey-st.—Advt. Fatal Flannelette. A child's life was lost through flannelette and want of forethought on Saturday evening when Joseph Webster Wilkinson, aged two years and nine months, who resided with his parents at 15, Taylor-street, Lower Walton, was burned to death. —At the inquest on Monday, the mother stated that she left the deceased and another child at 8.30 on Saturday night to make a purchase at a neighbouring shop. They had been pre- pared for bed, and were sitting in front of the fire in flannelette nightgowns. The fender and fireguard had been temporarily put on one side. Whilst she was making the purchase she heard screams, and on run- ning back found the clothes of the deceased in flames. Thejury returned a verdict of " Accidental death.' LINSEED COMPOUND 'of 40 years' proven efficacy for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. Kept Her Husband. At the Borough Police Court on Monday John Little, of Frederick-street, was summoned by his wife, Emma Little, for assault. Mr. Browne, who prose- cuted, stated that on Saturday, November 9, defendant came home, and only gave his wife 4s. 51. towards the house-keeping expenses. An altercation arose, which culminated in the defendant striking his wife several times in the face, breaking her false teeth. Defendant was in receipt of a week, as a result of an injury to his leg, and this encouraged him not to work. In order to maintain herself and the children the complainant had to take in washing. During the nineteen years of her married life her struggle to eke out a living has been a painful one. The magistrates imposed a fine of 20s. and costs, or fourteen days for the assault. A separation order was then granted, with an allowance of 7s. 6d. weekly. Buy HA.HRTS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASIILEY'S.—Advt. Shorthand Writers' Association. Wednesday evening was the "Ladies' night" at the above Association, and it was a very pronounced success. The meeting was held as usual at the Winmarleigh Cafe, the proceedings co nmencing at 7.30, when Miss Keleher was elected to the chair. Speed practice at various rates was read by several lady members, and some difficult outlines discussed. For the second half of the meeting Miss E. Houghton had prepared a paper on "Charlotte Bronte." The audience listened to the reading of this with great interest. The life of Charlotte Bronte was told in clear and convincing language, and the audience felt that they were listening to the career of a writer of genius.—A discussion ensued as to the desirability or otherwise of authors drawing characters from living personages, and after appreciations of the paper had been recorded, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer, which was given with applause.- el. vote of thanks was also accorded Miss Keleher for her , services in the chair which were carred out admirably. PETROLEUM OIL IN BARRELS and Drums delivered free. Ask prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Traction Engine Falls Through a Weighing Machine. A singular accident occurred in Manchester-road about twenty minutes to five on Tuesday afternoon. A new traction engine belonging to Mr. H. Massey, farmer and threshing machine owner, of Little Leigh, had discharged a load at Messrs. Rylands' new recreation ground in Gorsey-lane, it was proceeding home along the Manchester road when a tram car came up. behind. To allow the car to pass, the engine- driver, a man named Peter Beesley, of Elworth, Sand- bach, guided his engine on to the side of the road. Unfortunately .he failed to notice the street. weighing machine owned by Miss Hopkins, of the Post Office Opposite the end of School-brow—and in passing over it the weight of the engine —said to be between ten and twelve tons —proved too much. The back part of the engine crashed through and fell sideways, but the broken parts of the weighing machine luckily pre- vented it from toppling over. The driver, with presence of mind, quickly turned off the steam and lowered the fire. Police-Inspector Taylor was quickly on the spot, and two waggons were detached from the engine. The driver was fortunate to escape with only a shaking. During the night a number of men got to work, and extricated the engine, which resumed its journey at 5.30 on Wednesday. ' A Why do Johnston Watson's Eye-glasses and Spectacles produce such a delightful soothing sensation to tired and aching eyes? His clients say Because the correct lens is skilfully adapted to each eye."—Jo FINS CON WATSON, Eyesight and Spectacle Specialist, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. Low charges. Satisfaction guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
